#align(center)[OpenAI]
#align(center)[September 25, 2026]

== 1 Introduction <sec:introduction>

Pure infiniteness expresses the ability of positive elements of a $C^(*)$-algebra to contain several copies of
  themselves in Cuntz comparison. For a nonsimple algebra, an individual comparison and the simultaneous
  comparison of two elements are different demands: the latter must also control a mixed entry.
  We prove that proper infiniteness of every positive element already provides this compatibility.
  We then give two ways to remove a fixed finite amplification,
  one for exact algebras and an independent construction for nuclear algebras.
  In the separable nuclear case the result yields absorption of the Cuntz algebra $cal(O)_oo$.

=== 1.1 Infiniteness notions and main results <infiniteness-notions-and-main-results>

All $C^(*)$-algebras are complex and may be nonunital. Let $cal(K)$ denote the compact operators on an
  infinite-dimensional separable Hilbert space. For $u \, v in \( C ⊗ cal(K) \)_(+)$,
  write $u prec.tilde v$ if some sequence $x_j in C ⊗ cal(K)$ satisfies $x_j^(*) v x_j arrow.r u$ in norm,
  and write $u tilde.op_(upright("Cu")) v$ if comparison holds in both directions.
  This is stable Cuntz comparison #cite(label("Cuntz1978")).
  Finite matrices over $C$ occupy the standard corners of $C ⊗ cal(K)$;
  comparisons between different sizes use zero padding in the other coordinates.
  Write $h^(xor n)$ for the direct sum of $n$ copies of $h$.
  A positive element $h$ is #emph[properly infinite] if $h xor h prec.tilde h$.
  We include zero as properly infinite. For $h in C_(+)$, let

$ "Her" \( h \) = overline(h C h) \, wide "Id" \( h \) = upright("the closed two-sided ideal of ") C
  upright(" generated by ") h . $

We will use the following two comparison conditions:

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(P)")
 $ & h upright(" is properly infinite for every ") h in C_(+) \,\
 & h^(xor n) upright(" is properly infinite for every ") h in C_(+) \, quad
   upright("for one fixed integer ") n gt.eq 1 . $ <eq:property-P>]

In $upright("(F_n)")$, the same $n$ is chosen before all positive elements are tested.
  It therefore applies to every scalar spectral cutoff used later.
  The premise concerns scalar positives in $C$, with their indicated amplifications.

Condition $upright("(P)")$ is equivalent to pure infiniteness in the nonsimple sense of Kirchberg–Rørdam
  #cite(label("KR2000")) Theorem 4.16; the equivalence is also recalled in #cite(label("STW99problems"))
  Section 21. The existence of an $n$ satisfying $upright("(F_n)")$ is #emph[weak pure infiniteness] in the
  fixed-amplification sense. We use the following formulation of #emph[strong pure infiniteness],
  valid for arbitrary $C^(*)$-algebras #cite(label("KS")) Definition 3.1:
  for every $x \, y in C_(+)$ and $epsilon > 0$, there are $s \, t in C$ such that

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(1.1)")
 $ parallel s^(*) x^2 s - x^2 parallel < epsilon \, wide parallel t^(*) y^2 t - y^2 parallel < epsilon \, wide
   parallel s^(*) x y t parallel < epsilon . $ <eq:SPI-definition>]

The coefficients in this formulation have no prescribed norm bound.

Kirchberg–Rørdam introduced the weak and strong notions in #cite(label("KR2002")).
  Strong pure infiniteness implies ordinary pure infiniteness,
  and $upright("(P)")$ implies $upright("(F_n)")$ with $n = 1$.
  The reverse implications form the comparison question in Kirchberg–Rørdam’s Question 9.5,
  as recalled in #cite(label("STW99problems")) Section 21, Problem LXXII.
  In version 2 of that problem collection, the question asks whether the three notions agree in general and in the
  nuclear setting. Our first theorem proves the implication from ordinary to strong pure infiniteness for
  arbitrary complex $C^(*)$-algebras, in the following more flexible form.

#prodef([Theorem 1.1.])[
  #emph[Let $C$ be a complex $C^(*)$-algebra satisfying $upright("(P)")$.
    For all $a \, b in C_(+)$, $c in C$, and $epsilon > 0$, there are $s \, t in C$ such that
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(1.2)")
 $ parallel s^(*) a s - a parallel < epsilon \, wide parallel t^(*) b t - b parallel < epsilon \, wide
   parallel s^(*) c t parallel < epsilon . $ <eq:main-diagonalization>]

 Consequently $C$ is strongly purely infinite.]
] <thm:individual-to-strong>

The cross term $c$ is arbitrary and need not be a product of the diagonal inputs.
  Taking $\( a \, b \, c \) = \( x^2 \, y^2 \, x y \)$ gives the stated consequence.
  Arbitrary-entry diagonalization is a known equivalent form of strong pure infiniteness #cite(label("KS"))
  Remarks 3.2(i)–(ii); our proof obtains it directly from $upright("(P)")$.
  It also gives coefficient bounds for positive contraction inputs that are independent of the requested
  mixed-entry accuracy; see Proposition 16.

Recall that a $C^(*)$-algebra is exact if its minimal tensor product preserves short exact sequences.
  Exactness supplies the additional uniformity needed to remove the fixed amplification.

#prodef([Theorem 1.2.])[
  #emph[Let $C$ be an exact complex $C^(*)$-algebra. Suppose that there is one integer $n gt.eq
    1$ such that $h^(xor n)$ is properly infinite for every $h in C_(+)$.
    Then every positive element of $C$ is properly infinite. Consequently $C$ is strongly purely infinite.]
] <thm:exact-infiniteness>

These theorems give a positive answer to the ordinary-to-strong part of Problem LXXII in general and to the full
  comparison question for exact algebras, hence also for nuclear algebras.
  The weak-to-ordinary implication for arbitrary nonexact algebras remains outside these conclusions.
  The statements include nonseparable and nonunital algebras.
  Section 6 gives an independent proof of the first conclusion of Theorem 2 under nuclearity.
  Its statement is a restriction of that theorem, while its finite-tuple obstruction and merger of hereditary
  witnesses provide a different method. The same method extends to exact algebras by the deeper theorem that
  exactness passes to quotients; see Remark 23. The direct exact proof avoids that permanence theorem.

Let $cal(O)_oo$ be the Cuntz algebra generated by countably many isometries with mutually orthogonal ranges.
  Kirchberg–Rørdam proved the equivalence between strong pure infiniteness and $cal(O)_oo$-absorption for
  separable nuclear algebras that are stable or have an approximate unit of projections #cite(label("KR2002")).
  Kirchberg’s later central-sequence results cover general nonunital separable nuclear algebras
  #cite(label("Kirchberg2006")) Proposition 4.4 and Remark 4.6;
  see also the paragraph following #cite(label("KS")) Definition 3.1.
  At that scope they give the following tensorial consequence.

#prodef([Corollary 1.3.])[
  #emph[Let $A$ be a separable nuclear complex $C^(*)$-algebra.
    If there is one integer $n gt.eq 1$ such that $h^(xor n)$ is properly infinite for every $h in A_(+)$, then
$ A tilde.equiv A ⊗_min cal(O)_oo . $

]
] <cor:absorption>

For separable nuclear algebras, therefore, weak pure infiniteness, pure infiniteness, strong pure infiniteness,
  and $cal(O)_oo$-absorption are equivalent. The reverse implication from absorption to strong pure infiniteness
  is due to Kirchberg–Rørdam #cite(label("KR2002")); see also the discussion after #cite(label("KS"))
  Definition 3.1. Thus a single finite amplification test on positive elements characterizes this tensorial
  regularity even when the algebra is nonsimple or nonunital.

For example, the cone $C_0 \( \( 0 \, 1 \] \) ⊗ cal(O)_oo$ is separable nuclear and absorbs $cal(O)_oo$ by the
  self-absorption of $cal(O)_oo$. It is nonsimple, nonunital, and has no nonzero projections:
  the norm of a continuous projection-valued function takes only the values zero and one and must tend to zero at
  the missing endpoint.

=== 1.2 Earlier results and the role of ideal structure <earlier-results-and-the-role-of-ideal-structure>

The comparison question has been settled under several structural conditions.
  Kirchberg–Rørdam treat simple, real-rank-zero, and approximately divisible cases #cite(label("KR2002"));
  the real-rank-zero conclusion is also recalled in #cite(label("STW99problems")) Section 21.
  Blanchard–Kirchberg prove that ordinary pure infiniteness implies strong pure infiniteness when the primitive
  ideal space is Hausdorff #cite(label("BlanchardKirchberg2004")) Theorem 5.8.
  Rørdam’s proof of #cite(label("Rordam2004Z")) Theorem 5.2 gives the same implication for $cal(Z)$-absorbing
  purely infinite algebras, where $cal(Z)$ is the Jiang–Su algebra.
  These two implications do not require exactness or nuclearity.

Pasnicu–Rørdam establish equivalence of the three infiniteness notions for algebras with the ideal property,
  meaning that projections separate ideals #cite(label("PasnicuRordam2007")) Proposition 2.14.
  For algebras of topological dimension zero, Pasnicu–Rouzbehani prove the implication from ordinary to strong
  pure infiniteness #cite(label("PasnicuRouzbehani2025")) Theorems 3.10(i) and 3.13.
  Here topological dimension zero means that the primitive ideal space has a basis of compact open sets.
  Theorem 1 requires no restriction on this space or on the ideal structure.

The weak-to-ordinary implication is closely related to the problem of finding sufficiently many orthogonal
  equivalent elements in a hereditary subalgebra. Kirchberg–Rørdam showed that the Global Glimm Halving property
  permits this implication #cite(label("KR2002")) Proposition 4.15; see #cite(label("STW99problems")) Theorem 39.
  An equivalent formulation of the Global Glimm Property is that, for every $a in C_(+)$ and $epsilon > 0$,
  there is $x in "Her" \( a \)$ with $x^2 = 0$ such that $\( a - epsilon \)_(+)$ belongs to the closed two-sided
  ideal generated by $x$ #cite(label("TV2023")) Remark 3.2 and Theorem 3.6(3).
  Elliott–Rouzbehani prove weak-to-ordinary pure infiniteness in topological dimension zero
  #cite(label("ER2023")). Ng–Thiel–Vilalta recover that implication through the Global Glimm Property in a proof
  that includes nonseparable algebras #cite(label("NTV2026")) Theorem 2.3 and Corollary 2.5.
  The initial and range squares of a square-zero element are orthogonal and Cuntz equivalent.
  Our exact argument constructs such elements while retaining a nonzero image throughout a prescribed set of
  primitive quotients. The relevant sets are the norm slices ${ P in "Prim" \( C \) : parallel h +
  P parallel gt.eq r }$, where $h in C_(+)$ and $r > 0$; they are quasi-compact even when $"Prim" \( C \)$ is not
  Hausdorff. Repeated halving on the same slice then supplies enough copies to remove the fixed amplification.
  The nuclear argument instead combines representation-dependent positive elements into one element that covers a
  prescribed cutoff.

=== 1.3 Proof strategy and the independent routes <proof-strategy-and-the-independent-routes>

Two tasks drive the argument. To pass from $upright("(P)")$ to strong pure infiniteness,
  one must choose two hereditary corners that retain the relevant parts of their inputs while making the mixed
  term small. To remove $n$ from $upright("(F_n)")$, one must first produce sufficiently many equivalent
  orthogonal pieces inside a scalar hereditary subalgebra. Figure 1 separates these tasks and the two methods for
  the second one.

