===== 1.3.1 Comparison transport. <comparison-transport.>

Section 2 supplies the common tool. A Cuntz comparison, after a spectral cutoff,
  can be implemented by a rectangular partial isometry in the bidual.
  Its products with the cutoff hereditary algebra lie in the original algebra,
  and conjugation preserves norms in every representation, also at matrix levels.
  Thus one can place several orthogonal copies in a hereditary subalgebra and transport an entire finite tuple
  through the same map. These are stronger implementation data than a comparison of classes alone.

===== 1.3.2 From individual to simultaneous comparison. <from-individual-to-simultaneous-comparison.>

For $h gt.eq 0$, let $e_h$ be its support projection in the universal bidual.
  Starting from positive contractions $a \, b$ and a contraction $c$,
  the first goal is to find positive contractions $p in "Her" \( a \)$, $q in "Her" \( b \)$ with

$ parallel e_p c e_q parallel < 1 \, $

while requiring represented norm one of $p$ or $q$ whenever the corresponding input has represented norm greater
  than $1 \/ 2$. This retention of represented norms is what later gives ideal comparison.

Section 3 builds one corner as a graph inside several orthogonal copies of a cutoff of $a$.
  The graph has finitely many freely chosen algebra entries.
  If the positive defect $b \( 1 - c^(*) p^2 c \) b$ vanishes in a representation that detects the relevant part
  of $b$, equality in contraction inequalities forces all of those entries to equal fixed measurements,
  computed before the entries were chosen. Under $upright("(P)")$,
  Section 4 excludes these equalities with four entries. The first pair forces an isometry with a nonzero defect,
  using the scaling-element construction of Blackadar–Cuntz #cite(label("BlackadarCuntz1982"))
  #cite(label("PasnicuRordam2007")); the second pair is forced both into and away from that defect.
  Spectral cutoffs place the relevant supports in actual operator ranges,
  and a directed family of finite tests makes the contradiction work without separability.
  Two ideal cutoffs then give the strict support estimate above.

Section 5 upgrades strict contraction to arbitrarily small compression.
  Uniformly bounded comparison rows show that $upright("(P)")$ passes to bounded products.
  A product contradiction therefore supplies one contraction factor below one,
  and iteration preserves both represented norm signals while reducing the mixed compression.
  Finally, high spectral parts of $a \, b$ are compared into the separated corners.
  The coefficients have bounds determined by the diagonal cutoffs,
  independent of the requested mixed-entry accuracy. This order of choices completes Theorem 1.

===== 1.3.3 A separate nuclear proof of amplification removal. <a-separate-nuclear-proof-of-amplification-removal.>

Section 6 reuses the graph construction with a different obstruction.
  A finite tuple witnessing nonexactness has linear matrix norms that no tuple in an exact algebra can realize at
  every matrix level. Finite-dimensional compressions nevertheless approximate each fixed norm.
  Under $upright("(F_n)")$, one rectangular carrier transports each entire compressed tuple into $M_n \( C \)$.
  If every choice failed to exclude the graph relations, their limiting norms would occur in a quotient of
  $M_n \( C \)$. Nuclearity makes this quotient nuclear, hence exact, giving a contradiction.
  The matrix norm and fixed-element quotient arguments are related to Pisier’s operator-space localization methods
  #cite(label("Pisier1995")) pp. 162–165.

It remains to obtain the positive inputs that the graph needs and combine them.
  The fixed-amplification hypothesis and Kadison transitivity #cite(label("Kadison1957")) give,
  at each irreducible representation detecting a positive $u$,
  a positive contraction in $"Her" \( u \)$ whose every spectral cutoff is properly infinite and whose norm in
  that representation is one. This element may depend on the representation.
  Repeated graph shrinking and a polar deformation merge finitely many such elements while retaining all their
  norm signals. A finite ideal argument covers each cutoff of $u$ by one merged element and then compares two
  copies of that cutoff back to $u$. This proves $upright("(P)")$ under nuclearity,
  independently of the exact argument. The use of hereditary elements to cover ideals is related to the
  filling-family criteria of #cite(label("KS")) Theorem 1.1 and Definitions 4.2, 4.7;
  here the merger supplies the simultaneous norm control.

===== 1.3.4 The exact proof and its quantitative completion. <the-exact-proof-and-its-quantitative-completion.>

Section 7 starts from the nonexact universal unital $C^(*)$-algebra of one contraction.
  Its finite matrix tests and its embedded copy of $C^(*) \( bb(F)_2 \)$ were studied by Courtney–Sherman
  #cite(label("CS")), with Wassermann’s nonexact free-group example as a predecessor
  #cite(label("Wassermann1976")). Finite-word compression at all matrix levels turns nonexactness into one
  positive error threshold: no contraction in a unital exact algebra can contain approximately reducing copies of
  every finite matrix contraction. The same copy must approximately intertwine both the contraction and its adjoint.

Combining this obstruction with $upright("(F_n)")$ gives a perturbation that prevents cancellation simultaneously
  in every primitive quotient of a prescribed norm slice. Its lower norm bound is proportional to the perturbation
  size, with a constant independent of the slice and of $n$.
  Section 8 applies this estimate through three finite coupling steps.
  For a hereditary subalgebra nonzero on a prescribed quasi-compact subset of $"Prim" \( C \)$,
  it constructs a square-zero element nonzero at every point of that subset.
  Repeated halving on one fixed norm slice puts $n$ copies of a positive element below the input in Cuntz
  comparison; ideal comparison and a cutoff limit prove $upright("(P)")$. The general theorem then completes Theorem 2.

Section 9 gives an alternative final step under exactness.
  It constructs positives in two prescribed hereditary subalgebras with arbitrarily small product,
  while retaining a quotient norm floor fixed before the product error is chosen.
  Two compatible inverse perturbations preserve the two floors.
  Clipping the later comparison coefficients bounds their norms,
  so the small product survives implementation and gives strong pure infiniteness directly.
  Finally, for a separable nuclear algebra, the central-sequence absorption criterion applied in Section 6 gives
  Corollary 3.

== 2 Comparison and transport <sec:foundations>

The diagonalization argument repeatedly places several copies of a positive element inside one hereditary
  subalgebra. Comparison of Cuntz classes alone does not record enough information for this use:
  the copies must have a common implementation, their products must remain in the algebra,
  and their norms must agree in every representation. We establish these facts here,
  together with the ideal and cutoff rules used to retain norm signals.

Cuntz introduced the comparison relation in #cite(label("Cuntz1978")).
  The proper-target ideal comparison in Lemma 4(iii) is the standard consequence recorded in
  #cite(label("KR2000")) Proposition 3.5(ii); it is also used with that reference in
  #cite(label("KwasniewskiMeyer2023")) proof of Proposition 2.38.
  The cutoff implementation below is related to Rørdam’s comparison lemma #cite(label("Rordam1992"))
  Proposition 2.4, in the form recalled in #cite(label("AntoinePereraThiel2020")) Section 2.1.
  We give the rectangular and representation statements needed here with their proofs.

All comparisons below are stable: finite positive matrices are placed in a common stabilization by padding with
  zeros. For a $C^(*)$-algebra $D$ and $h in D_(+)$, let $"Id"_D \( h \)$ denote the closed two-sided ideal
  generated by $h$, and put

$ "Her"_D \( h \) = overline(h D h) \, wide e_h = upright(bold(1))_(\( 0 \, oo \)) \( h \) in D^(* *) . $

We omit the subscript when the ambient algebra is clear. Every support projection is taken in the universal
  bidual. Rectangular matrices over $C^(* *)$ are viewed in the corresponding corners of a matrix algebra over
  $C^(* *)$. The normal extension of a representation sends $e_h$ to $e_(pi \( h \))$:
  apply normality to $h \( h + 1 \/ j \)^(- 1) arrow.t e_h$.
  When using a unit or a support projection in a degenerate representation, we restrict to its essential subspace.
  This leaves the norm of every represented algebra element unchanged.

#prodef([Lemma 2.1.])[
  (Elementary comparison rules).  #emph[Let $D$ be a $C^(*)$-algebra.]
  + #emph[For $h in D_(+)$,
$ "Her"_D \( h \) = D ∩ e_h D^(* *) e_h . $

 If $0 lt.eq u lt.eq K v$ for $u \, v in D_(+)$ and $K gt.eq 0$, then $u in "Her" \( v \)$ and $u prec.tilde v$.
   More generally, every positive element of $"Her" \( v \)$ is Cuntz subequivalent to $v$.]
  + #emph[For a rectangular algebra matrix $z$, one has $z^(*) z tilde.op_(upright("Cu")) z z^(*)$.
    Cuntz subequivalence is transitive, is preserved by block sums,
    and is closed under norm limits in its first variable when the second variable is fixed.
    Every $*$-homomorphism preserves Cuntz subequivalence and equivalence, after amplification.
    For integers $m \, n gt.eq 1$, $d in M_n \( D \)_(+)$, and $v in M_m \( D \)_(+)$,
    stable comparison $d prec.tilde v$ is equivalent to the existence of $r_j in M_(m \, n) \( D \)$ with $r_j^(*)
    v r_j arrow.r d$ in norm.]
  + #emph[If $d \, v$ are positive elements in a common stabilization, $d in "Id" \( v \)$,
    and $v$ is properly infinite, then $d prec.tilde v$.]
] <lem:comparison-rules>

#proof[
  We first prove the bidual description in (i), including its norm closure assertion.
    One inclusion follows on $h D h$ and then by closure. Conversely, suppose $u in D_(+)$ and $e_h u = u$.
    Take increasing continuous positive contractions $f_j$ with $f_j \( 0 \) =
    0$ and $f_j \( t \) arrow.t 1$ for $t > 0$. On the compact state space of the unitization, the continuous functions
  $ phi mapsto phi #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] u \( 1 - f_j \( h \) \) u #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] $
  decrease pointwise to zero. Indeed, the normal extension of each state evaluates the strong limit $f_j \( h \)
    arrow.t e_h$. Dini’s theorem makes this convergence uniform.
    The positive norm formula and $\( 1 - f_j \( h \) \)^2 lt.eq 1 -
    f_j \( h \)$ then give $parallel \( 1 - f_j \( h \) \) u parallel arrow.r 0$.
    Thus $u = lim_j f_j \( h \) u f_j \( h \) in "Her" \( h \)$.
    Here $f_j \( h \) in "Her" \( h \)$ follows from continuous functional calculus,
    by first cutting $f_j$ off near zero. For a general element supported on both sides by $e_h$,
    apply the positive case to the positive and negative parts of its real and imaginary parts.
  If $0 lt.eq u lt.eq K v$, compression by $1 - e_v$ gives $u^(1 \/ 2) \( 1 - e_v \) = 0$.
    Hence $u$ is supported by $e_v$ and belongs to $"Her" \( v \)$.
    If $u in "Her" \( v \)_(+)$, take a cutoff approximate identity $g_j \( v \)$ for $"Her" \( v \)$,
    with every $g_j$ zero near zero. The quotient function below is continuous when extended by zero at zero,
    and gives elements
  $ x_j = frac(g_j \( v \), sqrt(v)) thin u^(1 \/ 2) in D \, wide x_j^(*) v x_j =
    u^(1 \/ 2) g_j \( v \)^2 u^(1 \/ 2) arrow.r u . $
  This proves the comparison assertion.
  For (ii), the rectangular coefficient $z \( z^(*) z + 1 \/ j \)^(- 1 \/ 2)$ compares $z^(*) z$ to $z z^(*)$,
    since its conjugation of $z z^(*)$ equals
  $ \( z^(*) z \)^2 \( z^(*) z + 1 \/ j \)^(- 1) arrow.r z^(*) z . $
  Apply the same calculation to $z^(*)$ for the reverse comparison.
    The inverses lie in unitizations and are multiplied by algebra matrices.
    Block-diagonal coefficients prove preservation under block sums.
    For transitivity, first approximate $u$ by $x^(*) v x$, then approximate $v$ by $y^(*) w
    y$ within the requested error divided by $max { 1 \, parallel x parallel^2 }$;
    the coefficient $y x$ compares $u$ to $w$. A diagonal choice of coefficients proves closure under norm limits
    in the first variable.
  If $phi.alt : D arrow.r E$ is a $*$-homomorphism, apply $phi.alt ⊗ "id"_(cal(K))$ to a comparison sequence;
    continuity preserves its norm limit. Applying this in both directions preserves equivalence.
    For finite $d \, v$, let $P_n \, P_m$ be the scalar coordinate projections in the multiplier algebra of $D ⊗
    cal(K)$. If $x_j^(*) v x_j arrow.r d$ in the stabilization, then
  $ r_j = P_m x_j P_n in M_(m \, n) \( D \) \, wide r_j^(*) v r_j = P_n x_j^(*) v x_j P_n arrow.r d . $
  Conversely, rectangular coefficients embed in the stabilization and give the same convergence there.
    The multiplier compressions retain algebra membership without a unit, and the argument includes zero matrices.
  For (iii), work in the common stabilization $D$. The closed span of $D v^(1 \/ 2) D$ is $"Id"_D \( v \)$,
    so finite sums $sum_(i = 1)^r x_i v^(1 \/ 2) y_i$ approximate $d^(1 \/ 2)$. For such a sum, define
  $ X = \[ x_1 med dots.h.c med x_r \] in M_(1 \, r) \( D \) \, wide
    Y = \[ y_1 med dots.h.c med y_r \]^T in M_(r \, 1) \( D \) \, $
  and put $V = "diag" \( v \, dots.h \, v \) in M_r \( D \)$. Then
  $ z = sum_(i = 1)^r x_i v^(1 \/ 2) y_i = X V^(1 \/ 2) Y . $
  The order and rectangular rules already proved give
  $ z^(*) z lt.eq parallel X parallel^2 Y^(*) V Y \, wide Y^(*) V Y tilde.op_(upright("Cu")) V^(1 \/ 2) Y Y^(*)
    V^(1 \/ 2) lt.eq parallel Y parallel^2 V . $
  It follows that $z^(*) z prec.tilde V$. Proper infiniteness, block addition,
    and induction give $V prec.tilde v$ for every finite $r$.
    Now $z^(*) z arrow.r d$, and the norm-limit rule yields $d prec.tilde v$. This proof includes $v = 0$. ◻
]

In particular, Cuntz equivalence preserves proper infiniteness,
  and a block sum of properly infinite positives is properly infinite.
  These statements follow from block addition and transitivity.
  Also, $u prec.tilde v$ implies $"Id" \( u \) subset "Id" \( v \)$,
  since each comparison approximant belongs to $"Id" \( v \)$.

#prodef([Lemma 2.2.])[
  (Transport after a cutoff).  #emph[Let $C$ be a $C^(*)$-algebra, let $m \, n gt.eq 1$ be integers,
    and let $d in M_n \( C \)_(+)$ and $v in M_m \( C \)_(+)$. Suppose $d prec.tilde v$ in $C ⊗ cal(K)$.
    For $epsilon > 0$, put $h = \( d - epsilon \)_(+)$. There is $w in M_(m \, n) \( C^(* *) \)$ such that
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(2.1)")
 $ w^(*) w = e_h \, wide w w^(*) lt.eq e_v \, $ <eq:transport-supports>]

 and
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(2.2)")
 $ w "Her"_(M_n \( C \)) \( h \) subset M_(m \, n) \( C \) \, wide
   w "Her"_(M_n \( C \)) \( h \) w^(*) subset "Her"_(M_m \( C \)) \( v \) . $ <eq:transport-membership>]

 Conjugation by $w$ is an injective $*$-homomorphism on $"Her" \( h \)$. For $l in "Her" \( h \)_(+)$,
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(2.3)")
 $ \( w l w^(*) - tau \)_(+) = w \( l - tau \)_(+) w^(*) \, wide
   w \( l - tau \)_(+) w^(*) tilde.op_(upright("Cu")) \( l - tau \)_(+) quad \( tau gt.eq 0 \) \,
   $ <eq:transport-cutoffs>]

 and
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(2.4)")
 $ w "Her" \( l \) w^(*) = "Her" \( w l w^(*) \) . $ <eq:transport-hereditary-image>]

 Every representation, with matching matrix amplifications, satisfies
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(2.5)")
 $ parallel pi^(\( m \)) \( w k w^(*) \) parallel = parallel pi^(\( n \)) \( k \) parallel
   wide \( k in "Her" \( h \) \) . $ <eq:transport-represented-norm>]

 The products in these formulas are algebra elements; $w$ itself is only asserted to lie in the bidual.]
] <lem:transport>

#proof[
  Compress a stabilization comparison coefficient to the first $m$ target and first $n$ source coordinates.
    These scalar multiplier projections give $s in M_(m \, n) \( C \)$ with $parallel d -
    s^(*) v s parallel < epsilon \/ 2$. Put $z = v^(1 \/ 2) s h$.
    Order in the unitization and functional calculus for $d$ give
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(2.6)")
   $ epsilon / 2 h^2 lt.eq z^(*) z = h s^(*) v s h lt.eq \( parallel d parallel + epsilon \/ 2 \) h^2 .
     $ <eq:transport-order>]
  For the lower bound, use $h d h gt.eq epsilon h^2$. The two inequalities and Lemma 4(i) show
    $"Her" \( z^(*) z \) = "Her" \( h \)$. Consequently $"Her" \( \| z \| \) = "Her" \( h \)$ and $e_(z^(*) z) =
    e_h$. Let $z = w \| z \|$ be the rectangular polar decomposition in the bidual.
    Then $w^(*) w = e_h$; the factorization through $v^(1 \/ 2)$ gives $w w^(*) = e_(z z^(*)) lt.eq e_v$.
  For $k in "Her" \( h \) = "Her" \( \| z \| \)$, take cutoff functions $g_j$ for $\| z \|$, zero near zero,
    with $g_j \( \| z \| \) k arrow.r k$. Since
  $ w g_j \( \| z \| \) = z thin frac(g_j \( \| z \| \), \| z \|) in M_(m \, n) \( C \) \, $
  norm closure gives $w k in M_(m \, n) \( C \)$. If $k gt.eq 0$,
    then $w k w^(*) = \( w k^(1 \/ 2) \) \( w k^(1 \/ 2) \)^(*)$ belongs to $M_m \( C \)$.
    It is supported under $e_v$, hence belongs to $"Her" \( v \)$. Linear decomposition handles arbitrary $k$.
    The identity $w^(*) w = e_h$ proves multiplicativity and injectivity. It also proves (2.3):
    functional calculus vanishing at zero commutes with the homomorphism,
    and the two squares of $w \( l - tau \)_(+)^(1 \/ 2)$ are the asserted positives.
  For (2.4), normal functional calculus gives $e_(w l w^(*)) = w e_l w^(*)$.
    Thus the image of $"Her" \( l \)$ is supported under $e_(w l w^(*))$, which gives one inclusion by Lemma 4(i).
    A sandwich by the image of $l$ has the form
  $ \( w l w^(*) \) b \( w l w^(*) \) = w #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] \( w l \)^(*) b \( w l \)
    #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] w^(*) . $
  The inner algebra element is supported on both sides by $e_l$, so lies in $"Her" \( l \)$.
    Since conjugation is an injective $*$-homomorphism on $"Her" \( l \)$, its image is norm closed;
    taking closures gives the reverse inclusion. Finally, the normal extension of $pi$ carries (2.1) to the same
    identities with represented supports. Conjugating back by the represented $w^(*)$ recovers
    $pi^(\( n \)) \( k \)$. The two contractive conjugations give both inequalities in (2.5). ◻
]

If $d_0 in M_ell \( C \)_(+)$ and $d = d_0^(xor r) in M_(r ell) \( C \)_(+)$,
  split $w in M_(m \, r ell) \( C^(* *) \)$ into block columns $w_i in M_(m \, ell) \( C^(* *) \)$. Then

$ w_i^(*) w_j = delta_(i j) e_(\( d_0 - epsilon \)_(+)) . $

The $r$ copies have orthogonal ranges and the same initial support.
  The single rectangular map also transports every entry of a matrix over the common hereditary algebra.
  Returning to the notation of Lemma 5, for $j gt.eq 1$ the same support identities apply to $1_j ⊗ w in
  M_(j m \, j n) \( C^(* *) \)$. Thus, for every $K in M_j \( "Her"_(M_n \( C \)) \( h \) \)$,
  entrywise conjugation gives an algebra matrix and preserves its norm in every representation,
  with the matching $j m$ and $j n$ amplifications. This is the joint norm assertion needed when a finite tuple is
  transported.

For orthogonal $c_1 \, dots.h \, c_r in C_(+)$, the row $R = \[ c_1^(1 \/ 2) \, dots.h \, c_r^(1 \/ 2) \] in
  M_(1 \, r) \( C \)$ satisfies

$ R^(*) R = "diag" \( c_1 \, dots.h \, c_r \) \, wide R R^(*) = sum_i c_i . $

Thus their block sum and their sum in $C$ are Cuntz equivalent.
  Moreover, $f \( sum_i c_i \) = sum_i f \( c_i \)$ for every continuous $f$ with $f \( 0 \) = 0$,
  first for polynomials with zero constant term and then by continuous functional calculus.
  Apply this calculation in $M_m \( C \)$ to the orthogonal transported copies $theta_i \( y \) =
  w_i y w_i^(*)$ of $y in "Her"_(M_ell \( C \)) \( \( d_0 - epsilon \)_(+) \)_(+)$.
  The cutoff and comparison assertions in Lemma 5 give

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(2.7)")
 $ (sum_(i = 1)^r theta_i \( y \) - tau)_(+) tilde.op_(upright("Cu")) \( \( y - tau \)_(+) \)^(xor r)
   wide \( tau gt.eq 0 \) . $ <eq:orthogonal-copy-cutoffs>]

In particular this includes the zero cutoff.

#prodef([Lemma 2.3.])[
  (Ideals and spectral cutoffs).  #emph[Let $D$ be a $C^(*)$-algebra.]
  + #emph[If $J = overline(union.big_lambda J_lambda)$ for an increasing directed family of closed ideals and $z
    in J_(+)$, then for every $epsilon > 0$, $\( z - epsilon \)_(+)$ lies in some $J_lambda$.
    In particular, the ideals $"Id" \( \( z - delta \)_(+) \)$, directed as $delta arrow.b 0$,
    densely exhaust $"Id" \( z \)$.]
  + #emph[For $u \, v in D_(+)$, one has $u in "Id" \( v \)$ if and only if every irreducible representation $pi$
    with $pi \( v \) = 0$ also satisfies $pi \( u \) = 0$.]
  + #emph[If $f \, g$ are continuous nonnegative functions of a positive element $a$, vanish at zero,
    and have the same open positivity set in $"sp" \( a \)$, then $"Her" \( f \( a \) \) =
    "Her" \( g \( a \) \)$ and $f \( a \) tilde.op_(upright("Cu")) g \( a \)$.]
] <lem:ideals-cutoffs>

#proof[
  For (i), choose $b in J_lambda$ with $parallel z - b parallel < epsilon$.
    Then $parallel z + J_lambda parallel < epsilon$, and functional calculus in the quotient gives
    $\( z - epsilon \)_(+) in J_lambda$. Since $\( z - delta \)_(+) arrow.r z$, the particular exhaustion follows.
    For (ii), apply separation of points by irreducible representations to the quotient $D \/ "Id" \( v \)$.
    For (iii), the two support projections coincide. Lemma 4(i) gives equality of the hereditary algebras and
    mutual comparison. ◻
]

#prodef([Definition 2.4.])[
  A positive contraction $a$ is #emph[rich] if $\( a - epsilon \)_(+)$ is properly infinite for every $epsilon gt.eq 0$.
] <def:rich>

Zero is rich, and under $upright("(P)")$ every positive contraction is rich.
  A #emph[ramp] is a continuous nondecreasing function with values in $\[ 0 \, 1 \]$,
  zero on a neighborhood of zero and constant at $1$ beyond a specified larger threshold.
  A ramp of a rich contraction is rich. Indeed, for $0 lt.eq tau < 1$,
  the open positivity set of $\( f \( a \) - tau \)_(+)$ is either empty or the positivity set of
  $\( a - rho \)_(+)$ for some $rho gt.eq 0$. Lemma 6(iii) and richness apply; for $tau gt.eq
  1$ the cutoff is zero. Lemma 5 gives the same conclusion for transported ramps.

== 3 Graph measurements and conditional shrinking <sec:graph>

For a $C^(*)$-algebra $C$, write $tilde(C)$ for its minimal unitization when $C$ is nonunital, and set $tilde(C) =
  C$ when $C$ is unital. We use its canonical action on $C^(* *)$ in products with support projections.
  Given positive contractions $a \, y in C$, with $a$ rich, and a contraction $c in tilde(C)$,
  we seek positive contractions $p_1 in "Her" \( a \)$ and $q_1 in "Her" \( y \)$ with two properties:
  each has represented norm one wherever its corresponding input has norm greater than $1 \/ 2$,
  and $parallel e_(p_1) c e_(q_1) parallel < 1$. The norm signals will later give ideal comparison.

The construction has two parts. A graph of orthogonal copies first turns possible failure into finitely many
  explicit equalities. Once those equalities have been excluded,
  ideal cutoffs convert the resulting nonvanishing into one global support estimate.
  We prove both parts here while leaving the finite exclusion problem to the later arguments.
  Throughout this section put

$ s = 1 / 4 \, wide alpha_0 = 1 / 32 . $

When the integer $k gt.eq 1$ has been chosen, fix $gamma_g \, sigma_g > 0$ with

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(3.1)")
 $ gamma_g^2 + k sigma_g^2 < 1 . $ <eq:graph-constants>]

For example, $gamma_g = 1 \/ 2$ and $sigma_g = 1 \/ \( 2 sqrt(k + 1) \)$ satisfy this inequality.
  Both the number of coordinates and these coefficients are fixed before the measurements below are computed.

#prodef([Proposition 3.1.])[
  (Fixed measurements of a graph).  #emph[Let $C$ be a $C^(*)$-algebra, let $a \,
    y in C_(+)$ be contractions with $a$ rich, and let $c in tilde(C)$ be a contraction.
    For an integer $k gt.eq 1$ and fixed positive $gamma_g \, sigma_g$ satisfying (3.1), there are
$ X in "Her" \( \( a - 1 \/ 8 \)_(+) \)_(+) \, wide B_1 \, dots.h \, B_k in "Her" \( \( a - 1 \/ 8 \)_(+) \) $

 with the following property. For any contractions
$ S_i in "Her" \( \( X - alpha_0 \)_(+) \) \, wide 1 lt.eq i lt.eq k \, $

 one can form a rich positive contraction $p in "Her" \( a \)$ such that, for every representation $pi$ of $C$,
   $parallel pi \( a \) parallel > 1 \/ 2$ implies $parallel pi \( p \) parallel = 1$.
   If a representation $pi$ of $C$ satisfies
$ pi #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] y \( 1 - c^(*) p^2 c \) y #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] = 0 \, wide
  parallel pi \( y \) parallel > s \, $

 then, in that same representation,
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(3.2)")
 $ parallel pi \( X \) parallel > s \, wide pi \( B_i \) =
   pi \( S_i \) quad \( 1 lt.eq i lt.eq k \) . $ <eq:graph-failure>]

 The elements $X \, B_1 \, dots.h \, B_k$ are fixed before the $S_i$ are chosen.]
] <prop:graph>

#proof[
  Let $e = e_(\( a - 1 \/ 8 \)_(+))$ and $H = "Her" \( \( a - 1 \/ 8 \)_(+) \)$.
    Since $a$ is properly infinite, $a^(xor \( k + 2 \)) prec.tilde a$.
    Apply Lemma 5 with cutoff $1 \/ 8$, and split the resulting row into slots labelled $0 \, 1 \, dots.h \,
    k \, *$. They satisfy
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(3.3)")
   $ v_i^(*) v_j = delta_(i j) e \, wide v_i v_i^(*) lt.eq e_a \, wide v_i H subset C . $ <eq:graph-slots>]
  Choose a ramp $t = t \( a \) in H$ that is zero on $\[ 0 \, 1 \/ 4 \]$ and one on $\[ 1 \/ 2 \, 1 \]$.
    It is rich, and its represented norm is one whenever the represented norm of $a$ exceeds $1 \/ 2$.
  We now compute the measurements, before making any choice of $S_i$. Put $F = c y c^(*) in C_(+)$.
    The block calculation behind their definition is as follows.
    Suppose $L in C^(* *)$ satisfies $L^(*) L = e$ and has slot coordinates $v_0^(*) L = gamma_g e$, $v_i^(*) L =
    sigma_g S_i$, and suppose $F = L H_0 L^(*)$ for a positive $H_0 in e C^(* *) e$. Then
  $ v_0^(*) F v_0 = gamma_g^2 H_0 \, wide v_i^(*) F v_0 = sigma_g gamma_g S_i H_0 . $
  Vanishing of the defect in a representation will force this form there, with $t H_0 =
    H_0$ and with $t$ acting identically on the prescribed supports of $S_i$.
    The first measurement therefore recovers $H_0$, and an inverse on its spectral cutoff recovers each $S_i$ from
    the second. These are conditional identities used to design the measurements;
    their verification follows after the graph is built.
  Choose a continuous nonnegative function $h_(upright("inv"))$ on $\[ 0 \, oo \)$, zero at zero,
    equal to $lambda^(- 1)$ for $lambda gt.eq alpha_0$, and bounded by $alpha_0^(- 1)$. Define
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(3.4)")
   $ X = gamma_g^(- 2) t v_0^(*) F v_0 t \, wide B_i = \( sigma_g gamma_g \)^(- 1) t v_i^(*) F v_0 t thin
     h_(upright("inv")) \( X \) . $ <eq:graph-measurements>]
  The sandwiches are $\( v_i t \)^(*) F \( v_0 t \)$, hence belong to $C$.
    They are supported on both sides by $e$, so they lie in $H$;
    the same is true after multiplication by $h_(upright("inv")) \( X \)$.
    Thus $X gt.eq 0$ and $X \, B_i$ have the asserted algebra membership.
    The slots, $t \, c \, y$ determine them without using any $S_i$.
  For the chosen $S_i$, define in the corner $e C^(* *) e$
  $ J = (\( 1 - gamma_g^2 \) e - sigma_g^2 sum_(i = 1)^k S_i^(*) S_i)^(1 \/ 2) . $
  Then define in $C^(* *)$
  $ L = gamma_g v_0 + sigma_g sum_(i = 1)^k v_i S_i + v_(*) J \, wide p = L t L^(*) . $
  Here $S_i in H$, since $X in H$. The radicand is at least $\( 1 - gamma_g^2 - k sigma_g^2 \) e$.
    Its square root belongs to $H + bb(C) e$ and multiplies $H$ into itself.
    Hence $L h in C$ for every $h in H$, term by term in (3.3). The slot identities also give
  $ L^(*) L = e \, wide e_a L = L . $
  It follows, by the membership and support argument of Lemma 5,
    that $h mapsto L h L^(*)$ is an injective $*$-homomorphism from $H$ into $"Her" \( a \)$.
    Indeed, for $h gt.eq 0$ its value is $\( L h^(1 \/ 2) \) \( L h^(1 \/ 2) \)^(*) in C$, supported under $e_a$;
    and $L^(*) L = e$ gives multiplicativity and recovers $h$ on conjugating back.
    The same identities give preservation of cutoffs, their Cuntz classes, and represented norms,
    exactly as in that lemma. Thus $p$ is a rich positive contraction with the stated representation signal.
    If $e = 0$, all the operators on this corner are zero and these assertions still hold.
  It remains to see what vanishing of the defect forces. Work on the essential subspace of a representation in which
  $ d = y \( 1 - c^(*) p^2 c \) y = 0 \, wide parallel y parallel > s \, $
  and omit the representation symbols. The normal extensions preserve the preceding support identities.
    For every vector $xi$,
  $ parallel y xi parallel = parallel p c thin y xi parallel lt.eq parallel c thin y xi parallel lt.eq
    parallel y xi parallel . $
  Equality first on $"ran" y$ and then on its closure shows that $c$ is isometric on $e_y cal(H)$.
    For a positive contraction $p$, equality $parallel p zeta parallel = parallel zeta parallel$ implies $p zeta =
    zeta$, by applying $1 - p^2$. Consequently $c e_y cal(H)$ lies in the $p = 1$ subspace,
    and the positive operator $F = c y c^(*)$ is supported on that subspace.
    Since $p = L t L^(*)$ and $L^(*) L = e$, the $p = 1$ subspace lies in the range of $L$,
    and its pullback lies in the $t = 1$ subspace. With $H_0 = L^(*) F L$, we obtain
  $ F = L H_0 L^(*) \, wide t H_0 = H_0 = H_0 t \, wide parallel H_0 parallel = parallel F parallel =
    parallel y parallel > s . $
  The last equality uses the isometric action of $c$ on $e_y cal(H)$.
  Now $v_0^(*) L = gamma_g e$, $v_i^(*) L = sigma_g S_i$, and $L^(*) v_0 = gamma_g e$.
    Substituting these identities into (3.4) yields
  $ X = t H_0 t = H_0 \, wide B_i = t S_i H_0 t thin h_(upright("inv")) \( X \) . $
  Since $t X = X$, $t$ is the identity on $overline("ran" X) = e_X cal(H)$.
    Each represented $S_i$ has both supports under $e_(\( X - alpha_0 \)_(+))$.
    Therefore $t S_i = S_i$ and $S_i X h_(upright("inv")) \( X \) = S_i$.
    The last display gives $B_i = S_i$, while $parallel X parallel = parallel H_0 parallel > s$. This proves (3.2). ◻
]

Thus it is enough to choose the $S_i$ in their prescribed spectral corner so that the simultaneous relations (3.2)
  cannot hold. The graph then supplies the nonvanishing implication assumed in the next lemma.
  The lemma uses the copied element only through its richness and its representation signal,
  so it is independent of how the finite equalities were excluded.

#prodef([Lemma 3.2.])[
  (From nonvanishing to shrinking).  #emph[Let $C$ be a $C^(*)$-algebra, let $a \,
    y in C_(+)$ be contractions with $a$ rich, and let $c in tilde(C)$ be a contraction.
    Suppose $p in "Her" \( a \)$ is a rich positive contraction satisfying
$ parallel pi \( a \) parallel > 1 \/ 2 quad arrow.r.double quad parallel pi \( p \) parallel = 1 wide
  upright("for every representation ") pi upright(" of ") C . $

 Put $d = y \( 1 - c^(*) p^2 c \) y in C_(+)$, and assume
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(3.5)")
 $ pi \( d \) = 0 quad arrow.r.double quad parallel pi \( y \) parallel lt.eq 1 / 4 wide
   upright("for every representation ") pi upright(" of ") C . $ <eq:common-nonvanishing>]

 Then there are positive contractions
$ p_1 in "Her" \( a \) \, wide q_1 in "Her" \( y \) $

 such that $p_1$ is rich,
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(3.6)")
 $ parallel pi \( a \) parallel > 1 \/ 2 & med arrow.r.double med parallel pi \( p_1 \) parallel = 1 \,\
parallel pi \( y \) parallel > 1 \/ 2 & med arrow.r.double med parallel pi \( q_1 \) parallel = 1 wide
  upright("for every representation ") pi upright(" of ") C \, $ <eq:shrinking-signals>]

 and
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(3.7)")
 $ parallel e_(p_1) c e_(q_1) parallel_(C^(* *)) < 1 . $ <eq:shrinking-support>]

 The last inequality is a single norm bound in the universal bidual.]
] <lem:conditional-shrinking>

#proof[
  The contraction assumptions give $0 lt.eq d lt.eq y^2 lt.eq 1$.
    By (3.5), every irreducible representation vanishing on $d$ vanishes on $\( y - s \)_(+)$.
    The primitive ideal test in Lemma 6(ii) therefore gives $\( y - s \)_(+) in "Id" \( d \)$. Set $s' = 3 \/ 8$.
    Apply cutoff exhaustion in Lemma 6(i) to $\( y - s \)_(+)$ with cutoff $s' - s$.
    After decreasing the resulting parameter if necessary, there is $0 < delta < 1$ with
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(3.8)")
   $ \( y - s' \)_(+) in "Id" \( h_d \) \, wide h_d = \( d - delta \)_(+) . $ <eq:shrinking-first-ideal>]
  Define
  $ z = y h_d^(1 \/ 2) in C \, wide q = z z^(*) = y h_d y in "Her" \( y \) . $
  The order $d lt.eq y^2 lt.eq 1$ gives
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(3.9)")
   $ delta h_d lt.eq h_d^(1 \/ 2) d h_d^(1 \/ 2) lt.eq z^(*) z = h_d^(1 \/ 2) y^2 h_d^(1 \/ 2) lt.eq
     h_d . $ <eq:shrinking-ideal-order>]
  Hence $z^(*) z$ and $h_d$ have the same hereditary algebra, and $z^(*) z tilde.op_(upright("Cu")) z z^(*) = q$.
    It follows that $"Id" \( q \) = "Id" \( h_d \)$. A second application of cutoff exhaustion,
    to $\( y - s' \)_(+)$ with cutoff $1 \/ 2 - s'$, gives $delta' > 0$ such that
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(3.10)")
   $ \( y - 1 \/ 2 \)_(+) in "Id" \( \( q - delta' \)_(+) \) . $ <eq:shrinking-second-ideal>]
  Let $q_1$ be a ramp of $q$, zero on $\[ 0 \, delta' \/ 2 \]$ and one on $\[ delta' \, oo \)$.
    It is a positive contraction in $"Her" \( q \) subset "Her" \( y \)$.
    If $parallel pi \( y \) parallel > 1 \/ 2$, then $pi \( \( y - 1 \/ 2 \)_(+) \) eq.not 0$.
    The ideal inclusion (3.10) forces $pi \( \( q - delta' \)_(+) \) eq.not 0$,
    so $parallel pi \( q \) parallel > delta'$ and $parallel pi \( q_1 \) parallel = 1$.
  To bound the universal-bidual norm, work in the universal nondegenerate representation.
    For $xi in e_(h_d) cal(H) = upright(bold(1))_(\( delta \, oo \)) \( d \) cal(H)$,
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(3.11)")
   $ parallel p c thin y xi parallel^2 & = parallel y xi parallel^2 - chevron.l d xi \, xi chevron.r\
 & lt.eq parallel y xi parallel^2 - delta parallel xi parallel^2 lt.eq \( 1 - delta \) parallel y xi parallel^2 .
   $ <eq:shrinking-norm-loss>]
  The last inequality uses that $y$ is a contraction. Apply this estimate to vectors in
    $"ran" \( h_d^(1 \/ 2) \)$. The closure of $"ran" \( y h_d^(1 \/ 2) \)$ is $e_q cal(H)$, so continuity gives
  $ parallel p c e_q parallel lt.eq sqrt(1 - delta) . $
  Choose $sqrt(1 - delta) < tau < 1$, and take $p_1$ to be a ramp of $p$, zero on $\[ 0 \, tau \]$ and one at $1$.
    Functional calculus gives $e_(p_1) lt.eq tau^(- 2) p^2$, while $e_(q_1) lt.eq e_q$. Consequently
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(3.12)")
   $ parallel e_(p_1) c e_(q_1) parallel lt.eq tau^(- 1) parallel p c e_(q_1) parallel lt.eq sqrt(1 - delta) /
     tau < 1 . $ <eq:shrinking-final-bound>]
  The ramp of the rich $p$ is rich and remains in $"Her" \( a \)$.
    Spectral mapping at its plateau shows that it has norm one in every representation where $p$ has norm one.
    This proves the first signal in (3.6); the second was proved above. The proof also covers zero inputs. ◻
]

== 4 Four entries under individual proper infiniteness <sec:four-variables>

Assume in this section that $C$ satisfies $upright("(P)")$.
  We choose four entries that rule out the fixed measurement relations in Proposition 8.
  This will supply the nonvanishing hypothesis of Lemma 9 and hence a strict contraction between hereditary
  supports. A spectral cutoff is essential: it places a support in an actual operator range,
  where the following positivity argument applies.

#prodef([Lemma 4.1.])[
  (A spectral cutoff in an actual range).  #emph[Let $x \, Q$ be bounded positive operators on a Hilbert space
    $cal(H)$, and put $U = x Q^2 x$. For every $eta > 0$,
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.1)")
 $ e_(\( U - eta \)_(+)) cal(H) subset "ran" \( x Q \) \, wide ker Q ∩ "ran" \( x Q \) = { 0 } . $ <eq:actual-range>]

]
] <lem:actual-range>

#proof[
  Let $f_eta$ be the bounded Borel function equal to $lambda^(- 1)$ for $lambda > eta$ and zero otherwise.
    Its norm on the spectrum is at most $eta^(- 1)$. If $zeta in e_(\( U - eta \)_(+)) cal(H)$, then
  $ zeta = U f_eta \( U \) zeta = x Q #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] Q x f_eta \( U \) zeta
    #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] \, $
  which proves the actual-range inclusion. If $zeta = x Q xi$ and $Q zeta = 0$, then
  $ 0 = chevron.l Q x Q xi \, xi chevron.r = parallel x^(1 \/ 2) Q xi parallel^2 \, $
  so $zeta = x Q xi = 0$. The assertion concerns the actual range; no closed-range conclusion is being used. ◻
]

#prodef([Proposition 4.2.])[
  (Four entries avoid the fixed measurements).  #emph[Suppose $C$ satisfies $upright("(P)")$.
    Let $X in C_(+)$ and $B_1 \, B_2 \, B_3 \, B_4 in C$. With
$ alpha_0 = 1 / 32 < alpha = 1 / 16 < beta = 1 / 8 < s = 1 / 4 \, wide h = 1 / 2 \, $

 there exist contractions $T_1 \, dots.h \, T_4$ in $"Her" \( \( X - alpha_0 \)_(+) \)$ for which no
   representation $pi$ of $C$ satisfies
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.2)")
 $ parallel pi \( X \) parallel > s \, wide pi \( B_i \) =
   pi \( T_i \) quad \( 1 lt.eq i lt.eq 4 \) . $ <eq:four-forbidden>]

]
] <prop:four-avoidance>

#proof[
  We argue by contradiction. If every four-entry choice could be matched in a representation,
    the fixed elements could be passed to a norm ultraproduct.
    In an irreducible representation of the resulting image of $C$,
    the first pair would give an isometry with nonzero defect, while the last pair would rule out that defect.
  All formulas described as fixed below depend only on $X \, B_i$ and the displayed constants. Define
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.3)")
   $ x = \( X - alpha \)_(+) \, wide V = \( X - beta \)_(+) \, wide Q = \( h 1 - B_1 B_1^(*) \)_(+) \, wide U =
     x Q^2 x . $ <eq:four-first-fixed>]
  Here $Q in tilde(C)_(+)$, whereas $x \, V \, U in C_(+)$.
    Let $"Fin" \( C \)$ denote the set of finite subsets of $C$, and order
  $ Lambda = bb(N)_(gt.eq 1) times "Fin" \( C \) \, $
  by the usual order in the first coordinate and inclusion in the second.
    At $lambda = \( m \, E \)$, put $eta = 1 \/ m$, so $eta arrow.r 0$ along this directed set,
    and every fixed $w in C$ eventually belongs to $E$.
  #emph[The first two entries.] The first pair will force $U w V =
    0$ for every fixed $w in C$ in a limiting image of $C$. At the index $\( m \, E \)$,
    we test only the finitely many $w in E$. A positive sum lets one norm bound control all of these tests: set
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.4)")
   $ A_eta = \( U - eta \)_(+) \, wide D_0 = A_eta (sum_(w in E) w V w^(*)) A_eta \, wide D = g_eta \( D_0 \) \,
     $ <eq:four-D>]
  where $g_eta$ is a ramp that is zero on $\[ 0 \, eta \]$ and one on $\[ 2 eta \, oo \)$. In fact,
  $ D_0 = sum_(w in E) \( A_eta w V^(1 \/ 2) \) \( A_eta w V^(1 \/ 2) \)^(*) . $
  Thus a small norm for $D_0$ makes each $A_eta w V^(1 \/ 2)$ small. We have
  $ D_0 in "Her" \( A_eta \) ∩ "Id" \( V \) \, wide e_D lt.eq e_(\( D_0 - eta \)_(+)) lt.eq e_(A_eta) lt.eq e_x . $
  Since $V$ is properly infinite, ideal comparison gives $D_0 xor D_0 prec.tilde V$.
    Apply Lemma 5 with cutoff $eta$, split the carrier into two columns,
    and multiply these columns by $D^(1 \/ 2)$. Taking adjoints of the resulting two elements gives contractions
    $T_1 \, T_2 in C$ with
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.5)")
   $ T_i T_j^(*) = delta_(i j) D \, wide e_V T_i^(*) = T_i^(*) quad \( i \, j = 1 \, 2 \) . $ <eq:four-first-entries>]
  Their left supports lie under $e_D$ and their right supports under $e_V$.
    Both are under $e_x lt.eq e_(\( X - alpha_0 \)_(+))$; they therefore belong to the required hereditary algebra.
  Consider a representation satisfying (4.2) for the entries at this index, and omit its symbols.
    Then $B_1 B_1^(*) = D$ and $Q = \( h 1 - D \)_(+)$. The spectral subspace of $D$ for $\( h \, oo \)$ is killed
    by $Q$ and lies in $e_D cal(H) subset e_(\( U - eta \)_(+)) cal(H)$. Lemma 10 shows that this subspace is zero. Thus
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.6)")
   $ B_1 B_1^(*) = D lt.eq h 1 \, wide parallel D_0 parallel lt.eq 2 eta quad
     upright("in this representation") . $ <eq:four-first-bounds>]
  For the second conclusion, if the norm of $D_0$ were at least $2 eta$,
    spectral mapping and the plateau of $g_eta$ would give $parallel D parallel = 1 > h$.
  #emph[A fixed prospective defect and the last two entries.] Choose ramps
  $ H = h_0 \( X \) \, wide K = k_0 \( X \) $
  so that $h_0$ vanishes on a neighborhood of $\[ 0 \, alpha \]$ and equals one on $\[ beta \, oo \)$,
    and $k_0$ vanishes on a neighborhood of $\[ 0 \, alpha_0 \]$ and equals one on $\[ alpha \, oo \)$.
    In particular $K H = H$. Set
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.7)")
   $ r_i = h^(- 1 \/ 2) B_i^(*) H quad \( i = 1 \, 2 \) \, wide b_0 = \( 1 - H^2 \)^(1 \/ 2) . $ <eq:four-r>]
  The reason for these definitions is the following conditional calculation.
    In the limiting irreducible representation below, the first pair will give
  $ r_i^(*) r_j = delta_(i j) H^2 \, wide H r_i = r_i quad \( i \, j = 1 \, 2 \) \, wide H eq.not 0 . $
  These relations are not being asserted in $C$ at this stage. They imply $b_0 r_i = 0$, and hence
  $ v = r_1 + b_0 \, wide v^(*) v = H^2 + b_0^2 = 1 \, wide v^(*) r_2 = 0 . $
  Since $r_2^(*) r_2 = H^2 eq.not 0$, the isometry $v$ has a nonzero defect projection $1 - v v^(*)$.
    This is the scaling-element construction of Blackadar–Cuntz #cite(label("BlackadarCuntz1982")),
    in the form recalled in #cite(label("PasnicuRordam2007")) Remark 2.4;
    the second entry supplies the additional nonzero orthogonal range.
  To choose the last two entries before taking any limit, we encode this prospective defect by one fixed element
    of $C$. Compression by $K$ puts the formula in the required hereditary algebra,
    and clipping makes it a positive contraction even before the preceding relations hold.
    Let $chi \( t \) = min { 1 \, max { 0 \, t } }$ on $bb(R)$, and define
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.8)")
   $ P_0 =
     chi #h(-0.16667em)
     (K #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\[] 1 - \( r_1 + b_0 \) \( r_1 + b_0 \)^(*) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\]] K) in
     C . $ <eq:four-P0>]
  The compressed selfadjoint element is in $C$ and supported under $e_K$; since $chi \( 0 \) = 0$,
    continuous functional calculus in $"Her" \( K \)$ gives
  $ e_(P_0) lt.eq e_K lt.eq e_(\( X - alpha_0 \)_(+)) . $
  This membership and support statement uses no relation from a representation.
  Define the following additional fixed elements:
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.9)")
   $ x' = \( P_0 - 1 \/ 2 \)_(+) \, wide Q' = \( h 1 - B_3 B_3^(*) \)_(+) \, wide R =
     x' \( Q' \)^2 x' . $ <eq:four-second-fixed>]
  At the same index $eta$, property $upright("(P)")$ gives
  $ \( \( R - eta \)_(+) \)^(xor 2) prec.tilde \( R - eta \)_(+) . $
  Apply Lemma 5 to this comparison with cutoff $eta$. If $t_R$ is a ramp of $R$,
    zero on $\[ 0 \, 2 eta \]$ and one on $\[ 4 eta \, oo \)$,
    the two carrier columns times $t_R$ give contractions $T_3 \, T_4 in C$ such that
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.10)")
   $ T_3^(*) T_4 = 0 \, wide T_3^(*) T_3 = T_4^(*) T_4 = t_R^2 . $ <eq:four-last-entries>]
  Their left supports lie under $e_(\( R - eta \)_(+))$ and their right supports under $e_(\( R - 2 eta \)_(+))$.
    Both are under $e_(x') lt.eq e_(\( X - alpha_0 \)_(+))$. Transport preserves the represented norms, so
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.11)")
   $ parallel pi \( R \) parallel > 4 eta quad arrow.r.double quad parallel pi \( T_3 \) parallel =
     1 . $ <eq:four-last-norm>]
  In a representation satisfying (4.2), $B_3 = T_3$. Apply Lemma 10 to $x' \, Q' \, R$.
    The spectral subspace of $B_3 B_3^(*)$ above $h$ is killed by $Q'$ and lies under $e_(\( R - eta \)_(+))$,
    so it is zero. Equation (4.11) then gives
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.12)")
   $ B_3 B_3^(*) lt.eq h 1 \, wide parallel R parallel lt.eq 4 eta quad
     upright("in this representation") . $ <eq:four-last-bounds>]
  #emph[Passing only fixed elements to an ultraproduct.] Suppose, for a contradiction,
    that every index in $Lambda$ admits a representation satisfying (4.2).
    Choose one, denoted $pi_lambda$, on its nondegenerate essential subspace.
    There is no size issue in this choice: from any such representation,
    choose a vector detecting $parallel pi_lambda \( X \) parallel >
    s$ and restrict to its reducing cyclic subrepresentation. A set of cyclic representations suffices.
    Choose an ultrafilter $omega$ containing all tails of $Lambda$, and form the norm ultraproduct
  $ cal(B) = product_omega B \( cal(H)_lambda \) =
    frac(product_lambda B \( cal(H)_lambda \), { \( z_lambda \) : lim_omega parallel z_lambda parallel = 0 }) . $
  The norm of a class is $lim_omega parallel z_lambda parallel$.
    Let $Phi : C arrow.r cal(B)$ be $Phi \( z \) = \[ \( pi_lambda \( z \) \) \]$, and write $B = Phi \( C \)$.
    The image is a closed $C^(*)$-subalgebra. The unital extensions carry the unitized formulas into
    $C^(*) \( B \, 1_(cal(B)) \)$. We suppress $Phi$ on fixed elements from now on.
  For any fixed $w in C$, eventually $w in E$. Positivity of the summands in (4.4) and (4.6) yield
  $ parallel pi_lambda \( \( U - eta \)_(+) w V^(1 \/ 2) \) parallel^2 lt.eq 2 eta $
  on that tail. Since $parallel U - \( U - eta \)_(+) parallel lt.eq eta$,
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.13)")
   $ parallel pi_lambda \( U w V \) parallel lt.eq sqrt(2 eta) thin parallel V parallel^(1 \/ 2) +
     eta parallel w parallel parallel V parallel arrow.r 0 . $ <eq:four-UCV-estimate>]
  It follows that $U B V = 0$ in the image algebra. The other exact relations and bounds at each index give
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.14)")
   $ parallel X parallel gt.eq s \, wide B_1 B_1^(*) lt.eq h 1 \, wide B_i B_j^(*) = delta_(i j) B_1 B_1^(*) \,
     quad H B_i^(*) = B_i^(*) quad \( i \, j = 1 \, 2 \) \,\
U B V = 0 \, wide R = 0 \, wide B_3 B_3^(*) lt.eq h 1 \, wide B_3^(*) B_4 = 0 \, quad B_3^(*) B_3 =
  B_4^(*) B_4 . $ <eq:four-ultraproduct>]
  For $H B_i^(*) = B_i^(*)$, use $e_V T_i^(*) = T_i^(*)$ and $H = 1$ on the support of $V$.
    Fix also a continuous $h_P : \[ 0 \, 1 \] arrow.r \[ 0 \, 1 \]$ with $h_P \( 0 \) = 0$ and $h_P =
    1$ on $\[ 1 \/ 2 \, 1 \]$. The left support of $T_4$ lies under $e_(x')$, so we obtain
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.15)")
   $ h_P \( P_0 \) B_4 = B_4 . $ <eq:four-B4-support>]
  Every element in (4.14) and (4.15) is the image of a fixed algebra element or of its fixed unitized functional
    calculus. We have not placed the varying $T_i \, D \, D_0 \, t_R$ in $B$,
    and we do not require proper infiniteness of $B$ or of $cal(B)$.
    The directed finite sets in (4.13) handle all $w in C$ without a separability assumption.
  #emph[A nonzero defect gives the contradiction.] Since $parallel X parallel gt.eq s > beta$,
    the image $V = \( X - beta \)_(+)$ is nonzero. Choose an irreducible representation of $B$ in which $V eq.not
    0$. Extend this nondegenerate representation to $C^(*) \( B \, 1_(cal(B)) \) = B +
    bb(C) 1_(cal(B))$ by sending $1_(cal(B))$ to the identity operator;
    this remains valid if $B$ has a unit different from $1_(cal(B))$.
    Thus the unitized expressions $Q \, b_0 \, Q'$ and the defect below have their stated represented meanings.
    Again suppress the representation symbols. The closed span of $B V cal(H)$ is a nonzero reducing subspace,
    and hence all of $cal(H)$. The relation $U B V = 0$ therefore gives $U = 0$. Put $D_1 = B_1 B_1^(*)$.
    Since $D_1 lt.eq h 1$, we have $Q = h 1 - D_1$, and
  $ 0 = U = \( Q x \)^(*) \( Q x \) quad arrow.r.double quad D_1 x = h x . $
  By closure $D_1 = h 1$ on $e_x cal(H)$. The support of $H$ lies there. Equations (4.7) and (4.14) give
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.16)")
   $ r_i^(*) r_j = delta_(i j) H^2 \, wide H r_i = r_i quad \( i \, j = 1 \, 2 \) . $ <eq:four-isometry-relations>]
  Also $H V = V eq.not 0$, so $H eq.not 0$ and $r_2 eq.not 0$.
  The conditional calculation preceding (4.8) now applies: $v = r_1 + b_0$ is an isometry with $v^(*) r_2 = 0$.
    Its defect projection
  $ P = 1 - v v^(*) $
  is nonzero, since $P r_2 = r_2 eq.not 0$.
  We check that this projection is the represented fixed element $P_0$,
    a point for which both sides of the cutoff matter. The scalar ramps give $K H = H$.
    From $H r_1 = r_1$ follows $K r_1 = r_1$, and the definition $r_1 = h^(- 1 \/ 2) B_1^(*) H$ gives $r_1 K =
    r_1$. Also $K$ commutes with $b_0$. Expanding the defect gives
  $ P = H^2 - r_1 r_1^(*) - r_1 b_0 - b_0 r_1^(*) . $
  Each term is fixed by multiplication by $K$ on both sides.
    Therefore $K P K = P$, and $chi \( P \) = P$ because $P$ is a projection. Formula (4.8) yields
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.17)")
   $ P_0 = P . $ <eq:four-P0-is-P>]
  Now $x' = \( P_0 - 1 \/ 2 \)_(+) = P \/ 2$. Put $D_3 = B_3 B_3^(*) lt.eq h 1$; then $Q' = h 1 - D_3$.
    The relation $R = 0$ gives $Q' x' = 0$, so
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.18)")
   $ D_3 P = h P . $ <eq:four-last-contradiction>]
  Equations (4.15) and (4.17) say $B_4 = P B_4$. But $B_3^(*) B_4 = 0$, so $D_3 B_4 = 0$,
    whereas (4.18) gives $D_3 B_4 = h B_4$. Thus $B_4 = 0$, and $B_3^(*) B_3 = B_4^(*) B_4$ gives $B_3 = 0$.
    This contradicts (4.18), since $h > 0$ and $P eq.not 0$.
  The contradiction shows that some index has no representation satisfying (4.2).
    The four entries chosen at that index prove the proposition. ◻
]

We now apply the avoidance proposition to the graph measurements.
  This gives the form of shrinking that will be used throughout the next section.

#prodef([Proposition 4.3.])[
  (Shrinking under individual proper infiniteness).  #emph[Suppose $C$ satisfies $upright("(P)")$.
    Let $a \, y in C$ be positive contractions and let $c in tilde(C)$ be a contraction.
    There are positive contractions $p_1 in "Her" \( a \)$ and $q_1 in "Her" \( y \)$, with $p_1$ rich,
    such that for every representation $pi$ of $C$,
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.19)")
 $ parallel pi \( a \) parallel > 1 \/ 2 med arrow.r.double med parallel pi \( p_1 \) parallel = 1 \, wide
   parallel pi \( y \) parallel > 1 \/ 2 med arrow.r.double med parallel pi \( q_1 \) parallel = 1 \,
   $ <eq:shrinking-P-signals>]

 and, in the universal bidual,
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.20)")
 $ parallel e_(p_1) c e_(q_1) parallel < 1 . $ <eq:shrinking-P-support>]

]
] <prop:shrinking-P>

#proof[
  Every positive contraction of $C$ is rich under $upright("(P)")$,
    since each of its scalar cutoffs is properly infinite. In Proposition 8, fix $k =
    4$ before the measurements are computed, and choose, for example, $gamma_g = 1 \/ 2$ and $sigma_g =
    1 \/ \( 2 sqrt(5) \)$. Then $gamma_g^2 + 4 sigma_g^2 < 1$.
    The graph proposition gives fixed $X$ and $B_1 \, dots.h \, B_4$ for the inputs $a \, y \, c$.
    Apply Proposition 11 to these elements and use its contractions $T_i$ as the graph choices $S_i$.
    The resulting rich positive contraction $p in "Her" \( a \)$ has the required norm-one signal for $a$.
  Put $d = y \( 1 - c^(*) p^2 c \) y$. If a representation $pi$ had $pi \( d \) =
    0$ and $parallel pi \( y \) parallel > 1 \/ 4$, the graph implication would give $parallel pi \( X \)
    parallel > 1 \/ 4$ and $pi \( B_i \) = pi \( T_i \)$ for every $i$.
    These are exactly the relations excluded by Proposition 11. Thus
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.21)")
   $ pi #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] y \( 1 - c^(*) p^2 c \) y #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] = 0 quad
     arrow.r.double quad parallel pi \( y \) parallel lt.eq 1 \/ 4 $ <eq:nonvanishing-P>]
  for every representation. The same $p$ is rich and carries the $a$-signal, so Lemma 9 applies to $a \, y \, c \,
    p$ and proves the stated conclusions. This argument also covers zero inputs. ◻
]

== 5 Uniform separation and general diagonalization <sec:separation>

We now prove Theorem 1. The strict support bound in Proposition 12 initially depends on the inputs.
  Products turn it into a universal constant below one; iteration then makes the support compression arbitrarily
  small. A final cutoff comparison gives coefficients whose norms are bounded independently of that requested smallness.

#prodef([Lemma 5.1.])[
  (Products preserve individual proper infiniteness).  #emph[Let $\( C_i \)_(i in I)$ be any family of
    $C^(*)$-algebras satisfying $upright("(P)")$. Their bounded product $D =
    product_(i in I) C_i$ satisfies $upright("(P)")$. More precisely,
    for a positive contraction $d = \( d_i \) in D$ and $0 < delta < 1 \/ 2$,
    there is a row $s = \( s_i \) in M_(1 \, 2) \( D \)$ such that
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(5.1)")
 $ s^(*) d s = \( d - 2 delta \)_(+) xor \( d - 2 delta \)_(+) \, wide parallel s parallel lt.eq
   delta^(- 1 \/ 2) . $ <eq:product-row>]

]
] <lem:product-P>

#proof[
  For an empty index set, $D = 0$ and the zero row has the asserted properties. Otherwise, for each $i$, put
  $ h_i = \( d_i - delta \)_(+) \, wide k_i = \( d_i - 2 delta \)_(+) xor \( d_i - 2 delta \)_(+) . $
  The positive $h_i$ is properly infinite. Apply Lemma 5 to $h_i xor h_i prec.tilde h_i$ with cutoff $delta$.
    If $v_i$ is the resulting rectangular partial isometry, then
  $ r_i = v_i k_i^(1 \/ 2) in M_(1 \, 2) \( C_i \) \, wide r_i^(*) r_i = k_i \, wide e_(h_i) r_i = r_i \, wide
    parallel r_i parallel lt.eq 1 . $
  The algebra membership follows from transport regularity; no membership of $v_i$ itself is needed. Define
  $ phi.alt_delta \( t \) =
    cases(delim: "{", t \/ delta^(3 \/ 2) \, & 0 lt.eq t lt.eq delta \,, t^(- 1 \/ 2) \, & t gt.eq delta .) $
  This continuous function vanishes at zero, is bounded by $delta^(- 1 \/ 2)$,
    and satisfies $t^(1 \/ 2) phi.alt_delta \( t \) = 1$ for $t > delta$. Consequently
  $ s_i = phi.alt_delta \( d_i \) r_i in M_(1 \, 2) \( C_i \) \, wide d_i^(1 \/ 2) s_i = r_i \, wide
    parallel s_i parallel lt.eq delta^(- 1 \/ 2) . $
  The uniform bound places $\( s_i \)$ in $M_(1 \, 2) \( D \)$. Coordinate functional calculus gives (5.1).
    Its right-hand side converges to $d xor d$ in norm as $delta arrow.b 0$, proving proper infiniteness of $d$.
    Scaling covers every positive element of $D$. ◻
]

The general product-comparison criterion of Antoine–Perera–Thiel #cite(label("AntoinePereraThiel2020")) Lemma 5.7
  also implies this permanence result: for each cutoff $epsilon$, one can take a target cutoff $delta =
  epsilon \/ 2$ and use proper infiniteness in every coordinate.
  The proof above records the particular bounded rows that make this inference possible.
  Coordinatewise comparison without a common cutoff bound would not suffice.

#prodef([Lemma 5.2.])[
  (A uniform support contraction).  #emph[There exists $theta
    in \( 0 \, 1 \)$ such that for every $C^(*)$-algebra $C$ satisfying $upright("(P)")$,
    every pair of positive contractions $a \, y in C$, and every contraction $c in C$,
    there are positive contractions $p in "Her" \( a \)$, $q in "Her" \( y \)$ such that,
    for every representation $pi$ of $C$,
$ parallel pi \( a \) parallel > 1 \/ 2 med arrow.r.double med parallel pi \( p \) parallel = 1 \, wide
  parallel pi \( y \) parallel > 1 \/ 2 med arrow.r.double med parallel pi \( q \) parallel = 1 \, $

 and
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(5.2)")
 $ parallel e_p c e_q parallel lt.eq theta . $ <eq:uniform-support>]

]
] <lem:uniform-shrinking>

#proof[
  If no such bound existed, for each $m gt.eq 2$ we could choose an algebra $C_m$ satisfying $upright("(P)")$ and
    contractions $a_m \, y_m \, c_m$, with the first two positive,
    such that every pair with the stated membership and simultaneous signals has support norm greater than $1 -
    1 \/ m$. Form their bounded product $D$ and the three elements $a = \( a_m \)$, $y = \( y_m \)$,
    $c = \( c_m \) in D$. Lemma 13 gives $upright("(P)")$ for $D$, so Proposition 12 produces $p \,
    q$ with the signals and $kappa = parallel e_p c e_q parallel < 1$.
  For each $m$, the coordinate homomorphism $D arrow.r C_m$ preserves hereditary membership,
    and its normal extension sends the two support projections to the coordinate supports.
    Thus the coordinate support norm is at most $kappa$. If $pi$ is any representation of $C_m$,
    its composition with the coordinate homomorphism is a representation of $D$,
    so both signals hold for the coordinate pair as well. Choosing $m$ with $1 -
    1 \/ m > kappa$ contradicts its selection. The resulting constant can be increased, if necessary,
    to lie in $\( 0 \, 1 \)$. ◻
]

This proof uses the normal coordinate maps, not an identification of $D^(* *)$ with a product of biduals.
  The input $c$ in Lemma 14 belongs to $C$. This ensures that the sequence $\( c_m \)$ belongs to $D$;
  a sequence of arbitrary individual unitizations need not belong to $D$ or its unitization.
  We need the uniform statement only in the stated form.

#prodef([Proposition 5.3.])[
  (Arbitrarily small support compression).  #emph[Suppose $C$ satisfies $upright("(P)")$.
    For positive contractions $a_(*) \, b_(*) in C$, a contraction $c_(*) in C$, and $zeta > 0$,
    there are positive contractions $p in "Her" \( a_(*) \)$, $q in "Her" \( b_(*) \)$ such that
$ parallel e_p c_(*) e_q parallel < zeta $

 and, for every representation $pi$,
$ parallel pi \( a_(*) \) parallel > 1 \/ 2 med arrow.r.double med parallel pi \( p \) parallel = 1 \, wide
  parallel pi \( b_(*) \) parallel > 1 \/ 2 med arrow.r.double med parallel pi \( q \) parallel = 1 . $

]
] <prop:iterated-separation>

#proof[
  Fix $theta$ from Lemma 14. On $\[ 0 \, 1 \]$ set
  $ f \( t \) = \( 2 t - 1 \)_(+) \, wide g \( t \) = min { 1 \, \( 4 t - 1 \)_(+) } . $
  Both vanish near zero, $f \( 1 \) = 1$, and $g = 1$ on $\[ 1 \/ 2 \, 1 \]$. For a positive contraction $h$,
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(5.3)")
   $ g \( h \) = e_h g \( h \) \, wide g \( h \) e_(f \( h \)) = e_(f \( h \)) . $ <eq:iteration-cutoffs>]
  Apply Lemma 14 once to $a_(*) \, b_(*) \, c_(*)$, obtaining $p_1 \, q_1$ with support norm at most $theta$.
    Suppose inductively that $parallel e_(p_k) c_(*) e_(q_k) parallel lt.eq theta^k$. The next normalized input is
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(5.4)")
   $ c^(\( k \)) = theta^(- k) g \( p_k \) c_(*) g \( q_k \) in C \, wide parallel c^(\( k \)) parallel lt.eq
     1 . $ <eq:iteration-normalization>]
  Apply the uniform lemma to $f \( p_k \) \, f \( q_k \) \, c^(\( k \))$.
    The new supports lie under those of $f \( p_k \)$ and $f \( q_k \)$; by (5.3),
  $ e_(p_(k + 1)) c_(*) e_(q_(k + 1)) = theta^k e_(p_(k + 1)) c^(\( k \)) e_(q_(k + 1)) \, wide
    parallel e_(p_(k + 1)) c_(*) e_(q_(k + 1)) parallel lt.eq theta^(k + 1) . $
  The hereditary algebras are nested. In every representation,
    norm one of $p_k$ implies norm one of $f \( p_k \)$, by spectral mapping and $f \( 1 \) = 1$,
    and similarly for $q_k$. Thus the two original signals persist at every step.
    All positive contractions needed as first inputs are rich under $upright("(P)")$.
    Choose $k$ with $theta^k < zeta$. ◻
]

#prodef([Proposition 5.4.])[
  (Comparison with bounded coefficients).  #emph[Suppose $C$ satisfies $upright("(P)")$.
    Let $a \, b in C_(+)$ be contractions and let $c in C$. Given
$ 0 < delta < 1 \/ 2 \, wide 0 < rho lt.eq 2 delta \, wide zeta > 0 \, $

 there exist $s \, t in C$ such that
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(5.5)")
 $ parallel s^(*) a s - a parallel \, med parallel t^(*) b t - b parallel & < 2 delta + rho \,\
parallel s^(*) c t parallel & < zeta \, wide parallel s parallel \, med parallel t parallel lt.eq
  delta^(- 1 \/ 2) . $ <eq:bounded-diagonals>]

]
] <prop:bounded-diagonalization>

#proof[
  Let $a_(*) \, b_(*)$ be ramps of $a \, b$, zero on $\[ 0 \, delta \]$ and one on $\[ 2 delta \, 1 \]$.
    Put $M_c = max { 1 \, parallel c parallel }$. Choose a finite iteration in Proposition 15 so that its outputs
    satisfy the signals and
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(5.6)")
   $ parallel e_p c e_q parallel < delta zeta . $ <eq:bounded-chosen-separation>]
  For example, apply the construction to $c \/ M_c$ and take $k$ with $M_c theta^k < delta zeta$.
    This choice precedes the comparison coefficients below.
  It remains to realize the diagonal cutoffs through $p$ and $q$ with a coefficient bound depending only on
    $delta$ and $rho$.
  Put $d_a = \( a - 2 delta \)_(+)$. If an irreducible representation detects $d_a$,
    its norm on $a$ is greater than $2 delta$. It then has norm one on $a_(*)$,
    and the signal gives norm one on $p$. Lemma 6(ii) yields $d_a in "Id" \( p \)$. Since
  $ e_p lt.eq e_(a_(*)) lt.eq upright(bold(1))_(\( delta \, oo \)) \( a \) \, wide p a p gt.eq delta p^2 \, $
  we have $"Id" \( p a p \) = "Id" \( p \)$: one inclusion is immediate, and the displayed order gives the other.
    The positive $p a p$ is properly infinite under $upright("(P)")$, so Lemma 4(iii) gives $d_a prec.tilde p a p$.
  Compressing a stabilization comparison coefficient to the scalar source and target corners supplies $w in C$ with
  $ parallel w^(*) p a p w - d_a parallel < rho . $
  Set $s = p w$. Then $e_p s = s$, $s^(*) a s = w^(*) p a p w$, and
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(5.7)")
   $ delta s^(*) s lt.eq s^(*) a s \, wide parallel s parallel^2 lt.eq
     frac(parallel d_a parallel + rho, delta) lt.eq frac(1 - 2 delta + rho, delta) lt.eq 1 /
     delta . $ <eq:final-coefficient-bound>]
  In particular the more general estimate $parallel p w parallel^2 lt.eq \( 1 + rho \) \/
    delta$ holds without the restriction $rho lt.eq 2 delta$.
    The same argument for $b \, q$ gives $t = q w'$, $e_q t = t$, and the identical bounds.
    Because $parallel a - d_a parallel lt.eq 2 delta$, these comparisons prove (5.5). Finally
  $ parallel s^(*) c t parallel lt.eq parallel s parallel parallel t parallel parallel e_p c e_q parallel lt.eq
    delta^(- 1) parallel e_p c e_q parallel < zeta . $
  The bound in (5.7) depends on $delta \, rho$, not on $p \, q$ or the separation accuracy.
    This is why the preceding choice (5.6) remains valid after the comparison coefficients are found. ◻
]

#proof[
  For arbitrary $a \, b in C_(+)$, $c in C$, and $epsilon > 0$, put $M = max { 1 \, parallel a parallel \,
    parallel b parallel }$. Choose $0 < delta < 1 \/ 2$ and $0 < rho lt.eq
    2 delta$ with $M \( 2 delta + rho \) < epsilon$. Apply Proposition 16 to $a \/ M \, b \/ M \, c$ with $zeta =
    epsilon$. Multiplying the two diagonal inequalities by $M$ gives all three inequalities in (1.2).
  For positive $x \, y in C$, specialize these inequalities to $\( a \, b \, c \) = \( x^2 \, y^2 \, x y \)$.
    They are precisely (1.1), so $C$ is strongly purely infinite. This uses the unrestricted definition directly.
    If one instead writes a block matrix $Z = mat(delim: "(", a, c; c^(*), b)$,
    the norm of $"diag" \( s \, t \)^(*) Z "diag" \( s \, t \) - \( a xor b \)$ is at most the maximum of the two
    diagonal errors plus $parallel s^(*) c t parallel$. Hence the same estimates also give diagonal approximation
    of every such block matrix, in particular of every positive one.
    The argument includes zero elements and the zero algebra. ◻
]

== 6 Removing amplification under nuclearity <sec:nuclear>

We now remove the fixed amplification under nuclearity. This proof uses the graph construction of Section 3:
  a finite tuple of matrix entries rules out the graph’s failure relations,
  and the resulting shrinking operation allows us to join rich positive elements detected by different
  representations. Its second output need not be rich; the merger retains $n$ orthogonal copies so that
  $upright("(F_n)")$ makes their sum rich. A finite ideal argument then reaches every positive element.
  The proof of the following theorem is independent of the exact localization argument in Sections 7–8.

#prodef([Theorem 6.1.])[
  (Removing amplification under nuclearity).  #emph[Let $C$ be a nuclear complex $C^(*)$-algebra.
    Suppose that one integer $n gt.eq 1$, fixed for the whole algebra,
    makes $h^(xor n)$ properly infinite for every $h in C_(+)$.
    Then every positive element of $C$ is properly infinite.]
] <thm:nuclear-to-individual>

Theorem 17 assumes neither separability nor a unit. We keep the fixed integer $n$ throughout this section.
  In the tuple construction it controls the number of graph entries,
  while the dimensions of the finite matrix approximations may vary.

=== 6.1 A finite tuple that exact algebras cannot realize <a-finite-tuple-that-exact-algebras-cannot-realize>

The graph measurements are fixed before their entries are chosen.
  To choose those entries under nuclearity, we use a finite tuple whose linear matrix norms cannot all occur in an
  exact algebra, even though each fixed such norm is a limit of norms of finite matrix tuples.
  Nonexact examples originate in work of Wassermann #cite(label("Wassermann1976")).
  Pisier develops the operator-space formulation and finite compression mechanism in #cite(label("Pisier1995"))
  Theorems 1 and 7, Lemma 2 and Equation (7). We use the existence of a nonexact algebra from this theory and give
  the tensor norm and compression arguments needed here. They retain the quantifiers across all matrix levels
  required for the graph entries.

Recall that a $C^(*)$-algebra $D$ is #emph[exact] if tensoring every short exact sequence with $D$ in the minimal
  tensor norm preserves exactness. Thus exactness identifies the quotient norm on
  $\( D ⊗_min J \) \/ \( D ⊗_min I \)$ with the spatial norm on $D ⊗_min \( J \/ I \)$,
  for every closed ideal $I lt.closed J$. The next proof starts from a strict inequality between these norms.

#prodef([Lemma 6.2.])[
  (A finite tuple and finite matrix approximations).  #emph[There are $ell gt.eq 1$, a $C^(*)$-algebra $W$,
    contractions $z_1 \, dots.h \, z_ell in W$, and contractions $z_j^(\( r \)) in M_(N_r) \( bb(C) \)$,
    where $N_r gt.eq 1$, $r gt.eq 1$, and $1 lt.eq j lt.eq ell$, with the following properties.]
  + #emph[No tuple $b_1 \, dots.h \, b_ell$ in any exact $C^(*)$-algebra satisfies
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(6.1)")
 $ ∥sum_(j = 1)^ell A_j ⊗ b_j∥ = ∥sum_(j = 1)^ell A_j ⊗ z_j∥ quad upright("for every ") m gt.eq
   1 upright(" and every ") A_j in M_m \( bb(C) \) . $ <eq:forbidden-tuple>]

]
  + #emph[For every fixed $m gt.eq 1$ and $A_j in M_m \( bb(C) \)$,
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(6.2)")
 $ lim_(r arrow.r oo) ∥sum_(j = 1)^ell A_j ⊗ z_j^(\( r \))∥ = ∥sum_(j = 1)^ell A_j ⊗ z_j∥ . $ <eq:tuple-approximations>]

]
] <lem:forbidden-tuple>

#proof[
  Take a nonexact $C^(*)$-algebra $W$, whose existence is recorded in #cite(label("Pisier1995")) Remark (i)
    following Theorem 1, pp. 163–164. There are $I lt.closed J$ such that the canonical surjection
  $ \( W ⊗_min J \) \/ \( W ⊗_min I \) arrow.r W ⊗_min \( J \/ I \) $
  is not injective. The map is surjective because its algebraic image is dense and the range of a
    $C^(*)$-homomorphism is closed. Choose a representative $T_0 in W ⊗_min J$ of a nonzero class in its kernel.
    Its norm modulo $W ⊗_min I$ is positive, whereas its image in $W ⊗_min \( J \/ I \)$ is zero.
    A sufficiently close finite algebraic approximation to $T_0$ retains a strict gap between these two norms.
    After normalizing its nonzero first factors and absorbing their norms into the second factors,
    we obtain contractions $z_j in W$ and elements $h_j in J$ for which
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(6.3)")
   $ ∥sum_j z_j ⊗ h_j + W ⊗_min I∥ > ∥sum_j z_j ⊗ \( h_j + I \)∥_min . $ <eq:tuple-gap>]
  For a tuple $upright(bold(v)) = \( v_j \)_(j = 1)^ell$ in a $C^(*)$-algebra $D$, write
  $ mu_(upright(bold(v)) \, m) \( A_1 \, dots.h \, A_ell \) = ∥sum_(j = 1)^ell A_j ⊗ v_j∥_(M_m \( D \)) . $
  We show that these functions determine both norms in (6.3), with the coefficient algebra and its ideal held fixed.
  For arbitrary coefficients $d_j in J$, represent the fixed nonzero algebra $J$ faithfully on a Hilbert space
    $cal(H)$. Then
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(6.4)")
   $ ∥sum_j v_j ⊗ d_j∥_min =
     sup_(m \, V) mu_(upright(bold(v)) \, m) \( V^(*) d_1 V \, dots.h \, V^(*) d_ell V \) \,
     $ <eq:tuple-compression-formula>]
  where $V : bb(C)^m arrow.r cal(H)$ ranges over finite-dimensional isometries.
    Indeed, also represent $D$ faithfully. The operators on the right are the two-sided compressions in the second
    Hilbert factor, so their norms are at most the left-hand norm.
    Finite-rank projections, directed by inclusion, converge strongly to the identity.
    Their two-sided compressions converge strongly to the tensor sum;
    evaluation at a fixed vector whose image nearly attains its norm gives the reverse inequality.
    This applies to arbitrary, including nonselfadjoint, tensor sums.
  For the quotient norm, choose a positive contractive approximate identity $\( f_lambda \)$ of the fixed ideal
    $I$. Put $J_D = D ⊗_min I$. For $T in D ⊗_min J$, define
  $ R_lambda \( T \) = \( 1 ⊗ \( 1 - f_lambda \) \) T \( 1 ⊗ \( 1 - f_lambda \) \) \, $
  using the unitized multiplier operations. Elementary tensors and density give
  $ T - R_lambda \( T \) in J_D \, wide parallel R_lambda \( K \) parallel arrow.r 0 quad
    upright("for each fixed ") K in J_D . $
  The cutdowns are contractions. Hence, for every fixed $K in J_D$,
  $ "dist" \( T \, J_D \) lt.eq parallel R_lambda \( T \) parallel lt.eq parallel T - K parallel +
    parallel R_lambda \( K \) parallel . $
  Taking lower and upper limits and then the infimum over $K$ yields the net limit
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(6.5)")
   $ ∥sum_j v_j ⊗ d_j + J_D∥ = lim_lambda ∥sum_j v_j ⊗ \( 1 - f_lambda \) d_j \( 1 - f_lambda \)∥_min .
     $ <eq:tuple-quotient-formula>]
  The cutdown coefficients lie in $J$, and no quasicentrality is needed.
    This argument uses convergence on each fixed element of $J_D$.
    A related fixed-element cutdown mechanism appears in #cite(label("Pisier1995")) proof of Lemma 3, p. 165;
    the calculation above establishes the stated formula for general $I lt.closed
    J$ and its approximate-identity net. Equations (6.4) and (6.5) show that the displayed quotient norm depends
    only on the functions $mu_(upright(bold(v)) \, m)$. Applying the same compression argument in $J \/
    I$ shows the same for $parallel sum_j v_j ⊗ \( d_j + I \) parallel_min$.
  If a tuple in an exact algebra had the same functions $mu_(upright(bold(z)) \, m)$ at every matrix level,
    the two formulas above would transfer this strict gap to that algebra.
    Exactness makes its canonical quotient map isometric, a contradiction. This proves (i).
    The conclusion excludes simultaneous equality for all finite matrix tests;
    it does not assert that a fixed finite list of tests suffices.
  For (ii), set $W_0 = C^(*) \( z_1 \, dots.h \, z_ell \)$.
    This algebra is separable, and its inclusion in $W$ preserves matrix norms.
    It has a faithful representation on a separable Hilbert space.
    Indeed, choose a dense sequence in the positive unit sphere and, for each member, a state attaining its norm.
    Their GNS representations have faithful direct sum: for every nonzero positive element,
    approximation by that sequence shows that some chosen state is positive on it.
    Choose increasing nonzero finite-rank projections $P_r arrow.r 1$ strongly in that representation, and set
  $ N_r = dim P_r cal(H) \, wide z_j^(\( r \)) = P_r z_j P_r \|_(P_r cal(H)) . $
  These are contractions. For each fixed $m$ and $\( A_j \)$,
    the corresponding two-sided compressions of $sum_j A_j ⊗ z_j$ converge strongly to that operator and have
    norms at most its norm. Evaluation at a fixed near-norming vector gives the matching lower limit,
    proving (6.2). The same sequence works for every fixed test.
    The assertion is pointwise in the test, and uses no multiplicativity of the compressions. ◻
]

=== 6.2 Choosing graph entries under nuclearity <choosing-graph-entries-under-nuclearity>

We use the finite tuples from Lemma 18 to choose entries in a spectral hereditary subalgebra.
  The central point is that the transport lemma provides one rectangular map for the whole tuple.
  Its conjugation preserves the norm of every joint linear matrix expression,
  not merely the norms of the individual entries.

#prodef([Proposition 6.3.])[
  (Nuclear avoidance of the fixed measurements).  #emph[Let $C$ be nuclear and satisfy $upright("(F_n)")$ for a
    fixed $n gt.eq 1$, and let $ell$ be as in Lemma 18. For $X in C_(+)$ and fixed elements $B_(j \, p q) in C$,
    where $1 lt.eq j lt.eq ell$ and $1 lt.eq p \, q lt.eq n$, there are contractions
$ S_(j \, p q) in "Her" \( \( X - alpha_0 \)_(+) \) $

 such that no representation $pi$ satisfies simultaneously
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(6.6)")
 $ parallel pi \( X \) parallel > s \, wide pi \( B_(j \, p q) \) =
   pi \( S_(j \, p q) \) quad \( 1 lt.eq j lt.eq ell \, med 1 lt.eq p \, q lt.eq n \) . $ <eq:nuclear-forbidden>]

 Here $alpha_0 = 1 \/ 32$ and $s = 1 \/ 4$, as in Proposition 8.]
] <prop:nuclear-avoidance>

#proof[
  Put $g = \( X - alpha_0 \)_(+)$, choose $0 < nu < s - alpha_0$, and put $h_g = \( g - nu \)_(+)$.
    Choose a ramp $t' = t' \( X \)$ that is zero on $\[ 0 \, alpha_0 + nu \]$ and one on $\[ s \, oo \)$.
    Then $t' in "Her" \( h_g \)$, and
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(6.7)")
   $ parallel pi \( X \) parallel > s quad arrow.r.double quad parallel pi \( t' \) parallel =
     1 . $ <eq:nuclear-tuple-signal>]
  The element $v = g^(xor n) in M_n \( C \)$ is properly infinite by $upright("(F_n)")$,
    applied to the scalar positive $g$. For the dimensions $N_r$ of Lemma 18,
  $ d_r = g^(xor N_r) prec.tilde v^(xor N_r) prec.tilde v . $
  The first comparison uses $g$ as a summand of $v$; the second uses proper infiniteness of $v$.
    Lemma 5, with cutoff $nu$, gives one rectangular carrier
  $ w_r in M_(n \, N_r) \( C^(* *) \) \, wide w_r^(*) w_r = 1_(N_r) ⊗ e_(h_g) \, wide w_r w_r^(*) lt.eq 1_n ⊗ e_g . $
  Its conjugation sends $M_(N_r) \( "Her" \( h_g \) \)$ into $M_n \( "Her" \( g \) \) =
    "Her"_(M_n \( C \)) \( v \)$. Use this same carrier for every $j$, defining
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(6.8)")
   $ S_j^(\( r \)) = w_r \( z_j^(\( r \)) ⊗ t' \) w_r^(*) in M_n \( "Her" \( g \) \) \, wide
     S_(j \, p q)^(\( r \)) = \( S_j^(\( r \)) \)_(p q) . $ <eq:nuclear-tuple-choices>]
  Each matrix has norm at most one, so its entries are contractions in the required hereditary subalgebra.
  Suppose that every $r$ admits a representation $pi_r$ satisfying (6.6) for these entries.
    Restricting to a reducing cyclic subrepresentation that still detects $parallel pi_r \( X \) parallel > s$,
    we may make these choices from a set of representations. Write $B_j = \[ B_(j \, p q) \] in M_n \( C \)$;
    these elements are fixed independently of $r$. At every matrix level $m$,
    the normal amplification of the single $w_r$ is isometric on the supported source algebra, by Lemma 5.
    The represented entry equalities and (6.7) therefore give, for every $A_j in M_m \( bb(C) \)$,
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(6.9)")
   $ ∥sum_j A_j ⊗ pi_r^(\( n \)) \( B_j \)∥ & = ∥(sum_j A_j ⊗ z_j^(\( r \))) ⊗ pi_r \( t' \)∥\
 & = ∥sum_j A_j ⊗ z_j^(\( r \))∥ . $ <eq:nuclear-exact-matrix-norms>]
  This equality is exact at each $r$. Using a different carrier for each $j$ would not give the joint equality.
  Define the $*$-homomorphism
  $ Psi : M_n \( C \) arrow.r cal(Q) =
    frac(product_r B \( cal(H)_r^(xor n) \), xor.big_r B \( cal(H)_r^(xor n) \)) \, wide
    Psi \( b \) = \[ \( pi_r^(\( n \)) \( b \) \)_r \] \, $
  where the numerator is the algebra of bounded sequences and the denominator is its ideal of norm-null sequences.
    Contractivity of the representations makes each displayed sequence bounded.
    The image $E = Psi \( M_n \( C \) \)$ is closed and isomorphic to $M_n \( C \) \/ ker Psi$.
    Since $M_n \( C \) tilde.equiv M_n ⊗ C$, nuclearity of matrix algebras and permanence under tensor products
    make $M_n \( C \)$ nuclear #cite(label("Blackadar")) II.9.4.2 and IV.3.1.1.
    Its quotient $E$ is nuclear by #cite(label("ChoiEffros1977")) Corollary 4,
    and hence exact by #cite(label("Blackadar")) II.9.6.6. These permanence facts require neither units nor
    separability. It is this quotient of a nuclear algebra that must be exact;
    the argument requires no exactness of the ambient $cal(Q)$.
  The norm of a class modulo norm-null sequences is the $limsup$ of its coordinate norms.
    Subtracting a norm-null sequence gives the lower bound, and subtracting finitely many initial entries gives
    the upper bound. The same formula holds at each fixed matrix level under the canonical identifications of
    finite matrices over a product and its norm-null ideal. Since $E subset cal(Q)$ is completely isometric,
    (6.9) and (6.2) give
  $ ∥sum_j A_j ⊗ Psi \( B_j \)∥_(M_m \( E \)) = limsup_r ∥sum_j A_j ⊗ pi_r^(\( n \)) \( B_j \)∥ = ∥sum_j A_j ⊗ z_j∥ $
  for every $m$ and every $\( A_j \)$. Only the fixed elements $B_j$ have been sent into $E$;
    the varying choices in (6.8) were used in the coordinatewise equalities.
    The displayed identity contradicts Lemma 18(i). Thus some $r$ admits no such representation,
    and its entries in (6.8) prove the proposition. ◻
]

#prodef([Proposition 6.4.])[
  (Shrinking two corners under nuclearity).  #emph[Let $C$ be nuclear and satisfy $upright("(F_n)")$ for one fixed
    $n gt.eq 1$. Let $a \, y in C_(+)$ be contractions, with $a$ rich, and let $c in tilde(C)$ be a contraction.
    There are positive contractions $p_1 in "Her" \( a \)$ and $q_1 in "Her" \( y \)$ such that $p_1$ is rich,
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(6.10)")
 $ parallel pi \( a \) parallel > 1 \/ 2 & quad arrow.r.double quad parallel pi \( p_1 \) parallel = 1 \,\
parallel pi \( y \) parallel > 1 \/ 2 & quad arrow.r.double quad parallel pi \( q_1 \) parallel = 1 wide
  upright("for every representation ") pi \, $ <eq:nuclear-shrinking-signals>]

 and $parallel e_(p_1) c e_(q_1) parallel_(C^(* *)) < 1$.]
] <prop:shrinking-nuclear>

#proof[
  Choose $ell$ from Lemma 18, set $k = ell n^2$, and choose graph coefficients $gamma_g \,
    sigma_g > 0$ with $gamma_g^2 + k sigma_g^2 < 1$. With these choices fixed,
    apply Proposition 8 to obtain its measurements $X \, B_i$.
    Group the $B_i$ into $ell$ matrices $B_j = \[ B_(j \, p q) \] in M_n \( C \)$,
    and choose the corresponding $S_(j \, p q)$ by Proposition 19.
    The graph gives a rich positive contraction $p in "Her" \( a \)$ with $parallel pi \( p \) parallel =
    1$ whenever $parallel pi \( a \) parallel > 1 \/ 2$. If $d =
    y \( 1 - c^(*) p^2 c \) y$ vanishes in a representation where $parallel pi \( y \) parallel > s$,
    the graph would give all of (6.6), contrary to the choice of the entries. Thus
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(6.11)")
   $ pi #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] y \( 1 - c^(*) p^2 c \) y #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] = 0 quad
     arrow.r.double quad parallel pi \( y \) parallel lt.eq s = 1 \/ 4 $ <eq:nonvanishing-N>]
  for every representation. Lemma 9 applies to this $p$, its norm signal, and the displayed implication,
    and gives the asserted $p_1 \, q_1$. The one fixed $n$ controlled both the number of entries and the algebra
    $M_n \( C \)$ in the quotient argument; only the finite dimensions $N_r$ varied. ◻
]

=== 6.3 Local rich elements and their merger <local-rich-elements-and-their-merger>

The shrinking proposition will let us join rich elements once they are available.
  For $u in C_(+)$, we first produce such an element inside $"Her" \( u \)$ at each irreducible representation
  that detects $u$. This local construction needs only the fixed-amplification hypothesis $upright("(F_n)")$;
  nuclearity will enter when the elements are joined.

#prodef([Lemma 6.5.])[
  (A rich element detected by one representation).  #emph[Let $C$ be a $C^(*)$-algebra,
    and suppose that one integer $n gt.eq 1$ makes $h^(xor n)$ properly infinite for every $h in C_(+)$.
    If $u in C_(+)$ and an irreducible representation $pi : C arrow.r
    B \( cal(H) \)$ satisfies $pi \( u \) eq.not 0$, then there is a rich positive contraction $p in
    "Her" \( u \)$ with $parallel pi \( p \) parallel = 1$.]
] <lem:local-rich>

#proof[
  We will find $n$ orthogonal targets in $"Her" \( u \)$, each detected by $pi$,
    and one positive element comparing into every target. Transporting $n$ copies of its cutoff will allow
    $upright("(F_n)")$ to produce richness inside $"Her" \( u \)$.
  Put $cal(H)_u = overline(pi \( u \) cal(H))$. This space is infinite dimensional.
    Otherwise $pi \( u \)$ would have finite rank $r > 0$. Applying the stabilized representation to proper
    infiniteness of $u^(xor n)$ would approximate $pi \( u \)^(xor 2 n)$, of rank $2 n r$,
    by positive operators of rank at most $n r$. On the $2 n r$-dimensional range of the target,
    each such approximant has a unit vector in its kernel. Its distance from the target is therefore at least the
    least positive eigenvalue of $pi \( u \)$, contradicting norm approximation.
  The action of $D = "Her" \( u \)$ on $cal(H)_u$ is irreducible.
    Indeed, $pi \( u \)$ has no kernel on its support, so $pi \( u \) xi eq.not 0$ for $0 eq.not xi in cal(H)_u$.
    Irreducibility of $pi$ gives $overline(pi \( C \) pi \( u \) xi) = cal(H)$.
    Applying $pi \( u \)$ and taking closure yields
  $ overline(pi \( u C u \) xi) = cal(H)_u . $
  Thus every nonzero vector is cyclic for the restricted hereditary algebra, which proves irreducibility.
  Apply the selfadjoint form of Kadison transitivity #cite(label("Kadison1957")) Theorem 1 to the irreducible
    unitization of this action. Choose $n + 1$ orthonormal vectors and prescribe $n +
    1$ distinct real eigenvalues. A selfadjoint interpolant has the form $h_0 + lambda 1$,
    with $h_0 in D_(upright("sa"))$. The corresponding eigenvalues of $pi \( h_0 \)$ remain distinct,
    so at most one is zero. Choose $n$ nonzero ones. Nonnegative continuous functions supported in pairwise
    disjoint neighborhoods of these values, avoiding zero and positive at the chosen values, give orthogonal elements
  $ b_1 \, dots.h \, b_n in D_(+) \, wide pi \( b_i \) eq.not 0 . $
  The functions vanish at zero, so these elements belong to $D$ also when $D$ is nonunital.
  There is an element $b' in D_(+)$, still detected by $pi$, with $b' prec.tilde b_i$ for every $i$.
    Start with $e' = b_1$. Given the current $e'$ and the next $b_i$,
    choose a vector on which $pi \( e' \)$ is nonzero. Cyclicity for $D$ and nonzero $pi \( b_i \)$ give $h in
    D$ such that $pi \( z \) eq.not 0$, where $z = b_i h e'$. Then
  $ z^(*) z in "Her" \( e' \) \, wide z z^(*) in "Her" \( b_i \) \, wide z^(*) z tilde.op_(upright("Cu")) z z^(*) . $
  Replacing $e'$ by $z^(*) z$ preserves nonzero image and every previous comparison, by transitivity.
    At the end we obtain $b'$ and
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(6.12)")
   $ \( b' \)^(xor n) prec.tilde b_1 xor dots.h.c xor b_n tilde.op_(upright("Cu")) sum_(i = 1)^n b_i prec.tilde
     u . $ <eq:local-rich-comparison>]
  The middle equivalence uses orthogonality, and the last comparison uses $sum_i b_i in "Her" \( u \)$.
  Choose $tau > 0$ with $2 tau < parallel pi \( b' \) parallel$.
    Transport the $n$ source copies in (6.12), after cutoff $tau$, into $"Her" \( u \)$.
    Let $t$ be a ramp of $b'$, zero on $\[ 0 \, tau \]$ and one on $\[ 2 tau \, oo \)$.
    It belongs to the common cutoff hereditary algebra and has represented norm one.
    The sum $p$ of its $n$ orthogonal transported copies is a positive contraction in $"Her" \( u \)$ with
    $parallel pi \( p \) parallel = 1$. By (2.7), for every $epsilon gt.eq 0$,
  $ \( p - epsilon \)_(+) tilde.op_(upright("Cu")) \( \( t - epsilon \)_(+) \)^(xor n) . $
  The right side is properly infinite by the hypothesis applied to the scalar positive $\( t - epsilon \)_(+)$.
    Thus $p$ is rich. ◻
]

We now join two rich elements while retaining the union of their norm signals.
  At each step Proposition 20 is used with $c = 1$ in the unitization.
  Its support bound is one global norm below one for that step; the numerical bound may change at the next step.

#prodef([Lemma 6.6.])[
  (Merging two rich elements).  #emph[Let $C$ be nuclear and satisfy $upright("(F_n)")$ for a fixed $n gt.eq 1$.
    For $u in C_(+)$ and rich positive contractions $a \, b in "Her" \( u \)$,
    there is a rich positive contraction $c_0 in "Her" \( u \)$ such that every representation $pi$ satisfies
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(6.13)")
 $ #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] parallel pi \( a \) parallel > 1 \/ 2 med upright("or") med
   parallel pi \( b \) parallel > 1 \/ 2 #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] quad arrow.r.double quad
   parallel pi \( c_0 \) parallel = 1 . $ <eq:merger-signal>]

]
] <lem:merger>

#proof[
  All comparisons remain in $C ⊗ cal(K)$, and all uses of shrinking are in $C$.
    Since $b$ is properly infinite, $b^(xor n) prec.tilde b$.
    Apply Lemma 5 with cutoff $1 \/ 8$ to obtain orthogonal copy maps
  $ theta_i \( h \) = w_i h w_i^(*) : "Her" \( \( b - 1 \/ 8 \)_(+) \) arrow.r
    "Her" \( b \) subset "Her" \( u \) quad \( 1 lt.eq i lt.eq n \) . $
  Let $y_0$ be a ramp of $b$, zero on $\[ 0 \, 1 \/ 4 \]$ and one on $\[ 1 \/ 2 \, 1 \]$, and set $a_0 = a$.
    For $1 lt.eq j lt.eq n$ we construct positive contractions $a_j \,
    y_j$ such that $a_j$ is rich and belongs to $"Her" \( u \)$,
    $y_j$ belongs to the common domain of the copy maps, and the following invariants hold:
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(6.14)")
   $ a_j theta_i \( y_j \) = 0 wide \( 1 lt.eq i lt.eq j \) \,\
parallel pi \( a \) parallel > 1 \/ 2 med arrow.r.double med parallel pi \( a_j \) parallel = 1 \,\
parallel pi \( b \) parallel > 1 \/ 2 med arrow.r.double med parallel pi \( y_j \) parallel = 1 quad
  upright("for every representation ") pi . $ <eq:merger-induction>]
  Once the induction reaches $j = n$, we will set $c_0 = a_n + sum_(i = 1)^n theta_i \( y_n \)$.
    The induction will make the summands orthogonal, and the final use of $upright("(F_n)")$ will prove the sum of
    the $n$ copies rich without requiring $y_n$ itself to be rich.
  Suppose $a_(j - 1) \, y_(j - 1)$ have been constructed. At $j = 1$, use the initial pair $a_0 \, y_0$;
    at later stages use the preceding invariants. Apply Proposition 20 to $a_(j - 1) \, theta_j \( y_(j - 1) \)$,
    and $1 in tilde(C)$, and call its outputs $p_j \, q_j$. Thus $p_j$ is rich and belongs to
    $"Her" \( a_(j - 1) \)$, while
  $ kappa_j = parallel e_(p_j) e_(q_j) parallel < 1 . $
  The hereditary-image equality (2.4) supplies a positive contraction $z_j in "Her" \( y_(j - 1) \)$ such that
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(6.15)")
   $ q_j = theta_j \( z_j \) . $ <eq:merger-pullback>]
  This pullback is an algebra element. Its represented norms equal those of $q_j$ by (2.5).
  For $0 lt.eq t lt.eq 1$, put $f \( t \) = \( 2 t - 1 \)_(+)$ and $g \( t \) = min { 1 \, 2 t }$.
    We next move $p_j$ away from a cutoff of $q_j$. The support bound ensures that multiplication by $1 -
    g \( q_j \)$ is bounded below on the support of $p_j$. The polar part of $\( 1 - g \( q_j \) \)
    p_j$ will therefore transport $p_j$, preserving its cutoff Cuntz classes and represented norms. Define
  $ y_j = f \( z_j \) \, wide H_j = 1 - g \( q_j \) \, wide T_j = H_j p_j . $
  Here $T_j = p_j - g \( q_j \) p_j in "Her" \( u \)$. In the universal representation,
    $g \( q_j \)$ is supported under $e_(q_j)$. For $xi in e_(p_j) cal(H)$, this gives
  $ parallel H_j xi parallel^2 gt.eq parallel \( 1 - e_(q_j) \) H_j xi parallel^2 =
    parallel \( 1 - e_(q_j) \) xi parallel^2 gt.eq \( 1 - kappa_j^2 \) parallel xi parallel^2 . $
  Since $H_j$ is a contraction, we obtain the order bounds
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(6.16)")
   $ \( 1 - kappa_j^2 \) p_j^2 lt.eq T_j^(*) T_j lt.eq p_j^2 . $ <eq:merger-polar-order>]
  Let $T_j = l_j \| T_j \|$ be its polar decomposition in the bidual.
    The order bounds give $"Her" \( \| T_j \| \) = "Her" \( p_j \)$ and $l_j^(*) l_j = e_(p_j)$.
    The polar membership argument in the proof of Lemma 5, applied now to $T_j$, gives
  $ a_j = l_j p_j l_j^(*) in "Her" \( T_j T_j^(*) \) subset "Her" \( u \) . $
  It is a positive contraction. The same argument makes each transported cutoff Cuntz equivalent to the
