    $l_j^(*) l_j = e_(p_j)$; conjugating back by $l_j^(*)$ shows that $a_j$ and $p_j$ have equal norm in every
    representation.
  We check that this change preserves every earlier orthogonality relation.
    Since $g f = f$, functional calculus and (6.15) give
  $ theta_j \( y_j \) H_j = f \( q_j \) \( 1 - g \( q_j \) \) = 0 \, $
  and hence $theta_j \( y_j \) T_j = 0$. For $i < j$, the previous orthogonality and $p_j in
    "Her" \( a_(j - 1) \)$ give $theta_i \( y_(j - 1) \) p_j = 0$,
