]

=== 6.4 Finite ideal assembly and absorption <finite-ideal-assembly-and-absorption>

Lemma 21 supplies a rich element wherever $u$ is detected,
  and Lemma 22 joins any finite family while retaining all of its norm signals.
  These two facts put $\( u - epsilon \)_(+)$, for every $epsilon > 0$, in the ideal of a single rich element.
  Ideal comparison then supplies the doubled cutoff comparison needed for Theorem 17.

#proof[
  Fix $u in C_(+)$, and let $cal(R)_u$ be the set of rich positive contractions in $"Her" \( u \)$.
    Let $J$ be the closed ideal generated by all $\( p - 1 \/ 2 \)_(+)$ with $p in cal(R)_u$.
    We claim that $u in J$. Otherwise an irreducible representation of $C \/ J$ would detect $u + J$.
    Its composition with the quotient map is an irreducible representation of $C$,
    so Lemma 21 gives $p in cal(R)_u$ with represented norm one.
    Then $\( p - 1 \/ 2 \)_(+)$ has nonzero image, contrary to its membership in $J$.
  The ideals generated by finite subfamilies of these cutoffs form an increasing directed family with dense union
    in $J$. For $epsilon > 0$, Lemma 6(i) therefore puts $\( u - epsilon \)_(+)$ in the ideal generated by
    $\( p_i - 1 \/ 2 \)_(+)$ for finitely many $p_1 \, dots.h \, p_r in cal(R)_u$.
    Starting from the rich zero element, merge them successively by Lemma 22.
    The result is a rich $p in "Her" \( u \)$ for which every representation satisfies
  $ parallel pi \( p_i \) parallel > 1 \/ 2 upright(" for some ") i quad arrow.r.double quad
    parallel pi \( p \) parallel = 1 . $
  A norm-one signal survives the next merger because it is above $1 \/ 2$.
    The primitive ideal test in Lemma 6(ii) now gives $\( p_i - 1 \/ 2 \)_(+) in "Id" \( p \)$ for every $i$.
    Consequently
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(6.17)")
   $ \( u - epsilon \)_(+) in "Id" \( p \) . $ <eq:nuclear-final-ideal>]
  If the cutoff is zero, the zero element already suffices.
  Since $p$ is properly infinite, ideal comparison with this target in the stabilization,
    followed by hereditary comparison, yields
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(6.18)")
   $ \( \( u - epsilon \)_(+) \)^(xor 2) prec.tilde p prec.tilde u . $ <eq:nuclear-final-comparison>]
  The target $u$ is fixed as $epsilon arrow.b 0$. Explicitly, choose $epsilon_j arrow.b 0$ and, for each $j$,
    a comparison coefficient in (6.18) with error less than $epsilon_j$ to the doubled cutoff.
    That cutoff differs from $u xor u$ by at most $epsilon_j$.
    The resulting coefficients therefore compare $u xor u$ to $u$ in norm. Thus $u$ is properly infinite.
    The fixed $n$ was applied only to scalar positives and their scalar cutoffs throughout the proof. ◻
]

#prodef([Remark 6.7.])[
  (Extension of the tuple proof to exact algebras).  The amplification-removal argument uses nuclearity only to
    obtain exactness of $E = Psi \( M_n \( C \) \)$ in Proposition 19.
    Quotients of exact $C^(*)$-algebras are exact, including in the nonseparable case #cite(label("Blackadar"))
    IV.3.4.20. Finite matrix amplification also preserves exactness,
    since its tensor products and ideal kernels are the corresponding finite matrices over those for $C$.
    This deeper quotient-permanence theorem therefore makes the same avoidance, shrinking, merger,
    and ideal assembly valid when $C$ is merely exact. It gives another proof of the amplification-removal
    conclusion of Theorem 2. The perturbation and localization proof below obtains that conclusion without
    invoking quotient permanence of exactness.
] <rem:tuple-exact>

#proof[
  Set $C = A$, the separable nuclear algebra in the corollary, with its stated fixed-amplification hypothesis.
    Theorem 17 gives $upright("(P)")$, and Theorem 1 gives strong pure infiniteness.
    The general nonunital absorption implication is recorded in the paragraph following #cite(label("KS"))
    Definition 3.1. We spell out the central-sequence interface that includes the nonunital case.
  Let $C_omega$ be the norm ultrapower of $C$ along a free ultrafilter on $bb(N)$, with $C$ embedded diagonally, and put
  $ F \( C \) = frac(C_omega ∩ C', "Ann" \( C \, C_omega \)) \, wide "Ann" \( C \, C_omega \) = { x in C_omega :
    x C = C x = 0 } . $
  Suppose first that $C eq.not 0$. Separability makes $C$ $sigma$-unital,
    so $F \( C \)$ is unital by #cite(label("Kirchberg2006")) Corollary 1.10(1).
    The central-sequence embedding statement immediately before #cite(label("Kirchberg2006")) Proposition 4.4
    gives a unital embedding $cal(O)_oo arrow.r.hook F \( C \)$:
    its ambient original algebra is the strongly purely infinite $C$,
    and the diagonal copy of $C$ is the required separable nuclear subalgebra of $C_omega$.
    The algebra $cal(O)_oo$ is unital and separable, and Remark 4.6 supplies self-absorption and connectedness of
    its unitary group. The “in particular” clause of Proposition 4.4(4) therefore gives stable
    $cal(O)_oo$-absorption. Since $U \( cal(O)_oo \) = U_0 \( cal(O)_oo \)$,
    where $U_0$ is the identity component, the commutator subgroup of $U \( cal(O)_oo \)$ lies in
    $U_0 \( cal(O)_oo \)$; part (5) therefore removes stabilization. Hence $C tilde.equiv C ⊗_min cal(O)_oo$.
    The Proposition permits $C$ to be nonunital; the unital embedding is into the annihilator quotient
    $F \( C \)$. If $C = 0$, the asserted isomorphism is immediate. ◻
]

== 7 Uniform perturbation from exactness <sec:exactness>

We now begin the exact proof that (F$""_n$) implies (P). Its first step is a perturbation estimate that applies
  simultaneously to every primitive quotient in which a fixed positive element has norm at least a prescribed
  positive threshold. The constant in that estimate comes from the following obstruction:
  there is one positive error threshold, independent of the unital exact algebra and the matrix size,
  below which a contraction cannot contain approximately reducing copies of every finite matrix contraction.

=== 7.1 The universal contraction obstruction <the-universal-contraction-obstruction>

Recall that a $C^(*)$-algebra $B$ is #emph[exact] if, for every closed two-sided ideal $I$ of every
  $C^(*)$-algebra $C$, the canonical map

$ \( B ⊗_min C \) \/ \( B ⊗_min I \) arrow.r B ⊗_min \( C \/ I \) $

is an isomorphism. Here and below, homomorphisms of $C^(*)$-algebras are $*$-homomorphisms.
  In the obstruction, the same finite-dimensional copy must approximately intertwine both a contraction and its adjoint.

#prodef([Proposition 7.1.])[
  (Uniform gap).  #emph[There is a constant $gamma > 0$ such that no unital exact $C^(*)$-algebra $B$ contains a
    contraction $v$ with the following property: for every integer $d gt.eq
    1$ and every contraction $D in M_d \( bb(C) \)$, there are a unital representation $pi : B arrow.r
    B \( H_pi \)$ and an isometry $J : bb(C)^d arrow.r H_pi$ satisfying
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(7.1)")
 $ parallel pi \( v \) J - J D parallel < gamma \, wide parallel pi \( v \)^(*) J -
   J D^(*) parallel < gamma . $ <eq:forbidden-models>]

 The representation and the isometry are allowed to depend on $d$ and $D$.]
] <prop:uniform-gap>

The proof has two parts. The universal unital algebra of a contraction fails exactness,
  as witnessed by a tensor quotient. If exact targets admitted the proposed reducing copies with arbitrarily small
  common error, their evaluations would give a lower bound on every matrix norm of each fixed polynomial space.
  Holding that space and one evaluation fixed while an ideal approximate unit varies would force the tensor
  quotient map to be injective. The two relations in (7.1) are what allow this argument to test words containing
  both the generator and its adjoint.

Let $cal(U) = C^(*) \( 1 \, u \)$ be the universal unital $C^(*)$-algebra generated by a contraction.
  Thus every contraction $T$ on a Hilbert space determines a unital homomorphism $u mapsto T$.
  One construction is to complete the unital $*$-algebra of polynomials in $u \,
  u^(*)$ in the supremum seminorm over all such evaluations, after dividing out its null space.
  The seminorm of each polynomial is finite because a word in a contraction and its adjoint has norm at most one.

We shall use the following elementary quotient-norm formula.
  If $D$ is unital and $\( e_lambda \)$ is a positive contractive approximate unit of an ideal $I subset C$, then,
  for $z in D ⊗_min C$,

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(7.2)")
 $ parallel z + D ⊗_min I parallel = lim_lambda parallel z \( 1 ⊗ \( 1 - e_lambda \) \) parallel .
   $ <eq:tensor-quotient-norm>]

When $C$ is nonunital, the multiplication is performed in its unitization;
  its result still belongs to $D ⊗_min C$. Indeed, $1 ⊗ e_lambda$ is an approximate unit for $D ⊗_min I$.
  The product on the right represents the same quotient class as $z$, so its norm bounds the left side from below.
  For any $y in D ⊗_min I$, contractivity gives

$ limsup_lambda parallel z \( 1 ⊗ \( 1 - e_lambda \) \) parallel lt.eq parallel z - y parallel +
  limsup_lambda parallel y \( 1 ⊗ \( 1 - e_lambda \) \) parallel = parallel z - y parallel . $

Taking the infimum over $y$ proves the formula, including existence of the limit.

The formula also gives the form of subalgebra permanence that we need:
  exactness passes to unital $C^(*)$-subalgebras; see also #cite(label("Blackadar")) IV.3.4.3.
  For a unital inclusion $D subset B$, the spatial tensor norms on $D ⊗
  C$ and $D ⊗ \( C \/ I \)$ are the norms inherited from the corresponding tensor products with $B$.
  Formula (7.2) therefore gives, for $z in D ⊗_min C$,

$ parallel z + D ⊗_min I parallel = parallel z + B ⊗_min I parallel . $

If $B$ is exact, this equals $parallel \( "id"_D ⊗ q \) z parallel$, where $q : C arrow.r
  C \/ I$ is the quotient map. This proves exactness of $D$.

#prodef([Lemma 7.2.])[
  #emph[The universal contraction algebra $cal(U)$ is not exact.]
] <lem:nonexact-universal>

#proof[
  We first embed the full group algebra $Q = C^(*) \( bb(F)_2 \)$ into $cal(U)$. The two free generators are sent to
  $ exp \( 2 pi i "Re" u \) \, wide exp \( 2 pi i "Im" u \) . $
  To see that the resulting homomorphism is injective, take any pair of unitaries $S \, T$ on a Hilbert space.
    Bounded Borel functional calculus gives selfadjoint contractions $H \, K$ such that $S =
    exp \( pi i H \)$ and $T = exp \( pi i K \)$. The contraction $\( H + i K \) \/
    2$ evaluates the two displayed unitaries to $S \, T$. Thus every unital representation of $Q$ is a restriction
    of an evaluation of $cal(U)$, and the homomorphism is isometric.
    The logarithms need only belong to the bounded operators on the representation space.
    Courtney and Sherman give a related embedding with a different normalization in #cite(label("CS")) Theorem 6.10.
  Nonexactness of $C^(*) \( bb(F)_2 \)$ was established by Wassermann #cite(label("Wassermann1976")).
    Here is an explicit proof for $Q$. We construct a representation of $Q ⊗_min Q$ that annihilates $Q ⊗_min I$,
    where $I$ is the kernel of the regular quotient. Exactness would make it descend to $Q ⊗_min \( Q \/ I \)$.
    A concrete tensor sum, however, has spatial norm in that algebra smaller than the norm the proposed descended
    representation would assign to it. This will exhibit the required failure of the tensor quotient map.
    Put $G = bb(F)_2$, and write $u_s$ for its canonical unitaries in $Q$. On $ell^2 \( G \)$ let
  $ lambda_s delta_g = delta_(s g) \, wide rho_s delta_g = delta_(g s^(- 1)) . $
  There is a state $phi$ on $Q ⊗_min Q$ with
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(7.3)")
   $ phi \( u_s ⊗ u_t \) = cases(delim: "{", 1 \, & s = t \,, 0 \, & s eq.not t .) $ <eq:diagonal-state>]
  For completeness, finite quotients supplying this state can be constructed directly.
    On the finite word ball of radius $m$ in $G$, partially defined left translation by each of the two generators
    is an injection between subsets of equal size, hence extends to a permutation of the ball.
    Freeness gives a homomorphism $q_m$ from $G$ to the finite permutation group generated by these extensions.
    The inverse of each extension agrees with inverse left translation whenever both endpoints lie in the ball.
    If a reduced word has length at most $m$, applying its permutations to the identity follows its successive
    suffixes inside the ball and ends at that word. Consequently $q_m$ distinguishes distinct elements of the word
    ball of radius $m$.
  For the finite group $F_m = q_m \( G \)$, its commuting left and right regular representations give a
    representation of $C^(*) \( F_m \) ⊗_min C^(*) \( F_m \)$:
    these algebras are finite direct sums of matrix algebras,
    for which the spatial and maximal tensor norms agree. Pulling its vector state at the identity back to
    $Q ⊗_min Q$ gives the coefficient $bold(1)_(q_m \( s \) = q_m \( t \))$ on $u_s ⊗ u_t$.
    These coefficients tend to those in (7.3). The group tensors span a dense $*$-subalgebra,
    and all the states have norm one, so their limit on that span extends to a state on the spatial completion.
  The GNS representation of $phi$ is precisely
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(7.4)")
   $ u_s ⊗ u_t mapsto lambda_s rho_t quad upright("on ") ell^2 \( G \) \, $ <eq:left-right-gns>]
  with cyclic vector $delta_e$. Indeed the cyclic vector associated with $u_s ⊗
    u_t$ corresponds to $delta_(s t^(- 1))$; the inner products agree because
  $ s^(- 1) a = t^(- 1) b quad arrow.l.r.double quad s t^(- 1) = a b^(- 1) \, $
  and left multiplication by $u_x ⊗ u_y$ becomes $lambda_x rho_y$. These vectors span $ell^2 \( G \)$.
  Let $I = ker \( Q arrow.r C_r^(*) \( G \) \)$. The linear unitary $R delta_g =
    delta_(g^(- 1))$ satisfies $R lambda_s R^(*) = rho_s$. Thus the representation in (7.4) annihilates $Q ⊗_min
    I$. Exactness of $Q$ would make it descend to a representation of $Q ⊗_min C_r^(*) \( G \)$,
    in which every $u_s ⊗ lambda_s$ fixes $delta_e$.
  This contradicts a spatial norm bound. In a faithful unital representation $pi : Q arrow.r B \( H \)$, the unitary
  $ V \( xi ⊗ delta_g \) = pi \( u_g \) xi ⊗ delta_g $
  satisfies $V \( 1 ⊗ lambda_s \) V^(*) = pi \( u_s \) ⊗ lambda_s$. If $a \, b$ are the free generators, the norm of
  $ u_a ⊗ lambda_a + u_(a^(- 1)) ⊗ lambda_(a^(- 1)) + u_b ⊗ lambda_b + u_(b^(- 1)) ⊗ lambda_(b^(- 1)) $
  in this faithful spatial representation is therefore the norm of the adjacency operator on the $4$-regular tree.
    That norm is at most $2 sqrt(3) < 4$. To check the bound, orient each edge away from the identity,
    and for a finitely supported vector $xi$ bound the contribution of a parent–child edge by
  $ 2 \| xi \( p \) xi \( c \) \| lt.eq 3^(- 1 \/ 2) \| xi \( p \) \|^2 + 3^(1 \/ 2) \| xi \( c \) \|^2 . $
  Every nonroot vertex has three children and one parent, and hence total coefficient $2 sqrt(3)$;
    the root has coefficient $4 \/ sqrt(3) lt.eq 2 sqrt(3)$. The quadratic-form bound extends by density and
    proves the asserted operator norm bound. In the purported descended representation the same sum has eigenvalue
    $4$ on $delta_e$, which is impossible for a $*$-representation. Thus $Q$ is not exact.
    Its unital inclusion in $cal(U)$ and the inheritance proved above show that $cal(U)$ is not exact either. ◻
]

We have obtained the algebraic obstruction. To turn it into a fixed error bound,
  we next test polynomial norms on finite contractions and control those tests at every matrix amplification.
  The latter uniformity will allow one polynomial model to be held fixed while an ideal approximate unit varies.

#prodef([Lemma 7.3.])[
  (Finite matrix tests).  #emph[For every integer $r gt.eq 1$ and every $Z in
    M_r \( cal(U) \)$ whose entries are $*$-polynomials in $u \, u^(*)$,
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(7.5)")
 $ parallel Z parallel_(M_r \( cal(U) \)) =
   sup_(d gt.eq 1 \, med D in M_d \( bb(C) \) \, med parallel D parallel lt.eq 1) parallel Z \( D \, D^(*) \)
   parallel . $ <eq:finite-matrix-tests>]

]
] <lem:finite-matrix-tests>

#proof[
  Contractivity of evaluations proves one inequality. Every unital representation of $cal(U)$ is evaluation at the
    image of $u$, so these evaluations also compute the matrix norm on the left.
    For the other inequality, evaluate at a contraction $T in B \( H \)$,
    and take a unit vector $xi = \( xi_1 \, dots.h \, xi_r \) in
    H^r$ arbitrarily close to attaining the norm of $Z \( T \, T^(*) \)$.
    If the entries of $Z$ have degree at most $m$, let
  $ H_0 = "span" { w \( T \, T^(*) \) xi_j : \| w \| lt.eq m \, med 1 lt.eq j lt.eq r } \, $
  where the empty word is included. This is a finite-dimensional nonzero space.
    Set $D = P_(H_0) T \|_(H_0)$, so that $D^(*) = P_(H_0) T^(*) \|_(H_0)$.
    Applying a word successively from the right never changes the relevant vectors under compression,
    since all its suffix vectors lie in $H_0$. Hence
  $ w \( D \, D^(*) \) xi_j = w \( T \, T^(*) \) xi_j quad \( \| w \| lt.eq m \) \, $
  and $Z \( D \, D^(*) \) xi = Z \( T \, T^(*) \) xi$. Taking the supremum over evaluations and over these vectors
    proves (7.5). The scalar norm-test formula is proved in #cite(label("CS")) Proposition 2.2;
    the finite-word compression used here is the argument in the proof of #cite(label("CS")) Theorem 3.1.
    Including all components of the test vector gives the matrix-valued assertion needed here. ◻
]

#prodef([Lemma 7.4.])[
  (A bound at every amplification).  #emph[Let $E subset cal(U)$ be a finite-dimensional linear space consisting
    of $*$-polynomials in $u \, u^(*)$. There is a finite constant $C_E gt.eq 0$ with the following property.
    Suppose $v$ is a contraction in a unital $C^(*)$-algebra $B$, and fix one number $theta > 0$.
    Assume that for every integer $d gt.eq 1$ and every contraction $D in M_d \( bb(C) \)$,
    there are a unital representation $pi : B arrow.r B \( H_pi \)$ and an isometry $J : bb(C)^d arrow.r
    H_pi$ satisfying the two inequalities in (7.1) with $theta$ in place of $gamma$.
    For the homomorphism $Phi : cal(U) arrow.r B$ with $Phi \( u \) = v$, one then has
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(7.6)")
 $ parallel Phi^(\( r \)) \( Z \) parallel gt.eq \( 1 - C_E theta \) parallel Z parallel
   wide \( r gt.eq 1 \, med Z in M_r \( E \) \) . $ <eq:all-amplification-bound>]

]
] <lem:uniform-matrix-bound>

#proof[
  If $E = 0$, take $C_E = 0$. Otherwise choose a basis $P_1 \, dots.h \, P_k$ of $E$,
    with coordinate functionals $f_1 \, dots.h \, f_k$, and choose polynomial representatives $P_i =
    sum_w a_(i w) w$. Define
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(7.7)")
   $ C_E = sum_(i = 1)^k parallel f_i parallel sum_w \| a_(i w) \| thin \| w \| . $ <eq:explicit-CE>]
  Constants are allowed in the representatives; the empty word has length zero.
    For $Z = sum_i B_i ⊗ P_i in M_r \( E \)$ and scalar unit vectors $alpha \, beta in bb(C)^r$,
  $ \| alpha^(*) B_i beta \| = \| f_i \( alpha^(*) Z beta \) \| lt.eq parallel f_i parallel parallel Z parallel . $
  It follows that $parallel B_i parallel lt.eq parallel f_i parallel parallel Z parallel$ for every matrix size $r$.
  Given a finite contraction $D$, choose $pi \, J$ as in the hypothesis.
    Both $pi \( v \)$ and $D$ are contractions, and telescoping the two intertwining relations gives
  $ parallel w \( pi \( v \) \, pi \( v \)^(*) \) J - J w \( D \, D^(*) \) parallel lt.eq \| w \| theta $
  for every word $w$, including words in both letters. More explicitly,
    the difference for a word $A_1 dots.h.c A_m$ is the sum of $m$ terms,
    each consisting of contractions on either side of one difference $pi \( v \) J - J D$ or $pi \( v \)^(*) J -
    J D^(*)$. Put $J_r = 1_(bb(C)^r) ⊗ J$. The coefficient bound and (7.7) yield
  $ parallel pi^(\( r \)) \( Phi^(\( r \)) \( Z \) \) J_r - J_r Z \( D \, D^(*) \) parallel lt.eq
    C_E theta parallel Z parallel . $
  Since $J_r$ is an isometry and $pi^(\( r \))$ is contractive,
  $ parallel Z \( D \, D^(*) \) parallel lt.eq parallel Phi^(\( r \)) \( Z \) parallel +
    C_E theta parallel Z parallel . $
  Taking the supremum supplied by Lemma 26 proves (7.6). In particular,
    $C_E$ depends on the fixed polynomial space and its chosen representatives, but on none of $r \, d \, D \,
    B \, v \, pi \, J$. ◻
]

#proof[
  If the Proposition were false, for every integer $j gt.eq
    1$ there would be a unital exact algebra $B_j$ and a contraction $v_j in B_j$ satisfying (7.1) with $1 \/
    j$ in place of $gamma$. Let $Phi_j : cal(U) arrow.r B_j$ send $u$ to $v_j$.
    Fix a finite-dimensional polynomial space $E$. Before choosing any matrix amplification, tensor factor,
    compression, or ideal, choose one integer $j = j \( E \) gt.eq max { 1 \, 2 C_E }$. Lemma 27 then gives
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(7.8)")
   $ parallel Z parallel lt.eq 2 parallel Phi_j^(\( r \)) \( Z \) parallel
     wide \( r gt.eq 1 \, med Z in M_r \( E \) \) . $ <eq:fixed-j-matrix-bound>]
  This same $j$ works after tensoring with an arbitrary $C^(*)$-algebra $C$: for every $z in E ⊙ C$,
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(7.9)")
   $ parallel z parallel lt.eq 2 parallel \( Phi_j ⊗ "id"_C \) z parallel \, $ <eq:fixed-j-tensor-bound>]
  where $⊙$ denotes the algebraic tensor product and all norms are spatial. The assertion is immediate if $C = 0$.
    Otherwise, represent $C$ faithfully on a nonzero Hilbert space $K$,
    and use faithful representations of $cal(U)$ and $B_j$ for their respective spatial tensor products.
    Compress the $C$ representation to a nonzero finite-dimensional subspace $K_0 subset K$.
    If $z = sum_i P_i ⊗ c_i$, its compression has coefficients $P_(K_0) c_i \|_(K_0) in M_d \( bb(C) \)$ and,
    after interchanging the tensor factors, belongs to $M_d \( E \)$.
    Inequality (7.8) bounds its norm by twice the norm of the corresponding compression on the $B_j$ side,
    which is at most $2 parallel \( Phi_j ⊗ "id"_C \) z parallel$.
    The norms of these compressions have supremum $parallel z parallel$:
    as $K_0$ increases, the projections $1 ⊗ P_(K_0)$ converge strongly to the identity,
    and their two-sided compressions converge strongly to $z$. This proves (7.9).
  Now fix any ideal $I subset C$, with quotient map $q : C arrow.r
    C \/ I$ and a positive contractive approximate unit $\( e_lambda \)$ of $I$.
    For $z in E ⊙ C$, every $z \( 1 ⊗ \( 1 - e_lambda \) \)$ still belongs to $E ⊙ C$.
    Consequently (7.9) applies to the entire net with the single index $j \( E \)$.
    Formula (7.2) and exactness of $B_j$ give
  $ parallel z + cal(U) ⊗_min I parallel & = lim_lambda parallel z \( 1 ⊗ \( 1 - e_lambda \) \) parallel\
 & lt.eq 2 lim_lambda parallel \( Phi_j ⊗ "id"_C \) \( z \) \( 1 ⊗ \( 1 - e_lambda \) \) parallel\
 & = 2 parallel \( Phi_j ⊗ "id"_(C \/ I) \) \( \( "id"_(cal(U)) ⊗ q \) z \) parallel\
 & lt.eq 2 parallel \( "id"_(cal(U)) ⊗ q \) z parallel . $
  Finite sums of polynomial tensors are dense in $cal(U) ⊗_min C$.
    Although $j \( E \)$ may vary with the polynomial space, the resulting constant $2$ does not.
    The last inequality therefore extends by continuity to every $z in cal(U) ⊗_min C$.
    It says that the canonical surjective homomorphism
  $ kappa : \( cal(U) ⊗_min C \) \/ \( cal(U) ⊗_min I \) arrow.r cal(U) ⊗_min \( C \/ I \) $
  has zero kernel: if $kappa \( z + cal(U) ⊗_min I \) = 0$, the quotient class has norm zero.
    An injective $*$-homomorphism of $C^(*)$-algebras is isometric,
    so the fixed factor $2$ suffices to prove that $kappa$ is an isomorphism.
    Since $C \, I$ were arbitrary, $cal(U)$ would be exact, contrary to Lemma 25. ◻
]

Proposition 24 therefore supplies a single $gamma > 0$ for all unital exact targets and all matrix sizes.
  The remaining part of this section converts that obstruction into a perturbation with a quotient norm lower bound.

=== 7.2 Uniform perturbation in primitive quotients <sec:perturbation>

We now turn the uniform gap into a perturbation that works in many primitive quotients at once.
  The fixed amplification condition (F$""_n$) supplies finite reducing copies on a spectral cut;
  the gap then prevents one fixed element from nearly cancelling every such copy.
  We first record the two consequences of the comparison and transport results in Section 2 that this argument uses.

#prodef([Lemma 7.5.])[
  (Fixed-amplification ideal comparison).  #emph[Let $C$ be a $C^(*)$-algebra, let $h in C_(+)$, and let $n gt.eq
    1$ be an integer such that $h^(xor n)$ is properly infinite.
    For every $x in "Id" \( h \)_(+)$ and every integer $k gt.eq 1$,
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(7.10)")
 $ x^(xor k) prec.tilde h^(xor n) . $ <eq:ideal-multiplicity>]

 In particular, if $C$ satisfies (F$""_n$), this conclusion holds for every $h in C_(+)$ with the same $n$.]
] <lem:ideal-comparison>

#proof[
  In the common stabilization,
  $ "Id"_(C ⊗ cal(K)) \( h^(xor n) \) = "Id"_C \( h \) ⊗ cal(K) . $
  Thus $x^(xor k)$ belongs to the ideal generated by $h^(xor n)$.
    The proper-target ideal comparison in Lemma 4 gives (7.10).
    This argument takes place in the stabilization and therefore covers every finite rectangular corner and
    nonunital $C$. It also covers $h = 0$, when $x = 0$. ◻
]

#prodef([Lemma 7.6.])[
  (Exact implementation of a spectral cut).  #emph[Let $C$ be a $C^(*)$-algebra, let $m \, s gt.eq 1$,
    and suppose that $x in M_m \( C \)_(+)$ and $h in M_s \( C \)_(+)$ satisfy $x prec.tilde h$.
    If $f : \[ 0 \, parallel x parallel \] arrow.r \[ 0 \, oo \)$ is continuous and vanishes on a neighborhood of
    zero, then there is $R in M_(s \, m) \( C \)$ such that
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(7.11)")
 $ R^(*) R = f \( x \) \, wide R R^(*) in "Her" \( h \) \, wide parallel R parallel^2 =
   parallel f \( x \) parallel . $ <eq:exact-cut>]

]
] <lem:cut-implementation>

#proof[
  If $f \( x \) = 0$, take $R = 0$. Otherwise, choose $delta > 0$ so that $f$ vanishes on $\[ 0 \, delta \]$,
    and choose $0 < epsilon < delta$. Then $f \( x \)^(1 \/ 2) in "Her" \( \( x - epsilon \)_(+) \)$.
    Apply Lemma 5 to $x prec.tilde h$ at this cutoff, and let $w in
    M_(s \, m) \( C^(* *) \)$ be its rectangular partial isometry.
    Its initial projection is the support of $\( x - epsilon \)_(+)$.
    The membership and support conclusions of that lemma therefore give
  $ R = w f \( x \)^(1 \/ 2) in M_(s \, m) \( C \) \, wide R^(*) R = f \( x \) \, wide R R^(*) =
    w f \( x \) w^(*) in "Her" \( h \) . $
  The norm identity follows from $parallel R parallel^2 = parallel R^(*) R parallel$.
    Only the product $R$, rather than $w$ itself, is asserted to belong to the rectangular algebra. ◻
]

For $P in "Prim" \( C \)$, a subscript $P$ denotes the image in $C \/ P$, also at matrix levels.
  An irreducible representation with kernel $P$ faithfully represents this quotient and computes its norm.
  The primitive ideal test in Lemma 6 will let us recognize ideal membership from these quotient images.

The uniform gap will be applied to the unitization of a matrix algebra over $C$.
  We record the two permanence facts that make this a valid exact target, also when $C$ has no unit.

#prodef([Lemma 7.7.])[
  (The exact target for matrix models).  #emph[If $A$ is an exact $C^(*)$-algebra and $n gt.eq 1$,
    then $accent(M_n \( A \), ̃)$ is exact.]
] <lem:exact-matrix-unitization>

#proof[
  Finite matrix exactness follows, for every $C^(*)$-algebra $D_0$, from the identifications
  $ M_n \( A \) ⊗_min D_0 = M_n \( A ⊗_min D_0 \) \, $
  which preserve the ideal kernels in the definition of exactness.
    For the unitization step, let $A_0$ be a nonunital exact algebra,
    let $I lt.closed D_0$ be a closed two-sided ideal, and write $q_0 : D_0 arrow.r D_0 \/ I$.
    The scalar character $chi : tilde(A)_0 arrow.r bb(C)$ gives, for every $z in tilde(A)_0 ⊗_min D_0$,
    the bounded decomposition
  $ z = z_0 + 1 ⊗ d \, wide d = \( chi ⊗ "id" \) \( z \) \, quad z_0 in A_0 ⊗_min D_0 . $
  Indeed, both projections are bounded, and the decomposition holds first on algebraic tensors and then on their
    completion. If $\( "id" ⊗ q_0 \) \( z \) = 0$, applying $chi ⊗ "id"$ gives $d in I$. The isometric inclusion
  $ A_0 ⊗_min \( D_0 \/ I \) subset tilde(A)_0 ⊗_min \( D_0 \/ I \) $
  then shows $\( "id" ⊗ q_0 \) \( z_0 \) = 0$. Exactness of $A_0$ gives $z_0 in A_0 ⊗_min I$,
    hence $z in tilde(A)_0 ⊗_min I$. If $M_n \( A \)$ is already unital, this second step is unnecessary. ◻
]

Fix, once and for all, a constant

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(7.12)")
 $ 0 < sigma < min { 1 \, gamma \/ 3 } \, $ <eq:perturbation-constant>]

where $gamma$ is supplied by Proposition 24. This $sigma$ is independent of the algebra and of the fixed
  amplification integer.

#prodef([Proposition 7.8.])[
  (Uniform perturbation).  #emph[Let $C$ be an exact $C^(*)$-algebra satisfying (F$""_n$) for one fixed integer $n
    gt.eq 1$. Let $p \, q in C$ be contractions and let $h \, k \, c in C_(+)$ satisfy
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(7.13)")
 $ p^(*) p h = h \, wide q q^(*) k = k \, wide c^(xor n) prec.tilde h \, wide
   c^(xor n) prec.tilde k . $ <eq:perturbation-hypotheses>]

 For every $T in C$ and every $eta \, alpha > 0$, there is a contraction $y in C$ such that
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(7.14)")
 $ parallel \( T + eta p y q \)_P parallel gt.eq sigma eta quad
   upright("for every ") P in "Prim" \( C \) upright(" with ") parallel c_P parallel gt.eq
   alpha . $ <eq:perturbation-conclusion>]

 If $h \, k$ belong to a hereditary subalgebra $cal(D) subset C$, then $y$ may be chosen in $cal(D)$.
   If, in addition or independently, $p \, q$ are positive and $p q = 0$, then $y$ may be chosen selfadjoint.
   Both conclusions may be imposed simultaneously. The constant $sigma$ is independent of all the data in the
   Proposition, including $n$ and the norms of $T \, h \, k \, c$.]
] <prop:perturbation>

#proof[
  Write $K = { P in "Prim" \( C \) : parallel c_P parallel gt.eq alpha }$.
    If $K$ is empty, $y = 0$ satisfies all conclusions. Suppose henceforth that $K$ is nonempty.
    The contradiction will have three levels of choices. First we fix comparison rows and a single contraction in
    $M_n \( C \)$. Each finite matrix contraction then determines an admissible perturbation.
    If it fails, a primitive quotient witnessing that failure supplies an approximate reducing copy for that fixed
    contraction. All these copies therefore come from one element of the same exact algebra $accent(M_n \( C \), ̃)$.
  #emph[Fixed comparison rows.] Choose a continuous function $e : \[ 0 \, oo \)
    arrow.r \[ 0 \, 1 \]$ that vanishes near zero and is one on $\[ alpha \/ 2 \, oo \)$.
    Apply Lemma 29 to the last two comparisons in (7.13). This gives rows $R \, S in M_(1 \, n) \( C \)$ with
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(7.15)")
   $ R^(*) R = S^(*) S = E = e \( c \)^(xor n) \, wide R R^(*) in "Her" \( h \) \, quad
     S S^(*) in "Her" \( k \) \, wide parallel R parallel \, parallel S parallel lt.eq 1 . $ <eq:perturbation-rows>]
  The relation $p^(*) p h = h$, together with its adjoint, shows that left and right multiplication by $p^(*)
    p$ are the identity on $"Her" \( h \)$. Hence $\( 1 - p^(*) p \) R R^(*) \( 1 - p^(*) p \) = 0$,
    which implies $\( 1 - p^(*) p \) R = 0$. The same argument for $S$ gives
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(7.16)")
   $ p^(*) p R = R \, wide q q^(*) S = S . $ <eq:perturbation-row-identities>]
  At this point fix the single element
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(7.17)")
   $ V = eta^(- 1) R^(*) p^(*) T q^(*) S in M_n \( C \) . $ <eq:fixed-testing-element>]
  The rows $R \, S$ and the element $V$ will not depend on any finite matrix model. Fix now also the global contraction
  $ hat(V) = V \( max { 1 \, \| V \| } \)^(- 1) in M_n \( C \) . $
  The inverse is evaluated in the unitization, so its product with $V$ remains in $M_n \( C \)$.
  #emph[Reducing copies of arbitrary finite contractions.] Let $d gt.eq
    1$ and let $D in M_d \( bb(C) \)$ be a contraction. Put $c_1 = \( c - alpha \/ 2 \)_(+)$.
    Condition (F$""_n$) and Lemma 28, applied to the scalar element $c_1$, give
  $ c_1^(xor d) prec.tilde c_1^(xor n) . $
  Choose a continuous $phi : \[ 0 \, oo \) arrow.r \[ 0 \, 1 \]$,
    vanishing near zero and equal to one on $\[ alpha \/ 4 \, oo \)$, and put $e_1 \( c \) = phi \( c_1 \)$.
    Lemma 29 yields $L in M_(n \, d) \( C \)$ such that
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(7.18)")
   $ L^(*) L = e_1 \( c \)^(xor d) \, wide L L^(*) in "Her" \( c_1^(xor n) \) \, wide parallel L parallel lt.eq
     1 . $ <eq:finite-model-column>]
  Because $e \( c \) c_1 = c_1$, multiplication by $E$ is the identity on $"Her" \( c_1^(xor n) \)$.
    The range-square argument used for (7.16) gives $E L = L$. Define
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(7.19)")
   $ w = L D L^(*) in M_n \( C \) \, wide parallel w parallel lt.eq 1 \, wide E w E = w . $ <eq:finite-model>]
  Scalar matrix multiplication here requires no unit in $C$.
  For each $P in K$, choose an irreducible representation $pi_P : C arrow.r B \( H_P \)$ with kernel $P$,
    and use its matrix amplifications. Identify $C \/ P$ with the image of the induced faithful representation on
    $H_P$, and make the same identification at rectangular matrix levels.
    The spectral subspace $1_(\( 3 alpha \/ 4 \, oo \)) \( pi_P \( c \) \) H_P$ is nonzero,
    since $parallel c_P parallel gt.eq alpha$. Choose a unit vector $xi_P$ in that subspace,
    so that $pi_P \( e_1 \( c \) \) xi_P = xi_P$. Define
  $ J_P : bb(C)^d arrow.r H_P^(xor n) \, wide J_P lambda = L_P \( lambda ⊗ xi_P \) . $
  Equation (7.18) proves $J_P^(*) J_P = 1_d$. Both $D$ and $D^(*)$ preserve $bb(C)^d ⊗ xi_P$,
    and the same equation gives
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(7.20)")
   $ w_P J_P = J_P D \, wide w_P^(*) J_P = J_P D^(*) . $ <eq:reducing-model>]
  Thus one copy reduces both $w_P$ and its adjoint. No eigenvector at the norm of $c_P$,
    and no continuous choice of the vectors $xi_P$, is required.
  #emph[Admissible tests and the two optional conclusions.] For the chosen $d \, D$, take
  $ y_0 = - R w S^(*) in C \, wide parallel y_0 parallel lt.eq 1 . $
  If $h \, k in cal(D)$, then $R R^(*) \, S S^(*) in cal(D)$.
    For a positive contractive approximate unit $\( a_lambda \)$ of $cal(D)$, the identity
  $ parallel \( 1 - a_lambda \) R parallel^2 = parallel \( 1 - a_lambda \) R R^(*) \( 1 - a_lambda \) parallel
    arrow.r 0 $
  and its analogue for $S$ give $a_lambda R w S^(*) a_lambda arrow.r R w S^(*)$ in norm.
    Each approximant belongs to $cal(D) C cal(D) subset cal(D)$, so $y_0 in cal(D)$.
    This uses the range squares of the rows and makes no assertion that their individual entries belong to $cal(D)$.
  If $p \, q gt.eq 0$ and $p q = 0$, then $p^2 R = R$ implies $p R = R$:
    multiply $\( 1 - p^2 \) R = 0$ by $\( 1 + p \)^(- 1)$ in the unitization. Likewise $q S = S$.
    It follows that $p S = q R = 0$ and $S^(*) R = 0$. Consequently $y_0^2 = 0$ and $p y_0^(*) q = 0$.
    The positive elements $y_0 y_0^(*)$ and $y_0^(*) y_0$ are orthogonal, whence
  $ parallel y_0 + y_0^(*) parallel^2 = parallel y_0 y_0^(*) + y_0^(*) y_0 parallel =
    parallel y_0 parallel^2 lt.eq 1 . $
  We may therefore use $y = y_0 + y_0^(*)$ when selfadjointness is requested, and $y = y_0$ otherwise.
    In both cases $p y q = p y_0 q$; hereditary membership is preserved if it is also requested.
  #emph[One global contraction and the uniform gap.] Suppose that no admissible $y$ satisfies (7.14).
    For each $d \, D$, choose a witness $P in K$ for the test just constructed. Then
  $ parallel \( T - eta p R w S^(*) q \)_P parallel < sigma eta . $
  Multiplying by the contractive outer factors $R^(*) p^(*)$ and $q^(*) S$, and using (7.16), gives
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(7.21)")
   $ parallel \( V - E w E \)_P parallel = parallel \( V - w \)_P parallel < sigma . $ <eq:local-near-model>]
  Although $P$ may depend on $d \, D$, the element $V$ is the one fixed in (7.17).
  Functional calculus commutes with quotient maps for the fixed global clipping $hat(V)$. Hence in each witness quotient
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(7.22)")
   $ parallel V_P - hat(V)_P parallel = parallel \( \| V_P \| - 1 \)_(+)
     parallel = \( parallel V_P parallel - 1 \)_(+) < sigma . $ <eq:quotient-clipping>]
  The last strict inequality follows from (7.21) and $parallel w_P parallel lt.eq 1$.
    The clipping identity follows by squaring the absolute value of $V_P -
    V_P max { 1 \, \| V_P \| }^(- 1)$ and applying scalar functional calculus.
    Combining (7.21), (7.22), and (7.20) gives
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(7.23)")
   $ parallel hat(V)_P J_P - J_P D parallel < 2 sigma < gamma \, wide parallel hat(V)_P^(*) J_P -
     J_P D^(*) parallel < 2 sigma < gamma . $ <eq:forbidden-intertwining>]
  By Lemma 30, $B = accent(M_n \( C \), ̃)$ is a unital exact algebra.
    The amplified representations $pi_P^(\( n \))$ extend unitally to $B$.
    Although the witnessing $P$ varies with $d \, D$, the contraction $hat(V)$ and the exact algebra $B$ remain
    fixed. Thus $hat(V) in B$ has, for every $d \, D$, a unital representation and an isometry satisfying both
    inequalities in (7.23). This contradicts Proposition 24 and proves the Proposition with both optional
    conclusions and the same $sigma$. ◻
]

== 8 Exact localization and removal of amplification <sec:localization>

We now use Proposition 31 to prove the fixed-amplification part of Theorem 2.
  Throughout this section, let $C$ be an exact $C^(*)$-algebra satisfying (F$""_n$) for one fixed integer $n gt.eq
  1$, and retain the constant $0 < sigma < 1$ from (7.12). For $P in "Prim" \( C \)$, write $a_P = a + P$,
  with its norm computed in any irreducible representation having kernel $P$.
  A hereditary subalgebra $cal(D) subset C$ is nonzero at $P$ when $cal(D) subset.not P$.

Our immediate objective is to construct a square-zero element in a hereditary subalgebra that is nonzero on a
  prescribed quasi-compact subset of $"Prim" \( C \)$. Its initial and range squares are orthogonal and Cuntz
  equivalent. Repeating the construction on one fixed norm slice will remove the amplification integer $n$ and
  give (P). Theorem 1 will then supply the strong pure infiniteness conclusion.
  All finite covers in the construction concern the primitive ideal space;
  the partition of unity used below is on a real interval.

=== 8.1 Local elements and finite covers <local-elements-and-finite-covers>

The next lemma uses only (F$""_n$): finite-dimensional hereditary images are excluded,
  and irreducibility then supplies common positive elements locally.
  Exactness enters later through Proposition 31, when these local choices must work simultaneously on a whole
  quasi-compact set.

#prodef([Lemma 8.1.])[
  (Local common elements).  #emph[Let $C$ be an exact $C^(*)$-algebra satisfying (F$""_n$) for one fixed integer
    $n gt.eq 1$, and let $P in "Prim" \( C \)$.]
  + #emph[If $h in C_(+)$ and $h_P eq.not 0$, then for every integer $m gt.eq
    1$ there are $m$ pairwise orthogonal elements of $"Her" \( h \)_(+)$, each nonzero at $P$.]
  + #emph[If $h \, k in C_(+)$ are nonzero at $P$, there is $c in C_(+)$ such that $c_P eq.not 0$ and
$ c^(xor n) prec.tilde h \, wide c^(xor n) prec.tilde k . $

 If $h \, k$ belong to a hereditary subalgebra $cal(D)$, $c$ can also be chosen in $cal(D)$.]
] <lem:local-common-element>

#proof[
  Fix an irreducible representation $pi : C arrow.r B \( H_pi \)$ with kernel $P$.
    First, no nonzero $pi \( a \)$, $a in C_(+)$, has finite rank.
    Indeed, if its rank were $d in bb(N)$, applying the amplified representation to $a^(xor 2 n) prec.tilde
    a^(xor n)$ would express an operator of rank $2 n d$ as a norm limit of operators of rank at most $n d$.
    This is impossible, since the set of operators of rank at most a fixed finite integer is norm closed.
  Put $H_h = overline(pi \( h \) H_pi)$. We claim that the restriction of $pi \( "Her" \( h \) \)$ to $H_h$ is
    strongly dense in $B \( H_h \)$. Irreducibility and the bicommutant theorem #cite(label("Blackadar")) I.9.1.1
    give strong density of $pi \( C \)$ in $B \( H_pi \)$. Consequently the strong closure of
    $pi \( "Her" \( h \) \)$ contains $pi \( h \) B \( H_pi \) pi \( h \)$.
    For $t > 0$, let $E_t = 1_(\[ t \, oo \)) \( pi \( h \) \)$.
    Every operator $E_t T E_t$, with $T in B \( H_h \)$ extended by zero on $H_h^perp$,
    has the form $pi \( h \) S pi \( h \)$: the inverse of $pi \( h \)$ on $E_t H_pi$ is bounded.
    As $t arrow.b 0$, $E_t T E_t arrow.r T$ strongly on $H_h$. This proves the claim.
  The space $H_h$ is infinite dimensional by the finite-rank obstruction.
    Choose orthonormal vectors $xi_1 \, dots.h \, xi_m$ in $H_h$ and distinct numbers $lambda_1 \, dots.h \,
    lambda_m in \( 0 \, 1 \)$. Let $T in B \( H_h \)$ be the positive contraction with $T xi_j =
    lambda_j xi_j$ and zero on their orthogonal complement. Choose nonnegative continuous functions $f_j$ on
    $bb(R)$, with disjoint compact supports in $\( 0 \, 1 \)$ and $f_j \( lambda_j \) = 1$.
    Kaplansky density #cite(label("Blackadar")) I.9.1.3 supplies a bounded net of selfadjoint elements $b_lambda$
    of the represented hereditary algebra converging strongly to $T$.
    Continuous functional calculus on bounded selfadjoint sets is strongly continuous;
    this follows by uniform polynomial approximation on a common bounded interval.
    Hence $f_j \( b_lambda \) xi_j arrow.r xi_j$. One $b_lambda$ makes all these $m$ vectors nonzero.
    Lift it to a selfadjoint $b in "Her" \( h \)$. The elements $f_j \( b \)$ lie in $"Her" \( h \)$,
    are pairwise orthogonal, and are nonzero at $P$. The functions vanish at zero,
    so this argument also applies to a nonunital hereditary algebra. This proves (i).
  For two positives $u \, v$ nonzero at $P$, strong density of $pi \( C \)$ gives $x in C$ with $pi \( u x v \)
    eq.not 0$. For example, choose a vector on which $pi \( v \)$ is nonzero and a rank-one operator carrying its
    image to a vector not annihilated by $pi \( u \)$; approximate that operator strongly on the image under
    $pi \( v \)$. With $z = u x v$, the positive $d = z z^(*)$ satisfies
  $ d_P eq.not 0 \, wide d in "Her" \( u \) \, wide d tilde.op_(upright("Cu")) z^(*) z in "Her" \( v \) . $
  Here and below $tilde.op_(upright("Cu"))$ denotes Cuntz equivalence,
    and the comparison rules are those of Lemma 4. For a finite list of positives nonzero at $P$,
    repeating this operation produces one positive, nonzero at $P$, subequivalent to every element of the list.
    At each step the new element lies in the hereditary algebra of the preceding one,
    so all previous comparisons and any prescribed hereditary membership persist.
  Apply (i) with $m = n$ separately to $h$ and $k$, obtaining orthogonal lists $h_1 \, dots.h \, h_n$ and $k_1 \,
    dots.h \, k_n$. Take a common element $c$ for the resulting list of $2 n$ positives. Then
  $ c^(xor n) prec.tilde xor.big_(j = 1)^n h_j tilde.op_(upright("Cu")) sum_(j = 1)^n h_j prec.tilde h \, $
  and the same argument gives $c^(xor n) prec.tilde k$. The asserted choice in $cal(D)$ follows from the
    hereditary membership just observed. ◻
]

We record the norm-attainment fact that ensures a strict inequality in the finite-cover argument.

#prodef([Lemma 8.2.])[
  (Pure-state norm attainment).  #emph[If $B$ is a $C^(*)$-algebra and $0 eq.not b in B_(+)$,
    there is an irreducible representation $pi$ of $B$ with $parallel pi \( b \) parallel = parallel b parallel$.]
] <lem:purestateattainment>

#proof[
  In the state space of the unitization $tilde(B)$, the set
  $ F_b = { phi : phi \( b \) = parallel b parallel } $
  is nonempty: evaluation at the largest spectral value of $b$ on $C^(*) \( 1 \, b \)$ extends to a state of
    $tilde(B)$ by the positive Hahn–Banach extension theorem #cite(label("Blackadar")) II.6.3.1.
    It is a weak-star compact face, since every state has value at most $parallel b parallel$ on $b$.
    By the Krein–Milman theorem it has an extreme point, which is therefore a pure state of $tilde(B)$;
    see #cite(label("Blackadar")) II.6.3.2–3. Let $tilde(pi)$ be its irreducible GNS representation
    #cite(label("Blackadar")) II.6.4.8. It does not annihilate $B$, because its value on $b$ is positive.
    The closure of $tilde(pi) \( B \) H_(tilde(pi))$ is a nonzero reducing subspace for
    $tilde(pi) \( tilde(B) \)$, hence the whole space. If an operator commutes with $tilde(pi) \( B \)$,
    then its commutator with $tilde(pi) \( x \)$ annihilates $tilde(pi) \( B \) H_(tilde(pi))$ for every $x in
    tilde(B)$, since $x B subset B$. It therefore commutes with $tilde(pi) \( tilde(B) \)$.
    Thus the restriction $pi = tilde(pi) \|_B$ is nondegenerate and irreducible.
    The vector state gives $parallel pi \( b \) parallel gt.eq parallel b parallel$, and contractivity gives equality. ◻
]

#prodef([Lemma 8.3.])[
  (Primitive norm slices).  #emph[Let $C$ be a $C^(*)$-algebra. For $c in C_(+)$ and $alpha > 0$, the set
$ Omega \( c \, alpha \) = { P in "Prim" \( C \) : parallel c_P parallel > alpha } $

 is open, and
$ K \( c \, alpha \) = { P in "Prim" \( C \) : parallel c_P parallel gt.eq alpha } $

 is quasi-compact. No Hausdorff hypothesis is required.]
] <lem:primitive-compactness>

#proof[
  The first set is ${ P : \( c - alpha \)_(+) in.not P }$, which is open in the hull-kernel topology.
    For the second assertion, extend any relative open cover to ambient open sets. Write those sets as
  $ cal(O)_lambda = { P : J_lambda subset.not P } \, wide I = overline(sum_lambda J_lambda) \, $
  where each $J_lambda$ is a closed ideal of $C$. If $parallel c + I parallel gt.eq alpha$,
    Lemma 33 applied in $C \/ I$ gives an irreducible representation attaining that norm.
    Its pullback has a primitive kernel containing $I$, belongs to $K \( c \, alpha \)$,
    and lies outside every $cal(O)_lambda$. This contradicts the covering property.
    Thus $parallel c + I parallel < alpha$; if $C \/ I = 0$, the same inequality is immediate.
    Choose $d in I$ with $parallel c - d parallel < alpha$, and then approximate $d$ by an element $d_F in
    sum_(lambda in F) J_lambda$ for a finite index set $F$, closely enough that $parallel c -
    d_F parallel < alpha$ still holds. Any primitive ideal containing every $J_lambda$, $lambda in F$,
    has $parallel c_P parallel < alpha$. Hence the finitely many $cal(O)_lambda$, $lambda in F$,
    cover $K \( c \, alpha \)$. The proof uses quasi-compactness only and does not assert that
    $K \( c \, alpha \)$ is closed. ◻
]

#prodef([Lemma 8.4.])[
  (Finite coupling).  #emph[Let $C$ be an exact $C^(*)$-algebra satisfying (F$""_n$) for one fixed integer $n
    gt.eq 1$. Let $K_0 subset "Prim" \( C \)$ be quasi-compact and let $cal(D) subset C$ be hereditary.
    Suppose that at each $P in K_0$ one can choose positive contractions $p \, q in cal(D)$ and $h \,
    k in cal(D)_(+)$ satisfying
$ p q = 0 \, wide p h = h \, wide q k = k \, wide h_P eq.not 0 \, quad k_P eq.not 0 . $

 There are finitely many such choices $\( p_i \, q_i \, h_i \, k_i \)$ and one selfadjoint $U in
   cal(D)$ such that, at every $P in K_0$,
$ \( p_i U q_i \)_P eq.not 0 $

 for at least one selected index $i$. Any support conditions in the original choices are retained in the selected ones.]
] <lem:finite-coupling>

#proof[
  If $K_0$ is empty, choose $U = 0$ and the empty family. Otherwise,
    Lemma 32 associates to every original choice a positive $c$ nonzero at its specified point,
    with $c^(xor n) prec.tilde h \, k$. Choose $0 < alpha < parallel c_P parallel$.
    The resulting open sets $Omega \( c \, alpha \)$ cover $K_0$, so choose a finite subcover $Omega_i =
    Omega \( c_i \, alpha_i \)$, $1 lt.eq i lt.eq N$.
  Set $theta = sigma \/ 4$, $eta_i = theta^(i - 1)$, and $U_0 = 0$.
    At stage $i$, apply Proposition 31 with $T = p_i U_(i - 1) q_i$, scale $eta_i$, and the selected data.
    The identities $p_i h_i = h_i$, $q_i k_i = k_i$ imply $p_i^2 h_i = h_i$, $q_i^2 k_i = k_i$.
    The simultaneous hereditary and selfadjoint options give a selfadjoint contraction $y_i in
    cal(D)$ such that $U_i = U_(i - 1) + eta_i y_i$ satisfies
  $ parallel \( p_i U_i q_i \)_P parallel gt.eq sigma eta_i wide \( P in K \( c_i \, alpha_i \) \) . $
  Let $U = U_N$. Later perturbations change the $i$th corner by at most
  $ sum_(j > i) eta_j lt.eq eta_i frac(theta, 1 - theta) = eta_i frac(sigma, 4 - sigma) < frac(sigma eta_i, 2) . $
  Consequently
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(8.1)")
   $ parallel \( p_i U q_i \)_P parallel > frac(sigma eta_i, 2) wide \( P in K \( c_i \, alpha_i \) \) .
     $ <eq:finite-coupling-bound>]
  In particular each selected corner is nonzero throughout its entire open set $Omega_i$, proving the assertion. ◻
]

When applying Lemma 35, it suffices to produce two orthogonal positives $u \, v$ nonzero at the specified point.
  Indeed, choose $0 < beta < parallel u_P parallel$ and a continuous $f : \[ 0 \, oo \)
  arrow.r \[ 0 \, 1 \]$ that is zero near zero and one on $\[ beta \, oo \)$.
  Then $p = f \( u \)$ and $h = \( u - beta \)_(+)$ satisfy $p h = h$ and $h_P eq.not 0$.
  The analogous construction from $v$ gives $q \, k$. All four elements retain the hereditary and spectral support
  restrictions of $u \, v$.

=== 8.2 A square-zero element on a quasi-compact set <a-square-zero-element-on-a-quasi-compact-set>

#prodef([Proposition 8.5.])[
  #emph[Let $C$ be an exact $C^(*)$-algebra satisfying (F$""_n$) for one fixed integer $n gt.eq 1$.
    Let $K_0 subset "Prim" \( C \)$ be quasi-compact, and let $cal(D) subset
    C$ be a hereditary subalgebra nonzero at every point of $K_0$. There exists $x in cal(D)$ such that
$ x^2 = 0 \, wide x_P eq.not 0 quad \( P in K_0 \) . $

]
] <prop:square-zero>

#proof[
  If $K_0 = diameter$, take $x = 0$. Assume henceforth that $K_0$ is nonempty.
    We first construct a selfadjoint element that is nonscalar on every relevant hereditary support space.
    Coupling its separated spectral windows and deleting the nearby spectral blocks will produce a selfadjoint
    element whose positive and negative parts are both nonzero at every point of $K_0$.
    A final coupling of those two orthogonal parts gives the required square-zero element.
  ===== 8.2.1 First coupling: a nonscalar selfadjoint element. <first-coupling-a-nonscalar-selfadjoint-element.>
  At each $P in K_0$, choose $h in cal(D)_(+)$ with $h_P eq.not 0$.
    Lemma 32(i), with $m = 2$, provides two orthogonal positives in $"Her" \( h \) subset cal(D)$ nonzero at $P$.
    Apply the preceding cutoff construction to these two positives and use the resulting four inputs in Lemma 35
    to obtain $U = U^(*) in cal(D)$. For an irreducible representation $pi$ with kernel $P$,
    put $H_(cal(D) \, pi) = overline(pi \( cal(D) \) H_pi)$. This space reduces $pi \( cal(D) \)$,
    which acts as zero on its orthogonal complement. At $P$ one selected $p U q$ is nonzero.
    Since $p \, q in cal(D)$ and $p q = 0$, this implies
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(8.2)")
   $ pi \( U \) \|_(H_(cal(D) \, pi)) upright(" is not a scalar operator") wide \( P in K_0 \) . $ <eq:nonscalarU>]
  In particular, nonscalarity is obtained on the support space of $cal(D)$,
    without using its zero action on the complement.
  ===== 8.2.2 Second coupling: separated spectral windows. <second-coupling-separated-spectral-windows.>
  At a specified $P in K_0$, the selfadjoint operator in (8.2) has two distinct spectral values.
    Choose disjoint closed intervals $I \, J subset bb(R)$ at positive distance,
    and continuous nonnegative functions $F \, G$ supported in $I \, J$, respectively,
    such that both $F \( pi \( U \) \) \|_(H_(cal(D) \, pi))$ and $G \( pi \( U \) \) \|_(H_(cal(D) \, pi))$ are
    nonzero. The functions may be chosen on all of $bb(R)$ with compact support.
    If a window contains zero, its function need not vanish at zero.
    We evaluate scalar functions in $cal(D) + bb(C) 1$ inside a fixed unitization $tilde(C)$ of $C$,
    and take spectral projections in $tilde(C)^(* *)$. Let $\( d_lambda \)$ be a positive contractive approximate
    identity of $cal(D)$. The positive elements
  $ F \( U \) d_lambda F \( U \) \, wide G \( U \) d_lambda G \( U \) $
  belong to $cal(D)$. Their images on $H_(cal(D) \, pi)$ converge strongly to $F \( pi \( U \) \)^2$ and
    $G \( pi \( U \) \)^2$, respectively, so a choice of middle elements makes both images nonzero.
    These positives are supported, globally in the bidual, on $1_I \( U \)$ and $1_J \( U \)$.
  The same cutoff construction gives the corresponding $p \, q \, h \, k$, retaining these spectral supports.
    Apply Lemma 35 again to obtain $V = V^(*) in cal(D)$ and finitely many selected pairs $\( p_i \, q_i \)$,
    supported in windows $\( I_i \, J_i \)$, such that
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(8.3)")
   $ p_i = 1_(I_i) \( U \) p_i 1_(I_i) \( U \) \, wide q_i = 1_(J_i) \( U \) q_i 1_(J_i) \( U \) \,\
upright("for every ") P in K_0 upright(" there is ") i upright(" with ") \( p_i V q_i \)_P eq.not 0 .
  $ <eq:spectralwindowV>]
  Put $rho = min_i "dist" \( I_i \, J_i \) > 0$. The positive elements $p_i \, q_i$ need not commute with $U$;
    only the displayed support identities will be used.
  ===== 8.2.3 Spectral deletion produces both signs. <spectral-deletion-produces-both-signs.>
  We retain the corners in (8.3) while removing all sufficiently narrow diagonal spectral blocks.
    A positive operator with all those diagonal blocks zero must vanish; the same holds for a negative operator.
    Since the retained corners prevent vanishing, this will force both signs in every quotient over $K_0$.
  Fix a compact interval $\[ - M \, M \]$ containing the spectrum of $U$ in the unitization.
    Take a finite continuous nonnegative partition of unity $f_1 \, dots.h \, f_s$ on this interval,
    whose supports $S_j$ have diameter less than $rho \/ 8$. Define
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(8.4)")
   $ H = sum_("dist" \( S_j \, S_k \) > rho \/ 2) f_j \( U \) V f_k \( U \) . $ <eq:deletionH>]
  The summation uses ordered pairs, and its condition is symmetric in $j \, k$, so $H = H^(*)$.
    Although a factor $f_j \( U \)$ may have a scalar part, it lies in $cal(D) + bb(C) 1$;
    the middle factor $V in cal(D)$ implies $H in cal(D)$.
  For each selected pair $\( p_i \, q_i \)$ we have
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(8.5)")
   $ p_i H q_i = p_i V q_i . $ <eq:window-preservation>]
  Indeed, expand $p_i V q_i$ using $sum_j f_j \( U \) = 1$ on both sides.
    For a summand $p_i f_j \( U \) V f_k \( U \) q_i$ to be nonzero, the supports $S_j \, S_k$ must meet $I_i \,
    J_i$, respectively, by (8.3). Their distances then satisfy
  $ "dist" \( S_j \, S_k \) gt.eq "dist" \( I_i \, J_i \) - "diam" \( S_j \) -
    "diam" \( S_k \) > frac(3 rho, 4) > rho / 2 . $
  Every potentially nonzero summand is therefore retained in (8.4).
    This argument requires no commutation of $p_i \, q_i$ with $U$.
    Equations (8.3) and (8.5) show that $H_P eq.not 0$ for every $P in K_0$.
  Let $B$ be any Borel interval of length less than $rho \/ 4$.
    The two supports in a retained pair cannot both meet $B$, since their distance exceeds $rho \/ 2$. Hence
  $ 1_B \( U \) H 1_B \( U \) = 0 . $
  Partition $\[ - M \, M \]$ into finitely many disjoint Borel intervals $B_j$ of length less than $rho \/ 4$,
    assigning endpoints so that their spectral projections sum to the identity.
    In any unital extension of a representation, the resulting projections $E_j$ thus satisfy $E_j pi \( H \)
    E_j = 0$ and $sum_j E_j = 1$. If $pi \( H \) gt.eq 0$, then
  $ parallel pi \( H \)^(1 \/ 2) E_j xi parallel^2 = chevron.l E_j pi \( H \) E_j xi \, xi chevron.r = 0 $
  for every $j \, xi$, whence $pi \( H \) = 0$. Applying the same argument to $-
    pi \( H \)$ excludes a nonzero negative semidefinite image.
    The spectral projection calculations can equivalently be made in the bidual and passed through the normal
    extensions of the representations. We conclude that
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(8.6)")
   $ \( H_(+) \)_P eq.not 0 \, wide \( H_(-) \)_P eq.not 0 wide \( P in K_0 \) . $ <eq:bothsigns>]
  Both positive parts belong to $cal(D)$ and are orthogonal.
  ===== 8.2.4 Third coupling: the square-zero product. <third-coupling-the-square-zero-product.>
  At every $P in K_0$, apply the same cutoff construction to $H_(+) \, H_(-)$ to obtain $p \,
    h in "Her" \( H_(+) \)$ and $q \, k in "Her" \( H_(-) \)$ for Lemma 35.
    The contractions $p \, q$ are orthogonal. The resulting $U' =
    U' ""^(*) in cal(D)$ has a selected nonzero corner at every point of $K_0$. Put
  $ x = H_(+) U' H_(-) in cal(D) . $
  If $x_P = 0$, then $\( p U' q \)_P = 0$ for every $p in "Her" \( H_(+) \)$ and $q in "Her" \( H_(-) \)$:
    approximate them in norm by elements $H_(+) a H_(+)$ and $H_(-) b H_(-)$, and use
  $ \( H_(+) a H_(+) \) U' \( H_(-) b H_(-) \) = H_(+) a thin x thin b H_(-) . $
  This contradicts the selected nonzero corner at $P$. Thus $x_P eq.not 0$ throughout $K_0$, while $x^2 =
    H_(+) U' \( H_(-) H_(+) \) U' H_(-) = 0$. All constructed elements lie in $cal(D)$;
    spectral projections have only been used to verify their supports. ◻
]

=== 8.3 Removing the amplification <removing-the-amplification>

We have constructed a square-zero element on every prescribed quasi-compact set where a hereditary subalgebra is
  nonzero. We now apply this result repeatedly on the same primitive norm slice.
  The resulting number of orthogonal equivalent copies can be made at least the single amplification integer in
  (F$""_n$).

#proof[
  Fix $a in C_(+)$ and $r > 0$. The set $K_0 = K \( a \, r \)$ is quasi-compact by Lemma 34.
    If it is empty, every primitive image of $\( a - r \)_(+)$ is zero, so $\( a - r \)_(+) = 0$,
    and $\( \( a - r \)_(+) \)^(xor 2) prec.tilde a$ is immediate. Suppose that $K_0$ is nonempty.
    Proposition 36 applied to $"Her" \( a \)$ gives $x in "Her" \( a \)$ with $x^2 =
    0$ and $x_P eq.not 0$ throughout $K_0$. Set $z_1 = x^(*) x$.
    Its Cuntz-equivalent positive $x x^(*)$ is orthogonal to it, since $\( x^(*) x \) \( x x^(*) \) =
    x^(*) x^2 x^(*) = 0$. Therefore
  $ z_1^(xor 2) tilde.op_(upright("Cu")) z_1 xor x x^(*) tilde.op_(upright("Cu")) z_1 + x x^(*) prec.tilde a \,
    wide \( z_1 \)_P eq.not 0 quad \( P in K_0 \) . $
  Repeat the construction inside $"Her" \( z_1 \)$, and then inside successive hereditary subalgebras,
    always over the same $K_0$. It yields $z_j in C_(+)$ nonzero throughout $K_0$ and $z_(j + 1)^(xor 2)
    prec.tilde z_j$. Finite direct sums and transitivity give
  $ z_j^(xor 2^j) prec.tilde a . $
  Choose a finite $j gt.eq 1$ with $2^j gt.eq n$. Dropping extra summands gives $z_j^(xor n) prec.tilde a$.
    If $\( z_j \)_P = 0$, then $P in.not K_0$, so $parallel a_P parallel < r$ and $\( \( a - r \)_(+) \)_P = 0$.
    The primitive ideal test in Lemma 6 therefore gives $\( a - r \)_(+) in "Id" \( z_j \)$.
    Condition (F$""_n$) makes $z_j^(xor n)$ properly infinite because $z_j$ is a scalar positive element.
    Lemma 28, used with two copies on the left, then implies
  $ \( \( a - r \)_(+) \)^(xor 2) prec.tilde z_j^(xor n) prec.tilde a . $
  Only scalar positives $z_j in C$ have been tested against (F$""_n$).
    Finally, $parallel a - \( a - r \)_(+) parallel lt.eq r$.
    As $r arrow.b 0$, norm-limit closure of comparison with the fixed target $a$ gives
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(8.7)")
   $ a xor a prec.tilde a wide \( a in C_(+) \) . $ <eq:individual-infiniteness>]
  This includes $a = 0$; when $n = 1$, it also follows directly from the hypothesis. Therefore $C$ satisfies (P).
    Theorem 1 now gives strong pure infiniteness, completing the proof. ◻
]

The removal of amplification also gives the comparison form needed for the direct quantitative argument in
  Section 9. The following conclusion concerns the entire closed ideal,
  rather than only one cutoff or primitive norm slice.

#prodef([Corollary 8.6.])[
  (Whole-ideal comparison).  #emph[Let $C$ be a $C^(*)$-algebra satisfying (P).
    For every $h in C_(+)$, $c in "Id" \( h \)_(+)$, and integer $k gt.eq 1$,
$ c^(xor k) prec.tilde h . $

 If $C$ is exact, Proposition 31 may be applied with multiplicity $n = 1$,
   with the same constant $sigma$ and all its stated options.]
] <cor:ideal-comparison>

#proof[
  Property (P) makes $h$ properly infinite, so Lemma 28 applies with multiplicity one.
    If $h = 0$, the ideal is zero and the assertion is immediate.
    For exact $C$, property (P) is precisely the amplification hypothesis of Proposition 31 with $n =
    1$ for every scalar positive element. Its constant was fixed independently of $n$,
    so neither the constant nor the hereditary and selfadjoint options change. ◻
]

== 9 Direct quantitative separation under exactness <sec:quantitative>

We give a direct proof of strong pure infiniteness from the exact perturbation principle.
  Throughout this section, $C$ is exact and satisfies $upright("(P)")$.
  This is $upright("(F_n)")$ with $n = 1$, so Proposition 31 is available with that multiplicity and its fixed
  constant $0 < sigma < 1$. Corollary 37 applies to the whole ideal $"Id" \( h \)$ generated by each positive $h$.
  For an exact algebra satisfying $upright("(F_n)")$, the scalar conclusion (8.7) proved in Section 8 supplies
  $upright("(P)")$ before that section invokes the general theorem.

The quantitative feature of this proof is a fixed lower norm bound in primitive quotients,
  retained while a mixed product tends to zero. We first construct positive elements in two prescribed hereditary
  subalgebras with this property. Spectral clipping then supplies bounded comparison coefficients,
  so the small product survives Cuntz implementation. The resulting estimates verify the strong-infiniteness
  inequalities directly. Thus this proof uses the comparison tools and the exact perturbation and localization
  results in Sections 2, 7, and 8.

#prodef([Lemma 9.1.])[
  (Separation with a fixed quotient norm bound).  #emph[Let $C$ be an exact $C^(*)$-algebra satisfying
    $upright("(P)")$, and let $0 < sigma < 1$ be the constant in Proposition 31 with $n = 1$.
    Let $a \, b in C_(+)$ be contractions, and let $0 < r < 1$. Set
$ m = frac(r sigma^2, 1024) . $

 For every $0 < ell < 1 \/ 10$ there are positives $Q_a in "Her" \( a \)$ and $Q_b in "Her" \( b \)$ such that
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(9.1)")
 $ parallel \( Q_a \)_P parallel & gt.eq m &  & upright("if ") parallel a_P parallel gt.eq r \,\
parallel \( Q_b \)_P parallel & gt.eq m &  & upright("if ") parallel b_P parallel gt.eq r \,\
parallel Q_a Q_b parallel & lt.eq 20 sqrt(ell) . $ <eq:separation-quotient-bounds>]

 Here and below $P$ ranges over $"Prim" \( C \)$.]
] <lem:separating-positives>

#proof[
  Write the polar decomposition in $C^(* *)$ as
  $ a b = v u \, wide u = \| a b \| \, wide u_(*) = \| \( a b \)^(*) \| . $
  All three positive elements $a \, b \, u$ are contractions.
    Choose continuous functions on $\[ 0 \, 1 \]$ to separate the small singular values of $a
    b$ from a spectral corner on which an inverse square root is available.
    The small values will give small cross products; on the remaining corner we will preserve exact cancellation
    while perturbing the two sides separately. Use the following functions:
  - $0 lt.eq L lt.eq 1$, $L = 1$ on $\[ 0 \, 5 ell \]$, and $L = 0$ on $\[ 6 ell \, 1 \]$; put $N = 1 - L$;
  - $0 lt.eq g lt.eq 1$, $g = 0$ on $\[ 0 \, ell \]$, and $g = 1$ on $\[ 2 ell \, 1 \]$;
  - $0 lt.eq R lt.eq 1$, $R = 0$ on $\[ 0 \, ell \/ 2 \]$, and $R \( s \) = sqrt(ell \/ s)$ for $s gt.eq ell$.
  Linear interpolation on the unspecified intervals gives such functions.
    A function letter without an argument denotes evaluation at $u$.
    $L$ selects the small singular values, $g$ confines the perturbations away from zero,
    and $R$ normalizes $u$ on that region. Functional calculus and the polar decomposition give
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(9.2)")
   $ L \( u_(*) \) v = v L \( u \) \, wide N \( u_(*) \) = v N v^(*) \, wide g N = N \, wide \( R u R - ell \) g =
     0 . $ <eq:separation-functional-identities>]
  The second identity uses $N \( 0 \) = 0$; the first remains valid with $L \( 0 \) = 1$. Moreover
  $ v R = a b thin frac(R \( u \), u) in C \, wide v N = a b thin frac(N \( u \), u) in C \, $
  where both quotient functions are extended continuously by zero at $s = 0$, since $R$ and $N$ vanish near zero.
    We use the unitization for scalar terms throughout this proof.
  #emph[The four columns and their products.] We first specify conditions under which four columns have the
    product estimates we need. Suppose that orthogonal positive contractions $e \, f in C$ and invertibles $Z \,
    W in tilde(C)$ satisfy
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(9.3)")
   $ g e = e \, quad g f = f \, quad Z^(*) W = 1 \, quad Z = 1 + g z g \, quad W = 1 + g w g \, quad
     parallel Z parallel \, parallel W parallel lt.eq 2 $ <eq:separation-inverse-conditions>]
  for some $z \, w in C$. For such a choice, define
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(9.4)")
   $ X_0 = a L \( u_(*) \) \, & X = ell^(- 1 \/ 2) a v R Z e \,\
Y_0 = b L \( u \) \, & Y = ell^(- 1 \/ 2) b R W f . $ <eq:separation-four-columns>]
  These elements belong to $C$: the only polar factor is $v R in C$,
    and all unitized factors occur multiplied by elements of $C$.
    For any such admissible choice, the proposed outputs are
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(9.5)")
   $ Q_a = \( X_0 X_0^(*) + X X^(*) \)^(1 \/ 2) \, wide Q_b = \( Y_0 Y_0^(*) + Y Y^(*) \)^(1 \/ 2) .
     $ <eq:separation-final-positives>]
  The norm of each positive output dominates the norms of its two columns in every primitive quotient.
    At the end, a row polar decomposition will bound the product of these outputs by the four cross products
    estimated next.
  Put $E_R = R u R - ell$. Since $E_R g = 0$ and $f = g f$, we have
  $ E_R W f = E_R f + E_R g w g f = 0 . $
  In particular, no commutation of $W$ with $g$ is needed. Using $v^(*) a b =
    u$ and retaining the indicated order of factors gives
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(9.6)")
   $ X^(*) Y = ell^(- 1) e Z^(*) R u R W f = e Z^(*) W f = e f = 0 . $ <eq:separation-high-orthogonality>]
  The other three products obey
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(9.7)")
   $ parallel X_0^(*) Y_0 parallel lt.eq 6 ell \, wide parallel X^(*) Y_0 parallel lt.eq 2 sqrt(6 ell) \, wide
     parallel X_0^(*) Y parallel lt.eq 2 sqrt(6 ell) . $ <eq:separation-mixed-bounds>]
  Indeed,
  $ X_0^(*) Y_0 & = v L \( u \) u L \( u \) \,\
X^(*) Y_0 & = ell^(- 1 \/ 2) e Z^(*) R u L \( u \) \,\
X_0^(*) Y & = ell^(- 1 \/ 2) v L \( u \) u R W f . $
  Here $parallel u L \( u \) parallel lt.eq 6 ell$. For $0 lt.eq s lt.eq ell$,
    $ell^(- 1 \/ 2) R \( s \) s L \( s \) lt.eq sqrt(ell)$; for $s gt.eq
    ell$ the same expression equals $sqrt(s) L \( s \) lt.eq sqrt(6 ell)$.
    Together with (9.3), these scalar estimates prove (9.7). We now construct $e \, f \, Z \, W$ so that,
    in each prescribed primitive quotient, at least one of the two relevant columns also has a fixed lower norm bound.
  #emph[Two orthogonal cores.] Set $h_0 = \( u - 2 ell \)_(+)$.
    Since $h_0 xor h_0 prec.tilde h_0$, Lemma 29 supplies a row $\[ r_1 \, r_2 \] in M_(1 \, 2) \( C \)$ such that
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(9.8)")
   $ r_i^(*) r_j = cases(delim: "{", theta \( h_0 \) \, & i = j \,, 0 \, & i eq.not j \,) wide r_1 r_1^(*) +
     r_2 r_2^(*) in "Her" \( h_0 \) \, $ <eq:separation-exact-cores>]
  where $theta : \[ 0 \, oo \) arrow.r \[ 0 \, 1 \]$ is continuous, vanishes near zero,
    and equals one on $\[ 2 ell \, oo \)$. Define
  $ e_0 = r_1 r_1^(*) \, wide f_0 = r_2 r_2^(*) . $
  They are orthogonal positive contractions in $"Her" \( h_0 \)$;
    each belongs to this hereditary algebra because it is dominated by their sum.
    In any primitive quotient with $parallel u_P parallel gt.eq 4 ell$, $parallel \( h_0 \)_P parallel gt.eq
    2 ell$, and hence
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(9.9)")
   $ parallel \( e_0 \)_P parallel = parallel \( f_0 \)_P parallel = parallel theta \( \( h_0 \)_P \) parallel =
     1 . $ <eq:separation-core-norms>]
  This includes equality at the threshold $4 ell$ by the chosen plateau.
  Put
  $ k_e = \( e_0 - 1 \/ 2 \)_(+) \, wide k_f = \( f_0 - 1 \/ 2 \)_(+) \, $
  and let $e = chi \( e_0 \)$ and $f = chi \( f_0 \)$, where $chi : \[ 0 \, 1 \]
    arrow.r \[ 0 \, 1 \]$ is continuous, vanishes near zero, and is one on $\[ 1 \/ 2 \, 1 \]$. Then
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(9.10)")
   $ e f = 0 \, wide e k_e = k_e \, wide f k_f = k_f \, wide g e = e \, wide g f =
     f . $ <eq:separation-core-identities>]
  For the last two identities, $g h_0 = h_0$ implies that multiplication by $g$ is the identity on
    $"Her" \( h_0 \)$. By (9.9), both $\( k_e \)_P$ and $\( k_f \)_P$ are nonzero whenever $parallel u_P parallel
    gt.eq 4 ell$.
  #emph[The quotient regions requiring a high column.] Define
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(9.11)")
   $ tau = frac(r sqrt(ell), 8) \, wide C_1 = a v R \, quad C_2 = b R \, wide h_i = \( \| C_i g \| - tau \)_(+) \,
     quad d_i = \( \| C_i N \| - 2 tau \)_(+) . $ <eq:separation-normalization-data>]
  We first check why a lower bound on the region $parallel \( d_i \)_P parallel gt.eq
    tau$ will recover any signal missed by the corresponding low column.
    Suppose $parallel a_P parallel gt.eq r$ and $parallel \( X_0 \)_P parallel < r \/ 2$.
    Since $a = a L \( u_(*) \) + a N \( u_(*) \)$, we have $parallel \( a N \( u_(*) \) \)_P parallel > r \/ 2$.
    The identity $N \( u_(*) \) = v N v^(*)$ gives $parallel \( a v N \)_P parallel > r \/ 2$.
    On $\[ 0 \, 1 \]$, the scalar inequality $R^2 N^2 gt.eq ell N^2$ holds:
    where $N$ is nonzero, $s > 5 ell$ and $R \( s \)^2 = ell \/ s gt.eq ell$. Consequently
  $ \( C_1 N \) \( C_1 N \)^(*) = a v R^2 N^2 v^(*) a gt.eq ell a v N^2 v^(*) a \, $
  and therefore
  $ parallel \( C_1 N \)_P parallel > frac(r sqrt(ell), 2) = 4 tau . $
  It follows that $parallel \( d_1 \)_P parallel > 2 tau$. Similarly, if $parallel b_P parallel gt.eq
    r$ and $parallel \( Y_0 \)_P parallel < r \/ 2$, then $parallel \( b N \)_P parallel > r \/ 2$.
    The order inequality $b R^2 N^2 b gt.eq ell b N^2 b$ gives $parallel \( d_2 \)_P parallel > 2 tau$.
    Thus every prescribed quotient either has a low column of norm at least $r \/
    2$ or belongs to the corresponding perturbation region.
  #emph[Comparison inputs and normalized left factors.] We now verify the inputs to Proposition 31 on these two
    regions. If $\( d_i \)_P eq.not 0$, then $C_i N = C_i g N$ implies $parallel \( C_i g \)_P parallel > 2 tau$,
    so $\( h_i \)_P eq.not 0$. The same assumption implies $N_P eq.not 0$,
    and therefore $parallel u_P parallel > 5 ell$. Consequently $\( k_e \)_P \, \( k_f \)_P eq.not 0$.
    The primitive ideal test and Corollary 37 give
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(9.12)")
   $ d_1 prec.tilde h_1 \, quad d_1 prec.tilde k_e \, wide d_2 prec.tilde h_2 \, quad
     d_2 prec.tilde k_f . $ <eq:separation-core-comparisons>]
  Let $D_i = C_i g$, and use continuous functional calculus in the unitization to set
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(9.13)")
   $ F_i = #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] max { tau \, \| D_i^(*) \| } #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)]^(- 1) \, wide
     p_i = F_i D_i . $ <eq:separation-left-normalization>]
  Thus $parallel F_i parallel lt.eq tau^(- 1)$ and $p_i in C$. The functional-calculus intertwining identity
  $ F_i D_i = D_i #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] max { tau \, \| D_i \| } #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)]^(- 1) $
  follows first for polynomials in $D_i D_i^(*)$ and $D_i^(*) D_i$, and then by uniform approximation. In particular,
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(9.14)")
   $ p_i^(*) p_i = \| D_i \|^2 #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] max { tau \, \| D_i \| }
     #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)]^(- 2) lt.eq 1 \, wide p_i^(*) p_i h_i = h_i . $ <eq:separation-normalized-core>]
  The use of $\| D_i^(*) \|$ in the left factor $F_i$ is essential to this identity.
  #emph[Two compatible inverse perturbations.] Fix
  $ eta = 1 / 8 \, wide eta' = frac(sigma eta, 16) = sigma / 128 . $
  Apply Proposition 31, with $n = 1$, to
  $ \( p \, q \, h \, k \, c \, T \, alpha \) = \( p_1 \, e \, h_1 \, k_e \, d_1 \, F_1 C_1 e \, tau \) $
  at scale $eta$. Its hypotheses follow from (9.10), (9.12), and (9.14). For the resulting contraction $y in C$, put
  $ Z_1 = 1 + eta g y g . $
  Since $parallel Z_1 - 1 parallel lt.eq eta < 1$, the element $Z_1$ is invertible;
    define $W_1 = \( Z_1^(*) \)^(- 1)$. Since $g e = e$, the conclusion of the Proposition becomes
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(9.15)")
   $ parallel \( F_1 C_1 Z_1 e \)_P parallel gt.eq sigma eta wide
     upright("if ") parallel \( d_1 \)_P parallel gt.eq tau . $ <eq:separation-first-test>]
  Next apply the same Proposition at scale $eta'$ to
  $ \( p \, q \, h \, k \, c \, T \, alpha \) = \( p_2 \, f \, h_2 \, k_f \, d_2 \, F_2 C_2 W_1 f \, tau \) . $
  The element $T$ is in $C$; the Proposition imposes no bound on its norm. With the resulting contraction $y' in C$, set
  $ W = W_1 + eta' g y' g . $
  Since $g f = f$, the second conclusion is
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(9.16)")
   $ parallel \( F_2 C_2 W f \)_P parallel gt.eq sigma eta' wide
     upright("if ") parallel \( d_2 \)_P parallel gt.eq tau . $ <eq:separation-second-test>]
  We record the inverse estimates, including the factorizations needed to preserve (9.15).
    For $a_0 in C$ with $parallel a_0 parallel < 1$, the Neumann series gives
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(9.17)")
   $ \( 1 + g a_0 g \)^(- 1) = 1 + g (sum_(k = 1)^oo \( - 1 \)^k a_0 \( g^2 a_0 \)^(k - 1)) g .
     $ <eq:separation-factored-series>]
  The series of middle factors converges in $C$ with norm at most $parallel a_0
    parallel \/ \( 1 - parallel a_0 parallel \)$. This formula does not require $g$ to be a projection.
    Applying it to $Z_1^(*)$ gives
  $ W_1 = 1 + g w_1 g \, wide parallel w_1 parallel lt.eq 1 / 7 . $
  Thus $W = 1 + g w g$ for $w = w_1 + eta' y' in C$, with
  $ parallel w parallel lt.eq 1 / 7 + 1 / 128 < 1 / 4 . $
  Applying (9.17) to $W^(*) = 1 + g w^(*) g$ now proves that $W$ is invertible. Define $Z = \( W^(*) \)^(- 1)$.
    The same series gives
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(9.18)")
   $ Z = 1 + g z g \, wide parallel z parallel lt.eq 1 / 3 \, wide parallel Z parallel lt.eq 4 / 3 \, quad
     parallel W parallel lt.eq 5 / 4 \, quad parallel Z_1 parallel lt.eq 9 / 8 . $ <eq:separation-inverse-bounds>]
  In particular, all conditions in (9.3) hold.
  The second perturbation changes the inverse used in the first column.
    To keep the first lower bound independent of $ell$, we must estimate this change without the potentially large
    factor $parallel F_1 parallel$. The inverse difference has the exact order
  $ Z - Z_1 = - eta' Z g \( y' \)^(*) g Z_1 . $
  As $Z g = g \( 1 + z g^2 \)$, its effect on the first normalized column is bounded by
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(9.19)")
   $ parallel F_1 C_1 \( Z - Z_1 \) e parallel & = eta' parallel p_1 \( 1 + z g^2 \) \( y' \)^(*) g Z_1 e parallel\
 & lt.eq eta' (1 + 1 / 3) 9 / 8 = 3 / 2 eta' . $ <eq:separation-no-normalization-loss>]
  Since $3 eta' \/ 2 lt.eq sigma eta \/ 2$, (9.15) remains bounded below by $sigma eta \/
    2$ with $Z$ in place of $Z_1$. Using $parallel F_i parallel lt.eq tau^(- 1)$ in each primitive quotient,
    and $tau ell^(- 1 \/ 2) = r \/ 8$, we obtain
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(9.20)")
   $ parallel X_P parallel & gt.eq r / 8 frac(sigma eta, 2) &  & upright("if ") parallel \( d_1 \)_P parallel
     gt.eq tau \,\
parallel Y_P parallel & gt.eq r / 8 sigma eta' &  & upright("if ") parallel \( d_2 \)_P parallel gt.eq
  tau . $ <eq:separation-high-lower-bounds>]
  The cancellation $tau ell^(- 1 \/ 2) = r \/ 8$ is what removes $ell$ from both lower bounds.
  #emph[Assembling the two positive outputs.]
  To check the hereditary membership of $Q_a \, Q_b$, (9.4) and the bounds on $Z \, W$ give
  $ X_0 X_0^(*) lt.eq a^2 \, quad X X^(*) lt.eq 4 ell^(- 1) a^2 \, wide Y_0 Y_0^(*) lt.eq b^2 \, quad
    Y Y^(*) lt.eq 4 ell^(- 1) b^2 . $
  By Lemma 4, the first two summands belong to $"Her" \( a \)$ and the last two to $"Her" \( b \)$.
    Thus $Q_a in "Her" \( a \)$ and $Q_b in "Her" \( b \)$. The earlier coverage reduction and (9.20),
    together with domination of the column norms by the output norms, prove (9.1) with
  $ m = r / 8 sigma eta' = frac(r sigma^2, 1024) . $
  Here $m lt.eq r \/ 2$ and $eta' lt.eq eta \/ 2$, so this single lower bound applies to all cases.
    It is independent of $ell$.
  Finally put $U_a = \[ X_0 \, X \]$ and $U_b = \[ Y_0 \, Y \]$ in $M_(1 \, 2) \( C \)$.
    Their range squares are $Q_a^2$ and $Q_b^2$. The rectangular polar decompositions in the bidual have the form
    $U_a = Q_a v_a$ and $U_b = Q_b v_b$, where $v_a v_a^(*)$ and $v_b
    v_b^(*)$ are the support projections of $Q_a$ and $Q_b$, respectively. Hence both identities
  $ U_a^(*) U_b = v_a^(*) Q_a Q_b v_b \, wide Q_a Q_b = v_a \( U_a^(*) U_b \) v_b^(*) $
  hold. Since partial isometries have norm at most one, they imply
  $ parallel Q_a Q_b parallel = parallel U_a^(*) U_b parallel lt.eq 6 ell + 4 sqrt(6 ell) lt.eq 20 sqrt(ell) \, $
  by (9.6) and (9.7). This proves (9.1).
  The construction also covers zero elements and empty norm regions. If $h_0 = 0$, the row in (9.8) may be zero.
    Empty perturbation targets permit zero perturbations, and the coverage argument then assigns every prescribed
    quotient to a low column. All inverse operations have either the positive floor $tau$ or the convergent series
    (9.17). ◻
]

The norms of the preceding outputs may grow as $ell$ decreases.
  The next observation controls the comparison coefficients using only the fixed spectral floor $m$.
  This is enough for their small product to survive Cuntz implementation.

#prodef([Lemma 9.2.])[
  (Comparison above a spectral floor).  #emph[Let $C$ be a $C^(*)$-algebra, let $Q \,
    c in C_(+)$ with $parallel c parallel lt.eq 1$, and let $m > 0$.
    If $c prec.tilde \( Q^2 - m^2 \/ 4 \)_(+)$, then for each $0 < delta < 1$ there is $x in C$ such that
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(9.21)")
 $ parallel \( Q x \)^(*) \( Q x \) - c parallel < delta \, wide parallel Q x parallel lt.eq 2 \, wide
   parallel x parallel lt.eq 4 / m . $ <eq:separation-bounded-implementation>]

]
] <lem:bounded-implementation>

#proof[
  Choose a comparison implementer $v in C$ with
  $ parallel v^(*) \( Q^2 - m^2 \/ 4 \)_(+) v - c parallel < delta . $
  Such an implementer lies in $C$ by compression to the two scalar matrix corners.
    On $\[ 0 \, parallel Q parallel \]$ define the continuous function
  $ j \( s \) = cases(delim: "{", s^(- 1) \( s^2 - m^2 \/ 4 \)_(+)^(1 \/ 2) \, & s > 0 \,, 0 \, & s = 0 \,) wide
    x = j \( Q \) v . $
  Then $\( Q x \)^(*) \( Q x \) = v^(*) \( Q^2 - m^2 \/ 4 \)_(+) v$ and $parallel Q x parallel^2 < 1 + delta < 2$,
    which in particular gives $parallel Q x parallel lt.eq 2$. Moreover the scalar inequality
  $ j \( s \)^2 lt.eq 4 / m^2 s^2 j \( s \)^2 $
  holds because $j$ vanishes on $\[ 0 \, m \/ 2 \]$. Functional calculus followed by multiplication by $v^(*)$ and
    $v$ gives
  $ x^(*) x lt.eq 4 / m^2 \( Q x \)^(*) \( Q x \) . $
  Thus $parallel x parallel lt.eq \( 2 \/ m \) parallel Q x parallel lt.eq 4 \/ m$,
    independently of the norm of the original comparison implementer $v$. ◻
]

#prodef([Proposition 9.3.])[
  (Direct exact proof).  #emph[Let $C$ be an exact $C^(*)$-algebra satisfying $upright("(P)")$.
    For every $a \, b in C_(+)$ and every $epsilon > 0$, there are $s \, t in C$ such that
$ parallel s^(*) a^2 s - a^2 parallel < epsilon \, wide parallel t^(*) b^2 t - b^2 parallel < epsilon \, wide
  parallel s^(*) a b t parallel < epsilon . $

 Consequently $C$ is strongly purely infinite.]
] <prop:direct-strong-infiniteness>

#proof[
  Fix the constant $sigma$ in Proposition 31 with $n = 1$. Let $a \,
    b in C_(+)$ be contractions and let $epsilon > 0$. Choose $0 < r < 1$ with $r^2 < epsilon \/ 3$ and put $m =
    r sigma^2 \/ 1024$. Next choose $0 < ell < 1 \/ 10$ so small that
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(9.22)")
   $ frac(320 sqrt(ell), m^2) < epsilon / 3 . $ <eq:separation-final-ell>]
  Take $Q_a \, Q_b$ from Lemma 38. If $\( a^2 - r^2 \)_(+)$ has nonzero image at $P$,
    then $parallel a_P parallel > r$, so $parallel \( Q_a \)_P parallel gt.eq m$.
    Thus $\( Q_a^2 - m^2 \/ 4 \)_(+)$ has nonzero image there. The primitive ideal test and Corollary 37 imply
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(9.23)")
   $ \( a^2 - r^2 \)_(+) prec.tilde \( Q_a^2 - m^2 \/ 4 \)_(+) \,
     wide \( b^2 - r^2 \)_(+) prec.tilde \( Q_b^2 - m^2 \/ 4 \)_(+) . $ <eq:separation-final-comparisons>]
  Finally choose $0 < delta < 1$ such that
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(9.24)")
   $ delta + \( 4 + delta \) delta < epsilon / 3 . $ <eq:separation-final-delta>]
  Apply Lemma 39 to the comparisons in (9.23). We obtain $x_a \, x_b in C$ with
  $ parallel \( Q_a x_a \)^(*) \( Q_a x_a \) - \( a^2 - r^2 \)_(+) parallel & < delta \,\
parallel \( Q_b x_b \)^(*) \( Q_b x_b \) - \( b^2 - r^2 \)_(+) parallel & < delta \, $
  and
  $ parallel Q_a x_a parallel \, parallel Q_b x_b parallel lt.eq 2 \, wide parallel x_a parallel \,
    parallel x_b parallel lt.eq 4 / m . $
  Consequently
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(9.25)")
   $ parallel x_a^(*) Q_a Q_b x_b parallel lt.eq 16 / m^2 parallel Q_a Q_b parallel lt.eq
     frac(320 sqrt(ell), m^2) . $ <eq:separation-implemented-product>]
  Since $Q_a in "Her" \( a \) = overline(a C a)$, $Q_a x_a in overline(a C)$;
    similarly $Q_b x_b in overline(b C)$. Choose $s \, t in C$ such that
  $ parallel a s - Q_a x_a parallel < delta \, wide parallel b t - Q_b x_b parallel < delta . $
  For elements $U \, V$ of norm at most $2$, replacing them by elements within $delta$ changes $U^(*) U$ or $U^(*)
    V$ by at most $\( 4 + delta \) delta$, by expanding the difference and using the triangle inequality.
    Also $parallel a^2 - \( a^2 - r^2 \)_(+) parallel \, parallel b^2 - \( b^2 - r^2 \)_(+) parallel lt.eq r^2$.
    Therefore
  $ parallel s^(*) a^2 s - a^2 parallel \, med parallel t^(*) b^2 t - b^2 parallel & lt.eq r^2 +
    delta + \( 4 + delta \) delta < epsilon \,\
parallel s^(*) a b t parallel & lt.eq frac(320 sqrt(ell), m^2) + \( 4 + delta \) delta < epsilon \, $
  by (9.22) and (9.24). The choice order is $r$, then $m$, then $ell$, then $delta$;
    the lower bound $m$ has no dependence on the later parameters.
  For arbitrary positives $a \, b$, put $M = max { 1 \, parallel a parallel \, parallel b parallel }$.
    Apply the preceding result to $a \/ M \, b \/ M$ with tolerance $epsilon \/ M^2$.
    Multiplication of all three inequalities by $M^2$ gives the required inequalities for $a \,
    b$ with the same $s \, t in C$. Zero elements, including the zero algebra, are included.
    These are precisely the three inequalities in (1.1), so $C$ is strongly purely infinite. ◻
]

